{id:"arcane-library-tower",name:"Arcane Library Tower",emoji:"🔮",tags:["Libraries", "Fantasy", "Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"10 hr",size:"21×25",photos:[P15],pr:[0.889],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A round wizard’s tower of mossy stone bricks with three floors of bookshelves, a spiral stair, a balcony of dark oak and a weathered-copper cone roof capped by an amethyst spire. On the top floor an enchanting table stands on an obsidian dais inside a ring of 26 bookshelves, lit by hanging chandeliers.",steps:[["Lay the stone plinth","Find flat ground at least 21 blocks wide and 25 deep, so there is room for the garden and the path at the front. The front is the side the door will be on, and left and right are as you face it. Mark the centre of the tower with a torch or any spare block: it is the 11th block from the left and the 11th block from the back, and every measurement in this guide counts from it. On layer 1, build a ring of stone round that centre. The outside edge of the ring is a circle 17 blocks across, and the middle 11 blocks stay empty for now, because the floor goes in there two steps from now. The simplest way is to lay the full 17-across circle row by row, from the back to the front, centring each row on the middle column: 5, 9, 13, 13, 15, 15, 17, 17, 17, 17, 17, 15, 15, 13, 13, 9 and 5 blocks. Then leave out the 11-across circle in the middle, starting at the 4th row from the back: 5, 7, 9, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5 blocks. Mix the stone as you place it, 76 stone bricks, 43 mossy and 9 cracked, and do not put the mossy ones in neat patterns. Check: the ring should be 3 blocks wide on each of the 4 straight sides, with the corners rounded off, 128 blocks in all.",[]],
["Ring it with steps","Now put a ring of steps round the outside of the plinth. On layer 1, add one more ring of stone brick stairs, 1 block thick, so that the whole build is now a circle 19 blocks across. The ring is 1 block thick wherever it runs straight, and 2 or 3 blocks wide per row on the diagonals. Counting rows from the back, it is 7 stairs; then 3, a gap of 5, and 3; 2, gap of 9, 2; 1, gap of 13, 1; 2, gap of 13, 2; 1, gap of 15, 1; 2, gap of 15, 2; five rows of 1, gap of 17, 1; then 2, gap of 15, 2; 1, gap of 15, 1; 2, gap of 13, 2; 1, gap of 13, 1; 2, gap of 9, 2; 3, gap of 5, 3; and a last row of 7. That gives a straight run of 7 stairs on each of the 4 sides, joined by short diagonal steps at the corners. Place every stair while standing outside the ring and facing the tower, so the high side ends up against the plinth and the low side faces outward. Walk once round the ring afterwards and check that no stair is turned the wrong way, and that there are no gaps, even at the front: the path joins on later without replacing any stair. 68 stairs.",[]],
["Lay the lapis and obsidian centre","Start the floor in the middle, on layer 1, inside the 11-across circle the plinth left empty. Find the centre block you marked in the first step and take the marker away. Lay a 3×3 square of 9 lapis lazuli blocks with that centre block in the middle, so the blue square reaches 1 block out on every side. Then lay a ring of 12 obsidian blocks round the lapis. Together they form a 5×5 square without its 4 corners, so the rows from the back are 3, 5, 5, 5 and 3 blocks. Leave the 4 corners empty: they are planks in the next step. Check: from above you should see a blue square inside a dark ring, 21 blocks in all, and the rows from the back should be 3, 5, 5, 5 and 3.",[]],
["Add the plank ring","Surround the centre with dark oak planks, still on layer 1. The floor circle you are filling now is 9 across, with rows from the back of 5, 7, 9, 9, 9, 9, 9, 7 and 5 blocks, 69 blocks in all. The lapis and obsidian already cover 21 of them, so lay a plank on every one of the other blocks of that circle: the 4 corners of the 5×5 square and the ring round it, 2 blocks thick at its widest. That is 48 planks. Work round the circle one row at a time, from the back to the front. Check: the finished disc should be round, 9 blocks across, with the blue and black centre showing no gaps.",[]],
["Edge the floor with stone","Finish the floor with its stone edge, on layer 1. Fill every block of the 11-across circle that is still empty with stone bricks. That makes a ring 1 block thick round the planks, which joins the floor to the plinth with no gap. The circle has rows from the back of 5, 7, 9, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5 blocks, 97 in all, so you need 28 blocks. Mix them as you place them, 17 stone bricks, 7 mossy and 4 cracked. These 28 blocks are also the ring the bookshelves will stand on, so lay them with no gaps. Check: together with the plinth, the floor now forms one solid round platform 17 blocks across, and the whole floor circle is 97 blocks.",[]],
["Frame the door","Build the doorway before the walls, so that you do not lose track of where it goes. The wall you will build next is a ring 13 blocks across (the 11-across floor circle plus 1 block all round), and its front row is 5 blocks wide: the 9th to the 13th blocks from the left of the whole build. This row is the door. Stack a dark oak log 4 high, on layers 2–5, on the 1st and on the 5th block of that row, which are the two ends. Leave the 3 blocks between them open: they are the doorway, 3 blocks wide and 3 tall (layers 2–4). Then put 3 dark oak planks across the top of that opening on layer 5, resting on the two logs. Stand in the doorway and check: you should be looking straight at the centre of the floor, with the lapis square directly ahead of you. That is 11 blocks: 8 logs and 3 planks.",[]],
["Raise the lower walls","Raise the lower storey of the wall, layers 2 to 7, which is 6 layers. The wall is a ring 1 block thick that outlines the 13-across circle and stands on the plinth right round the floor. Counting rows from the back, each layer of the ring is: 5 solid blocks; then 2 blocks, a gap of 5, and 2 blocks; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then five rows of 1, gap of 11, 1; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 2, gap of 5, 2; and a last row of 5, the front, where the door is. That is 40 blocks on a full layer. Build it with stone bricks, choosing plain, mossy and cracked ones at random so that it looks weathered: 115 stone bricks, 49 mossy and 35 cracked. On layers 2–5 the front row of 5 is the door you built in the last step, so skip it there; layers 6 and 7 carry on over the top of the door with 5 solid blocks. Leave 7 window gaps open on layers 4–6, 1 block wide and 3 tall. Give each position as (column, row) in the 13×13 square the ring fits in, counting from the left and from the back: (13,7) the middle of the right side, (11,11) front right, (3,11) front left, (1,7) the middle of the left side, (3,3) back left, (7,1) middle of the back and (11,3) back right. There is no window at the front, in the middle of the front row, because that is where the door is. Check: layers 2 and 3 have 35 blocks, layers 4 and 5 have 28, layer 6 has 33 and layer 7 has 40, 199 in all.",[]],
["Glaze the lower windows","Fill the 7 window gaps of the lower storey with glass panes: a column of 3 on layers 4, 5 and 6 in each gap, 21 panes in all. Glass panes are thin, and each one sits in the middle of its block, so the windows look like narrow slits of glass set in the wall. Walk round the outside and count the windows: right, front right, front left, left, back left, back and back right. Do not put any glass in the doorway. Tip: from the inside, each window should be framed by stone on both sides and above, with no stone in the glass itself.",[]],
["Lay the first log band","Lay a complete ring of dark oak logs on layer 8, right on top of the stone walls: 40 logs, with the same shape as every layer below it. It runs unbroken round the whole tower, over the top of the door at the front. This band closes the lower storey, and the lower floor will sit level with it, 1 block inside. It also gives the tower a dark stripe, which you will see again higher up, so the middle storey does not look like a plain stone drum. Check: walk once round the ring and make sure that it is unbroken, with no stone block left in it.",[]],
["Raise the middle walls","Raise the middle storey of the wall, layers 9 to 13, which is 5 layers, on top of the log band. Each layer of the ring is the same shape as before: 5 solid blocks; then 2 blocks, a gap of 5, and 2 blocks; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then five rows of 1, gap of 11, 1; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 2, gap of 5, 2; and a last row of 5, the front, where the door is. The front row is solid stone here, apart from the window above the door. Use the same random mix of stone bricks, 97 stone bricks, 55 mossy and 24 cracked. Leave 8 window gaps open on layers 10–12, 1 block wide and 3 tall, in these places, given as (column, row) in the 13×13 square: (13,7) the middle of the right side, (11,11) front right, (7,13) middle of the front, (3,11) front left, (1,7) the middle of the left side, (3,3) back left, (7,1) middle of the back and (11,3) back right. Notice that this storey has a window at the front too, right above the door. Check: layers 9 and 13 are full 40-block rings, and layers 10, 11 and 12 have 32 blocks each, 176 blocks in all.",[]],
["Glaze the middle windows","Fill the 8 window gaps of the middle storey with columns of 3 glass panes on layers 10, 11 and 12, 24 panes in all. This time all 8 positions have a window, including the one in the middle of the front, above the door. Place the panes the same way as before, 1 in the middle of each block. Check: from outside, the middle storey has a ring of 8 windows, evenly spaced, each one directly above a window of the lower storey, plus the one over the door.",[]],
["Lay the second log band","Lay the second complete ring of dark oak logs on layer 14: 40 logs, in the same shape as the first band, again without any break. It marks the end of the middle storey. The upper floor and the balcony will both sit level with it later. Check: the two bands should line up exactly, one above the other, 6 layers apart, with 5 layers of stone between them.",[]],
["Raise the top walls","Raise the top storey of the wall, layers 15 to 20, which is 6 layers, with the ring the same shape as before: 5 solid blocks; then 2 blocks, a gap of 5, and 2 blocks; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then five rows of 1, gap of 11, 1; then 1, gap of 9, 1; then 2, gap of 7, 2; then 2, gap of 5, 2; and a last row of 5, the front, where the door is. Use the stone mix again, 111 stone bricks, 62 mossy and 26 cracked. The front row is special here, because it holds the balcony doorway. Leave the 3 middle blocks of the front row open on layers 15–17. Set a dark oak log on each end of the row, the 1st and the 5th block, from layer 15 to 18. Lay 3 dark oak planks across the top of the opening on layer 18, resting on the two logs. Above that, on layers 19 and 20, the front row is solid stone. Leave 7 window gaps open on layers 17–19, 1 block wide and 3 tall, at the same places as the lower storey: (13,7) the middle of the right side, (11,11) front right, (3,11) front left, (1,7) the middle of the left side, (3,3) back left, (7,1) middle of the back and (11,3) back right. There is no window at the front, because the balcony doorway takes it. That is 210 blocks: 8 logs, 3 planks and 199 stone bricks. Layer 20 is the top of the wall.",[]],
["Glaze the top windows","Fill the 7 window gaps of the top storey with columns of 3 glass panes on layers 17, 18 and 19, 21 panes in all. There is no window at the front, because the balcony doorway is there, so leave that doorway open. The windows of this storey sit just above the bookshelves you will put inside, so they will stay clear. Check: walk round the tower: you should see 7 windows on this storey, one directly above each window of the middle storey except the front one.",[]],
["Buttress with pilasters","Stand 8 stone pilasters on the plinth, pressed against the outside of the wall, in between the windows so that they never block one. Give each position as (column, row) in the 17×17 square of the plinth, counting from its left edge and its back edge: (15,12), (12,15), (6,15), (3,12), (3,6), (6,3), (12,3) and (15,6). The 2 at (6,15) and (12,15) flank the door, 1 block outside each door post. Each pilaster is 6 stone bricks tall (layers 2–7) with a stone brick slab on top on layer 8. Use plain, mossy and cracked bricks: 33 plain, 9 mossy and 6 cracked, plus 8 bottom slabs. The slab tops are where the lanterns go later, so keep the top face free.",[]],
["Shelve the ground floor","Line the ground floor with books. On layers 2–6, stand a bookshelf on every block of the floor’s stone edge ring, the 28 stone bricks you laid as the edge of the floor, so that the shelves fit tightly against the inside of the wall. Leave out three kinds of block. First, the 3 middle blocks of the front row, so that you can walk in from the door. Second, the block right inside each window on layers 4–6, so the glass is not covered. Third, the spaces where the staircase will run along the left side, from the front left round to the back left: for each stair of the first flight, which you build in the next step, leave the stair block and the 2 blocks above it empty so you can walk up, and fill the column under it with bookshelves, so that each stair has something to rest on. The 3D view shows the exact blocks. That is 103 bookshelves. Tip: do it layer by layer, and walk round the ring after each layer.",[]],
["Climb the first flight","Build the first flight of the staircase from 6 dark oak stairs, hugging the left wall and curving from the front left round towards the back left. Use the same grid as the floors, column 1 on the left edge of the 11×11 square that holds the floor circle and row 1 at the back, and write each stair as (column, row, layer). Place each stair facing the way it climbs, so that its high side is toward the next step: (3,10,2) facing the back; (3,9,3) facing left; (2,9,4) facing the back; (2,8,5) facing left; (1,8,6) facing the back; (1,7,7) facing the back. Each stair is 1 layer higher than the one before and 1 block to its side or in front of it. The first stair sits on the stone edge of the floor, and every other one stands on the bookshelf you built under it, so it has something to rest on. Check: the last stair, (1,7,7), is on layer 7, 1 layer below where the lower floor will go in the next step.",[]],
["Lay the lower floor","Lay the lower floor with dark oak planks on layer 8, level with the first log band, so its edge touches the logs all the way round. It is the full 11-across circle, with rows from the back of 5, 7, 9, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5 blocks, but with 2 blocks left open for the stairs. To place the openings, count inside the 11×11 square that holds the floor circle, column 1 on the left and row 1 at the back: leave out (1,7) and (1,8), the 7th and 8th blocks from the back along the left edge. They sit right above the last 2 stairs of the flight, so you climb through them. You step off the stairs onto the floor at (1,6). That is 95 planks. Check: there is no gap between the floor and the logs, and you can walk up the stairs and out onto the floor without jumping.",[]],
["Shelve the middle storey","Repeat on the middle storey, on layers 9–12, standing on the planks of the lower floor: a bookshelf on every block of the stone edge ring, which is the ring along the inside of the wall, 4 layers high. As before, leave out the block right inside each of the 8 windows (layers 10–12), the 3 middle blocks of the front row, and the blocks above the landing at (1,6) and above every stair of the second flight, which you build in the next step (the 3D view shows them), so there is room to walk up. Keep a bookshelf under each stair of that flight, so each one has something to rest on. 65 bookshelves. The windows now look out between stacks of books.",[]],
["Climb the second flight","Build the second flight of the staircase from 5 dark oak stairs, carrying on from the landing at (1,6) on layer 8. It uses the same grid as the first flight: column 1 on the left, row 1 at the back, each stair written (column, row, layer), each facing the way it climbs. The flight is (1,5,9) facing the back; (1,4,10) facing right; (2,4,11) facing the back; (2,3,12) facing right; (3,3,13) facing the back. Each stair is 1 layer higher than the one before and 1 block to its side or in front of it, and each one is placed on the floor or on a bookshelf below it. The one exception is (2,3,12), which has nothing under it, so place it against the wall and the bookshelf beside it. The last 2 stairs will climb through the opening in the upper floor, which you lay in the next step, and you will step off onto the floor at (3,2), layer 14. Check: the last stair, (3,3,13), is on layer 13, 1 layer below the floor.",[]],
["Lay the upper floor","Lay the upper floor with dark oak planks on layer 14, level with the second log band. It is the same 11-across circle as the lower floor, with rows from the back of 5, 7, 9, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5 blocks, again with 2 blocks left open for the stairs. Count inside the 11×11 square again, column 1 on the left and row 1 at the back: leave out (2,3) and (3,3), the 2nd and 3rd blocks from the left in the 3rd row from the back. They sit above the last 2 stairs, and you step off onto the floor at (3,2). That is 95 planks. Check: the floor is flush with the logs all the way round, with no gap to the wall.",[]],
["Shelve the top storey","The top storey is lighter: shelves only 2 high, on layers 15–16, on the planks of the upper floor, so that the windows above them (layers 17–19) stay clear and the room feels open. Stand a bookshelf on every block of the wall’s inner ring, except the 3 middle blocks of the front row, which are the way out to the balcony, and except the cells where the stairs arrive, at the back left. 48 bookshelves. Tip: the middle of this floor is for the enchanting dais, so leave it open.",[]],
["Roll out the purple carpet","Furnish the ground floor, starting with the carpet. On layer 2, on top of the lapis and the obsidian in the middle of the room, lay purple carpet in a disc 5 across without its 4 corners: rows from the back of 3, 5, 5, 5 and 3 blocks, 21 carpets in all. It covers exactly the lapis and obsidian you laid first, so that pattern now lies under the carpet. Tip: lay the carpet from the outside of the disc inwards, so you never stand on a block you have just covered.",[]],
["Set the desks","Place 4 dark oak planks on layer 2 as desks, in the 4 diagonal spots 2 blocks out from the centre block in each direction: 2 blocks to the left or right and 2 blocks to the front or back. On layer 3, put a lantern on the front-right desk and on the back-left one, and a flower pot on the front-left and the back-right ones. That is 8 blocks: 4 planks, 2 lanterns and 2 flower pots. Check: seen from above, the 4 desks make a square round the carpet, with the lanterns on one diagonal and the pots on the other.",[]],
["Roll out the red carpet","Climb up to the middle floor. On layer 9, lay a ring of 24 red carpets round the centre, covering every block between about 2 and 3 blocks from the middle. The rows from the back are 3; 2, a gap of 1, and 2; 2, gap of 3, 2; 1, gap of 5, 1; 2, gap of 3, 2; 2, gap of 1, 2; and 3. That makes a rounded ring 7 across. The 3×3 square in the middle stays bare, because the table will go there, and so do the 4 blocks 2 straight out from the centre, because the barrels will stand there. Check: the ring should look like a round rug with a hole, with 4 small gaps at the compass points.",[]],
["Set the round table","On the middle floor, on layer 9, build a round table in the bare middle of the carpet: a 3×3 square of dark oak slabs, bottom slabs, 9 in all. Then stand 4 barrels round it, on layer 9, on the 4 bare blocks 2 blocks straight out from the centre in front, behind, left and right, and put a lantern on top of each barrel on layer 10. That is 17 blocks: 9 slabs, 4 barrels and 4 lanterns. Check: the barrels touch the edge of the table's square at the middle of each side, and the red carpet fits round them.",[]],
["Lay the obsidian dais","Now the heart of the tower. Climb the second flight up to the top floor. On layer 15, in the middle of the room, lay a 5×5 square of obsidian, 25 blocks, centred on the centre block, so it sits directly above the lapis square of the ground floor. The rows from the back are 5, 5, 5, 5 and 5 blocks, and the middle block of the square is the middle of the whole tower. Check: from the top of the stairs, the dais is exactly 1 block above the floor you are standing on, and the square is centred on the same column as the chandelier you will hang later.",[]],
["Frame it in lapis","Lay a frame of lapis lazuli round the obsidian, 1 block wide, on layer 15, so the whole dais is 7×7. Leave out the back-left corner of the frame, which is the 3rd block from the left in the 3rd row from the back (counting inside the 11×11 square of the floors again), because that is where the stairs arrive: the gap is your way up onto the dais. That makes 23 lapis blocks, 7 + 7 + 5 + 5 round the 4 sides with 1 missing corner. Check: the lapis frame has one gap at the back left, exactly where you step off the stairs.",[]],
["Place the enchanting table","Place the enchanting table in the exact centre of the dais, on layer 16, standing on the obsidian. It is only 3 quarters of a block tall, so the top is lower than a full block, with a red cloth and an open book above it. Do not put anything in the 8 blocks right round it, on layers 16 and 17: the table only counts bookshelves that have exactly 1 block of free air between them and the table, which is why the shelves in the next step stand 2 blocks away.",[]],
["Ring it with bookshelves","Surround the table with the bookshelves it needs for the strongest enchantments. Stand them on the obsidian, 2 blocks from the table, 2 high (layers 16–17), in a U shape: a column of 5 on the left edge of the dais and a column of 5 on the right edge, and a row of 3 across the back between them. The front is open, so you can walk up to the table from the stairs. Check the count: 5 + 5 + 3 = 13 on layer 16 and 13 on layer 17, 26 bookshelves, which is the 15 the table needs for the highest level, with plenty to spare. The ring of 8 blocks right round the table stays empty.",[]],
["Grow the dais crystals","Add amethyst crystals to the lapis frame of the dais. Build 3 stacks, each of amethyst blocks standing on a lapis block. On the back-right corner of the frame, stack 3 blocks high on layers 16–18. On each of the 2 front corners, stack 2 high on layers 16–17. Leave the back-left corner clear, because the stairs arrive there. That is 7 blocks: 3 + 2 + 2. Check: the tallest crystal stands at the back right, and the two shorter ones at the two front corners.",[]],
["Build the balcony","Build a balcony round the top storey. On layer 14, flush with the dark oak log band in the wall, lay a ring of dark oak planks 2 blocks thick all the way round the outside of the wall, out to the edge of a circle 17 blocks across. In rows from the back, the ring is 5, 9, then 4 + a gap of 5 + 4, then 2 + gap of 9 + 2, 2 + gap of 11 + 2 twice, then 2 + gap of 13 + 2 five times, 2 + gap of 11 + 2 twice, 2 + gap of 9 + 2, 4 + gap of 5 + 4, 9 and 5. The gaps are where the wall ring and the inside of the tower are, so you place no planks there. 88 planks. The stone pilasters stop at layer 8, so the balcony hangs out above them, and you reach it through the doorway you left on layers 15–17.",[]],
["Raise the posts","Stand 8 dark oak logs on the outside edge of the balcony, at the same compass points as the stone pilasters below, but 1 block further out, on the outermost ring of the balcony. Make each post 6 blocks tall, on layers 15–20, so the tops reach layer 20, level with the top of the wall. Between them they will hold up the roof. Check: from the front, there should be a post on each side of the door, and none in front of the door itself. 48 logs.",[]],
["Fence the balcony","Between the posts, along the outer edge of the balcony on layer 15, stand dark oak fences, 37 in all, so that the edge has a continuous rail. Leave a gap 3 blocks wide in the middle of the front, in front of the balcony doorway, so you can step out onto the balcony. Fences join each other and the posts on their own, so you do not have to do anything about the corners. The rest of the balcony, the inner half, stays open to walk on.",[]],
["Lay the roof eave","Start the roof with the eave, on layer 21. It sits on top of the wall, which ends on layer 20, and on the 8 posts. It is a ring of weathered copper from the 11-across circle in the middle (left open for the ceiling) out to the edge of a circle 17 blocks across. The outer edge of the ring, 1 block thick, is weathered copper stairs, 48 of them, placed facing the tower so that the high side is toward the middle. The 2 blocks inside the stairs are full weathered copper blocks, 80 in all. It hangs out over the balcony like the brim of a hat. 128 blocks.",[]],
["Close the ceiling","Fill the hole in the middle of the eave, on layer 21, with dark oak planks: the 11-across circle (rows of 5, 7, 9, 11, 11, 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5), 97 planks. This closes the top room. The ceiling is flush with the eave, so from outside the roof looks like one solid layer, but from the inside it is the dark oak ceiling of the study, and it is what the chandeliers hang from, so do it before them.",[]],
["Hang the lower chandeliers","Hang chains and lanterns from the lower floor, to light the ground floor. Hang each chandelier from the block above it, building the chain downward and finishing with a lantern. In the centre, hang the main one: a chain on layers 7 and 6, then a lantern on layer 5. Then hang 4 small ones, 2 blocks out from the centre to the front, back, left and right, each with 1 chain on layer 7 and a lantern on layer 6. That is 11 blocks: 6 chains and 5 lanterns. Check: the main lantern hangs lowest, on layer 5, and the 4 small ones make a diamond round it.",[]],
["Hang the middle chandelier","Hang one chandelier in the middle hall, from the upper floor, in the centre of the room. Build it downward: a chain on layers 13 and 12, then a lantern on layer 11. That is 3 blocks: 2 chains and 1 lanterns. The lantern hangs 2 layers above the table, and the 4 barrel lanterns you set on layer 10 are 2 blocks to its sides, so nothing touches.",[]],
["Hang the study chandeliers","Hang the big chandelier from the ceiling of the study that you closed earlier, in the centre: chains on layers 20, 19 and 18, and a lantern on layer 17, directly above the enchanting table. Then hang 4 small ones 3 blocks out from the centre to the front, back, left and right, each a chain on layer 20 with a lantern under it on layer 19. That is 12 blocks: 7 chains and 5 lanterns. Check: the 4 small chandeliers hang above the lapis frame of the dais, not above the bookshelves.",[]],
["Start the copper cone","Start raising the cone above the eave with weathered copper, 2 layers at a time. Each layer is a ring 2 blocks thick that steps in as it rises. Layers 22 and 23 have an outside circle 15 across, with a hole 11 across. The rows from the back are 5, 9, then 3 + gap of 5 + 3, 3 + gap of 7 + 3, 2 + gap of 9 + 2, five rows of 2 + gap of 11 + 2, then 2 + gap of 9 + 2, 3 + gap of 7 + 3, 3 + gap of 5 + 3, 9 and 5. On layer 22, make the outer edge of the ring, 1 block thick, stairs, standing outside the tower and placing each one facing the middle. Use full blocks for the rest. Layer 23 is the same ring again, going straight up, all full blocks. That is 160 blocks: 40 stairs and 120 blocks.",[]],
["Narrow the cone","Raise the cone 2 more layers, on layers 24 and 25. The ring steps in to an outside circle 13 across, with a hole 9 across, and is still 2 blocks thick. The rows from the back are 5, 9, 3 + gap of 5 + 3, 2 + gap of 7 + 2, five rows of 2 + gap of 9 + 2, then 2 + gap of 7 + 2, 3 + gap of 5 + 3, 9 and 5. On layer 24, make the outer edge of the ring, 1 block thick, stairs facing the middle, and use full blocks for the rest. Layer 25 is the same ring again, going straight up, all full blocks. That is 136 blocks: 40 stairs and 96 blocks.",[]],
["Step the cone in again","Raise the last two layers of weathered copper, layers 26 and 27, with a smaller ring each time. Layer 26 has an outside circle 11 across and a hole 7 across: the rows from the back are 5, 7, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, 3 + gap of 5 + 3, three rows of 2 + gap of 7 + 2, 3 + gap of 5 + 3, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, 7 and 5. Layer 27 has an outside circle 9 across and a hole 5 across: 5, 7, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, three rows of 2 + gap of 5 + 2, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, 7 and 5. On both layers, make the outer edge of the ring, 1 block thick, stairs facing the middle, and use full blocks for the rest. That is 108 blocks: 60 stairs and 48 blocks.",[]],
["Switch to oxidized copper","Switch to oxidized copper, the greener one, for the top of the cone, on layers 28–29. Layer 28 is a ring 2 blocks thick with an outside circle 9 across and a hole 5 across: the rows from the back are 5, 7, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, 2 + gap of 5 + 2 three times, 3 + gap of 3 + 3, 7 and 5. It is all full blocks, with no stairs. Layer 29 is a ring with an outside of 7 across and a hole 3 across: rows 3, 5, 2 + gap of 3 + 2 three times, 5 and 3. On layer 29, make the outer edge of the ring, 1 block thick, stairs facing the middle. That is 76 blocks: 60 full blocks and 16 stairs.",[]],
["Taper the green roof","Raise two solid discs of oxidized copper, 5 across, on layers 30 and 31. Each has the rows 3, 5, 5, 5 and 3 from the back. On layer 30, make the outer edge, 1 block thick, stairs facing the middle: 12 stairs round 9 full blocks. Layer 31 is the same disc again, all 21 full blocks. That is 42 blocks: 12 stairs and 30 full blocks. Check: the cone now narrows by 1 block on each side every 2 layers.",[]],
["Top out the roof","Finish the roof with the narrowest part. Layers 32 and 33 are a solid 3×3 square. On layer 32, make the outer ring of 8 blocks stairs facing the middle, with a full block in the very centre. Layer 33 is a full 3×3 square of 9 full blocks. Layer 34 is a single full block in the very middle. That is 19 blocks: 8 stairs and 11 full blocks. Check: from the front, the whole cone should narrow smoothly from the eave to this block.",[]],
["Crown the spire","Finish the roof with a spire on the single block at the top. Stack 2 amethyst blocks on it, on layers 35 and 36, and put a lightning rod on top of them on layer 37, so the tower is 37 layers tall in all. The rod is thin and stands in the middle of its block, so it looks like a needle. 3 blocks. Tip: step back and check the silhouette: the cone should narrow smoothly from the eave to the amethyst.",[]],
["Light the ground lamps","Add the lights outside, at ground level. First, put a lantern on top of each of the 8 pilaster slabs, on layer 9: 8 lanterns. Then, in front of the door, make 2 lamp posts on the ring of steps, 3 blocks to the left and 3 to the right of the door’s centre and 8 blocks in front of the centre block: 2 dark oak fences stacked on layers 2–3, with a lantern on top, on layer 4. That is 14 blocks: 10 lanterns and 4 fences. Check: the 2 lamp posts stand on the stairs, one on each side of the path, which comes later.",[]],
["Light the balcony","Stand 7 lanterns on top of the balcony fence, on layer 16, on the fences at the 8 compass points (the 4 sides and the 4 diagonals). Leave out the front, where there is a gap in the fence. Check: from a distance, the 7 lanterns make a ring of light round the tower at the level of the top storey.",[]],
["Stand the outdoor crystals","Stand 4 amethyst crystals on the ring of steps, each 2 blocks high, on layers 2–3. Place them 8 blocks to the left of the centre block and 3 blocks behind it, 8 left and 3 in front, 8 to the right and 3 behind, and 8 to the right and 3 in front. That is 8 blocks. They match the spire and the crystals on the dais.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Ring the steps with bushes, on layers 1–2. Just outside the ring of steps, on layer 1, set oak leaves and flowering azalea leaves in a loose, broken ring, about every second block, using 20 oak and 15 flowering azalea leaves in all. Stack a second leaf block on top of a few of them so the bushes differ in height. Leave a gap about 5 blocks wide in front of the door, where the path comes in. That is 35 blocks.",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","Just outside the bushes, on layer 1, scatter 5 short grass and the flowers: 3 alliums, 3 dandelions, 3 oxeye daisies, 2 pink tulips and 1 poppy. Keep the pattern irregular, and keep the same 5-block gap in front of the door. That is 17 blocks.",[]],
["Lay the path","Finish with the path. On layer 1, in front of the door, lay a path of cobblestone 3 blocks wide and 5 long, from the front of the ring of steps to the edge of the build, in line with the middle of the door: 15 blocks, 9 plain and 6 mossy. Mix the mossy ones in at random. Then step back and look at the whole build: the lit windows, the green roof, the amethyst spire, and, up inside, the quiet study where the enchanting table waits.",[]]]},
{id:"forgotten-library-ruins",name:"Forgotten Library Ruins",emoji:"📚",tags:["Libraries", "Medieval", "Nature"],diff:"Hard",time:"5 hr",size:"25×30",photos:[P16],pr:[1.296],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A roofless gothic library taken over by moss, leaves and an ancient tree: broken stone walls with arched window bays, a stub of a turret, surviving dark oak beams with hanging lanterns, shelf-lined aisles and a purple runner. At the far end an enchanting table stands on an obsidian dais ringed by 26 bookshelves and amethyst.",steps:[["Mark out the floor plan","Find flat ground and mark an area 25 wide and 30 deep. The gate is at the front, and left and right are as you face it. On layer 1, lay the floor of the ruin. The nave is a rectangle 11 wide, the 8th to 18th blocks from the left, and 15 long, with the gate wall as its front edge. At its back end add the apse, a half circle 11 across, whose rows from the nave to the back are 11, 11, 11, 9, 7 and 5 blocks wide. Then add 4 buttress pads, 2 blocks wide, on each side of the nave, on the 2nd, 6th, 10th and 14th rows of the nave counting from the back. Mix 93 stone bricks, 73 mossy and 44 cracked, and on the inside (the 9-wide nave floor and the apse), swap about 1 block in 8 for coarse dirt or moss, where the floor is breaking up: 15 coarse dirt and 10 moss blocks. That is 235 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the broken side walls","On the outer edge of the nave, build the 2 long walls, 15 blocks each, rows 1–15 counting from the apse end. Their heights, in blocks above the floor, are 10, 11, 9, 8, 10, 11, 10, 6, 5, 11, 9, 4, 3, 10, 6 for the left wall and 4, 6, 3, 4, 2, 11, 3, 2, 2, 11, 4, 3, 2, 6, 3 for the right one, so the left stays tall and the right is mostly stumps. The tall piers stand on rows 2, 6, 10 and 14. Window bays are rows 3–5, 7–9 and 11–13: wherever the wall is more than 2 blocks high, leave a hole 3 wide and 4 tall, layers 4–7, with a 1-wide pointed top 2 higher in the middle row of the bay (layers 8–9). Cap a few tops with a stone brick slab or stair: 7 slabs and 5 stairs. Mix 70 stone bricks, 44 mossy and 36 cracked. That is 162 blocks.",[]],
["Curve the apse wall","Round the back of the nave, build the curved wall of the apse, 1 block thick, standing on the half circle edge (the outline of the semicircle, 15 blocks long). Its heights, going from the left wall round to the right wall, are 10, 7, 11, 10, a gap, 10, 8, a gap, 11, 6, a gap, 11, 9, 8, 11. Each gap is a narrow window 1 wide, a wall 2 blocks tall only, so it opens from layer 4 to the top. Cap 3 tops with slabs and 2 with stairs. Mix 50 stone bricks, 48 mossy and 20 cracked. That is 123 blocks, up to layer 12.",[]],
["Build the gate wall","At the front of the nave, on its 15th row, build the front wall between the 2 side walls, with a gateway in the middle, 3 wide and 4 tall (layers 2–5), the 5th to 7th of the row’s 11 blocks. From the left, the whole front row is 6, 5, 7 and 10 blocks high, then the gate, then 10, 6, 3 and 3. The first and the last of those belong to the side walls you already built. Over the gate, lay a broken arch: 3 blocks on layer 6, 2 blocks on layer 7 (the left 2) and 1 block on layer 8 (the left one), so the arch has fallen in on the right. Mix 21 stone bricks, 16 mossy and 10 cracked. That is 47 blocks, not counting the 2 end blocks that belong to the side walls.",[]],
["Prop the walls with buttresses","On the 4 buttress pads on each side, build the buttresses: 2 columns against the wall, a tall one against the wall and a shorter one behind it. Going from the back, on the left the tall columns are 7, 9, 5 and 3 blocks and the short ones 4, 6, 2 and 1. On the right, the tall ones are 3, 5, 6 and 3 and the short ones 1, 2, 3 and 1. Put a stone brick stair on top of each tall one, 8 in all, with the high side toward the wall. That is 69 blocks: 8 stairs and 28 stone bricks, 23 mossy and 10 cracked.",[]],
["Add the broken turret","To the left of the nave, outside the buttresses and centred on the 4th row from the back, build the stump of a round stair turret, 5 across (the 5×5 square without its corners), standing on layer 1 (layers 1–9). Its ring is 12 blocks, 1 thick, ragged and 4 to 8 tall, and its floor inside is a mix of coarse dirt and moss. The leftmost wall block is 6 tall or more and has a window 2 tall in it, on layers 4–5. In the middle stands a pillar of 6 dark oak logs, and 5 stone brick stairs climb round it, 1 each on layers 2–6, starting on its right side and curling through the front to its left. That is 99 blocks.",[]],
["Stand the broken columns","Inside the nave, stand 6 columns from layer 2: on the 3rd and 7th blocks of the 9-wide inside, on the 4th, 8th and 12th rows from the back. Start each with a stone brick slab, and stack stone bricks above it, to a different height each. From the back, the left column heights are 9, 7 and 8 blocks, and the right ones are 4, 3 and 5, counting the slab. The short ones end with a cracked block. That is 36 blocks.",[]],
["Lay the surviving roof beams","On layer 11, between the tall piers, lay 2 dark oak log beams across the nave, 9 blocks long each, on the 6th and 10th rows from the back. On the middle of the first beam, stand a king post, 3 logs high on layers 12–14. Brace it with 4 dark oak stairs, 2 on each side on layers 12 and 13, with the high side toward the post. That is 25 blocks: 21 logs and 4 stairs.",[]],
["Roll out the runner","On layer 2, lay a runner 3 blocks wide down the middle of the nave, from the gate row (the 15th) to the 4th row from the back, so it ends just before the dais: 36 blocks. The middle strip is purple carpet and the 2 side strips red, but swap some for moss carpet, where the carpet has rotted: 8 purple, 14 red and 14 moss.",[]],
["Fill the bays with bookshelves","Line both walls inside with bookshelves, on the 1st and 9th blocks of the nave, along rows 1–14. They are normally 2 high on layers 2–3, and 4 high where a pier stands (rows 2, 6, 10 and 14). Leave random gaps, about 1 in 8 at the bottom and 1 in 3 higher up, so the shelves look picked over. That is 51 bookshelves.",[]],
["Set the reading nooks","On the 6th and 10th rows from the back, make 4 reading nooks, 1 on each side of the nave. On each side of the runner, put a dark oak slab on layer 2 as a desk, on the 2nd block from the left (and the 2nd from the right), with a lantern on top (layer 3) and a dark oak stair as a chair beside it, on the aisle side, with its back to the aisle. Then add 3 barrels on layer 2: on the 2nd block from the left at rows 4 and 12, and on the 2nd from the right at row 8. That is 15 blocks.",[]],
["Scatter fallen stone","Pile 12 blocks of fallen stone on layers 2–3, as if the walls had dropped them: 1–2 high, at the foot of both long walls inside, on the 2nd block from each wall, and a few nearer the aisle. It is mostly cobblestone (11) and 1 mossy cobblestone.",[]],
["Raise the altar dais","At the centre of the apse, on layer 2, build the dais. Lay a 5×5 square of obsidian, 25 blocks, with its middle on the apse’s centre block, which is 1 row behind the first row of the long walls. Round it, lay a 7×7 frame of lapis lazuli, 1 wide, but at the front, in the middle, put 3 stone brick stairs in place of lapis, facing the dais, so they work as steps. That is 49 blocks: 25 obsidian, 21 lapis and 3 stairs.",[]],
["Place the enchanting table","Place an enchanting table on layer 3, in the exact centre of the dais. It stands on the obsidian, and it is only 3 quarters of a block high. The 8 blocks round it must stay empty for the bookshelves to count.",[]],
["Surround it with bookshelves","Round the table, build the 26 bookshelves that make it strongest, 2 blocks from it and 2 high (layers 3–4): 2 columns of 5 on the left and right edges of the dais, and a row of 3 across the back between them. The front is open, where the steps are. That is 26 bookshelves.",[]],
["Grow the crystals","Grow amethyst on the lapis frame: 3 blocks high on both back corners (layers 3–5) and 2 high on both front corners (layers 3–4), 10 blocks. Then add a lone 2-high crystal pushing up through the floor on layers 2–3, on the 8th block from the left of the nave on the 4th row from the back. That is 12 amethyst blocks.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang lanterns from the 2 beams on chains. For each beam, hang 2 chains on layers 9–10 with a lantern below on layer 8, 1 block in from each end, and in the middle 3 chains on layers 8–10 with a lantern on layer 7. Then put a lantern on layer 5 on the top of the back corners of the altar shelves, and stand lanterns on top of 3 of the columns (layers 11, 10 and 7) and on top of each gate pier (layer 12). That is 14 chains and 13 lanterns.",[]],
["Restore a few windowpanes","In the window bays and the 3 narrow gaps of the apse, put a few glass panes, only where there is a block beside or below to hold them, about 2 in 5: 31 panes, on layers 4–9. They should look like the last bits of glass left in the frames.",[]],
["Let the ruin overgrow","Let nature reclaim the stone. Along the tops of the walls and the apse, set 8 moss carpets and clumps of leaves: 20 oak leaves and 17 flowering azalea leaves in all, spread between the wall tops, up the outside of the left and right walls (not at the buttresses) in strips 1–5 high, and 6 hanging under the second beam on layers 9–10. That is 45 blocks.",[]],
["Plant the old tree","Behind the right side of the apse, plant an ancient tree. Make a trunk 2×2 of dark oak logs, 8 tall (layers 2–9), with its nearest edge 2 blocks to the right of the apse wall and on the 5th and 6th rows from the back. Then the canopy: a 6-across disc on layer 8, 8 across on layer 9, 10 across on layer 10, 8 across on layer 11, 6 across on layer 12 and 4 across on layer 13, all rounded, using oak leaves with about 1 in 5 flowering azalea. That is 264 blocks: 32 logs and 232 leaves.",[]],
["Plant the garden","Around the ruin, on layers 1–2, plant what has grown up since it was abandoned: 7 moss blocks, 18 short grass, bushes of 12 oak and 8 flowering azalea leaves (some stacked 2 high), and wildflowers: 6 poppies, 3 oxeye daisies, 3 dandelions, 2 alliums and 2 pink tulips. Keep the gate path and the tree clear. That is 61 blocks.",[]],
["Lay the gate path","Finish with a path from the gate to the edge of the build, 3 blocks wide and 6 long, on layer 1: 10 cobblestone and 8 mossy. Then stand a lamp post on each side of it, 1 block out and 3 blocks in front of the gate: 2 dark oak fences on layers 1–2 with a lantern on top (layer 3). That is 24 blocks. Walk up the path and look through the gateway: the runner leads you to the altar at the far end.",[]]]},
{id:"fairy-mushroom-tower",name:"Fairy Mushroom Tower",emoji:"🍄",tags:["Fantasy", "Houses", "Nature"],diff:"Expert",time:"12 hr",size:"25×25",photos:[P17],pr:[0.853],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A tall fairy toadstool you can live in: a cream stem with a spiral wooden stair winding round it, three little rooms inside, a ring of glowing gills hung with fairy lights, and a great red cap spotted with white and fitted with dormer windows, balconies and a tiny brown chimney. A lily pond, a mossy path, a flowery garden and a lantern tree sit round the foot.",steps:[["Lay the ground","Everything stands on a round patch of grass, so start there, all on layer 1. Mark the centre block of your site and call it (13,13): columns are counted from the left and rows from the back, as you stand at the front, and the whole build fits in a 25 by 25 square. Fill a circle 25 blocks across around that centre. Counting rows from the back, the circle is: 7, 11, 15, 17, 19, 21, 21, 23, 23, then seven rows of 25, then 23, 23, 21, 21, 19, 17, 15, 11, 7. Then leave three patches empty, because they are laid in the next steps: the 9-across circle in the middle (that is the floor of the tower), the pond at the front left (columns 4–8, rows 18–22, a 5 by 5 square without its corners) and the winding path from the door. That leaves 380 blocks to place. Scatter them at random so the ground looks patchy: 200 grass blocks, 97 coarse dirt and 83 moss blocks, roughly half grass and a quarter each of dirt and moss. Avoid straight lines or stripes. Check: the ground is one flat layer, with a round hole in the middle and a few small gaps at the front.",[]],
["Lay the winding path","Make the path with 20 dirt path blocks, still on layer 1, filling the gaps you left at the front. Start right in front of the door, which will stand in the middle of the front, and let the path wander to the front right. Counting rows from the back: row 19 has 6 blocks (columns 12–17), rows 20 and 21 have 3 blocks each (columns 12–14), row 22 has 2 (columns 13–14), row 23 has 2 (columns 14–15), row 24 has 2 (columns 15–16) and row 25 has 2 (columns 16–17). Dirt path blocks are made by using a shovel on grass or dirt; in creative mode you can place them directly. Check: a 6-wide landing in front of the door that narrows to 3, then to 2 as it curves away to the right, 20 blocks in all.",[]],
["Dig the lily pond","Build the little fairy pond at the front left, columns 4–8 and rows 18–22. On layer 1 lay a ring of 12 mossy cobblestone: 3 blocks along the top row (columns 5–7, row 18), 3 along the bottom row (columns 5–7, row 22), and one on each side of the three middle rows (columns 4 and 8, rows 19, 20 and 21). Fill the 3 by 3 hole in the middle, columns 5–7 and rows 19–21, with water: 9 water blocks. On layer 2 float 7 lily pads on the water, on every water block except the two at the top left, (5,19) and (6,19): that is (7,19), (5,20), (6,20), (7,20), (5,21), (6,21) and (7,21). Finish the edge on layer 2 with 4 mossy cobblestone slabs at (5,18), (7,18), (8,21) and (7,22), and one dandelion at (6,22). Check: a small round pond with a rough mossy rim, mostly covered in lily pads.",[]],
["Lay the ground floor","Fill the middle circle you left empty, on layer 1, with the floor of the tower. The circle is 9 across, with rows from the back of 5, 7, then five rows of 9, then 7, 5, 69 blocks in all. Lay it in four rings, working outwards: a 3 by 3 square of dark oak planks in the middle (9 blocks); round it a ring of spruce planks that makes the shape 5 across without corners (12 blocks, the circle of 21 minus the 9); then a ring of dark oak planks to make it 7 across (16 blocks); and finally an outer ring of spruce planks that fills the 9-across circle (32 blocks). That is 25 dark oak planks and 44 spruce planks. Check: a round floor with dark and light rings like the rings of a tree trunk, touching the ground on every side.",[]],
["Raise the lower stem","Now raise the stem of the mushroom, the pale tower that holds everything up. The first storey is layers 2 to 8, 7 layers, and its shape is a ring 1 block thick, 11 across on the outside, standing on the ground round the edge of the floor. Counting rows from the back (rows 8 to 18), the ring on a full layer is: 5; 1, gap of 5, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 5, 1; 5. That is 28 blocks on a full layer. Build the wall with a random mix so it looks like a mushroom stalk: 90 mushroom stem, 33 white concrete, 27 smooth sandstone, plus 14 moss blocks scattered about 1 block in 5 on layers 2–4 only, to make the foot of the stem look mossy. Leave these gaps: the door in the middle of the front row (row 18), 3 blocks wide at columns 12–14 and 3 layers high, layers 2–4; and 4 window gaps, each 1 block wide and 2 high on layers 4–5, at (8,12) and (8,15) on the left, (13,8) in the middle of the back and (18,15) on the right. Frame the door with dark oak logs, 4 high on layers 2–5, at both side posts, which are the wall blocks at columns 11 and 15 of row 18, and lay a dark oak plank above each of the 3 door gaps on layer 5, so the doorway has a little lintel. Put a dark oak plank on layer 6 above each of the 4 window gaps. Count: 8 logs and 7 planks.",[]],
["Roof the front door","Give the front door a little awning, between layers 2 and 5. On layer 5, in the row just in front of the wall (row 19), place 5 dark oak stairs at columns 11, 12, 13, 14 and 15, each one facing the tower with the high side against the wall. Right in front of them, on layer 5 in row 20, place a dark oak slab on each of the same columns: 5 slabs. Hold the awning up with fence posts, 3 high on layers 2–4: two in row 20 at columns 11 and 15, and two further out in row 19 at columns 10 and 16, 12 fences in all. Top the two outer posts with a lantern each, on layer 5 at (10,19) and (16,19). Then hang a lantern from the underside of the slab on layer 4 at (12,20) and (14,20). That is 4 lanterns. Check: a 5-wide dark oak awning over the path, with a lantern at each end and 2 hanging above the doorway.",[]],
["Wind the stair: first flight","The staircase winds round the outside of the stem, counter-clockwise when you look down on it, and every layer is a short tread 2 blocks wide, the inside block touching the stem and the outside block next to it. Each tread starts with stairs that you climb by walking in the direction named (place each stair while looking that way), then planks, spruce planks on even layers and dark oak planks on odd ones. This first flight goes from layer 2 to layer 8, one tread per layer, 57 blocks: 16 stairs, 23 spruce planks and 18 dark oak planks. It starts at the front right, just beside the door, and climbs right, then round the back and up the left side. Layer 2: stairs at (16,18), (17,18), (17,19) facing right; spruce planks at (17,17), (18,16), (18,17), (18,18), (19,16), (19,17). Layer 3: stairs at (19,15), (20,15) facing toward the back; dark oak planks at (19,12), (19,13), (19,14), (20,12), (20,13), (20,14). Layer 4: stairs at (19,11), (20,11) facing toward the back; spruce planks at (17,9), (18,8), (18,9), (18,10), (19,9), (19,10). Layer 5: stairs at (17,7), (17,8) facing left; dark oak planks at (14,6), (14,7), (15,6), (15,7), (16,7), (16,8). Layer 6: stairs at (13,6), (13,7) facing left; spruce planks at (10,7), (11,6), (11,7), (12,6), (12,7). Layer 7: stairs at (9,7), (9,8), (10,8) facing left; dark oak planks at (7,9), (7,10), (8,8), (8,9), (8,10), (9,9). Layer 8: stairs at (6,11), (7,11) facing toward the front; spruce planks at (6,12), (6,13), (6,14), (7,12), (7,13), (7,14). Check: from above the treads form a spiral of about three quarters of a turn, and every tread is 1 block higher than the last.",[]],
["Fence the first flight","Put a fence on top of the outside block of every tread of the first flight, so the stair has a handrail on its outer edge, 28 fences in all. Each tread has 4 outer blocks, and each fence stands 1 layer above its tread, on layers 3–9. Layer 3: (17,19), (18,18), (19,17), (19,16). Layer 4: (20,15), (20,14), (20,13), (20,12). Layer 5: (20,11), (19,10), (19,9), (18,8). Layer 6: (17,7), (16,7), (15,6), (14,6). Layer 7: (13,6), (12,6), (11,6), (10,7). Layer 8: (9,7), (8,8), (7,9), (7,10). Layer 9: (6,11), (6,12), (6,13), (6,14). Then stand 4 lanterns on top of fence posts, so the stair is lit as you climb: (19,16) on layer 4, (19,9) on layer 6, (12,6) on layer 8, (6,11) on layer 10. Check: a continuous fence runs along the outside of the stair, never on the inside edge against the stem, and a lantern glows every few treads.",[]],
["Glaze the lower windows","Fill the 4 window gaps of the lower storey with glass panes, a column of 2 on layers 4 and 5 in each gap, 8 panes in all, at (8,12), (8,15), (13,8) and (18,15). Panes are thin and sit in the middle of the block, so each window looks like a narrow slit in the stem. Outside the two windows on the left and the one at the back, lay a dark oak plank on layer 3 just below the gap as a sill: at (7,12), (7,15) and (13,7). The fourth window, on the right at (18,15), already has the stair tread below it, so it needs no sill. Finish by putting a flower pot on layer 4 outside each of the 4 windows, one block out from the glass, on top of its sill or tread: at (7,12), (7,15), (13,7) and (19,15). That is 4 flower pots and 3 sill planks. Plant a flower in each pot if you like; the red and pink ones look best. Check: 4 small windows round the foot of the tower, 3 with a plank ledge and a pot, 1 with a pot on the stair.",[]],
["Raise the middle stem","Raise the middle storey of the stem, layers 9 to 20, which is 12 layers, on top of the lower one. The ring is the same shape as before, 11 across on the outside and 28 blocks on a full layer: 5; 1, gap of 5, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 5, 1; 5. Use the same random mix, 173 mushroom stem, 75 white concrete, 33 smooth sandstone, and no more moss. This storey has more openings, so build it layer by layer. Layer 9 is a plain ring. On layers 10–12 leave a doorway, door B, with 3 gaps on the front left, at (8,14), (8,15) and (9,16), and frame it with dark oak logs 4 high, on layers 10–13, at the two blocks on either side of it, (8,13) and (10,17). On layer 13 lay the lintel: dark oak planks at (8,14), (8,15), (9,16), and also in the two outside corners (8,16) and (9,17), which are not part of the ring but join the lintel into one line. Leave 5 window gaps, each 1 block wide and 2 high: at (14,8) on layers 13–14, at (11,8), (13,18) and (18,14) on layers 14–15, and at (18,11) on layers 15–16, with a dark oak plank above each one, on layers 15, 16, 16, 16 and 17. Leave the bay opening in the middle of the front, 3 gaps at (12,18), (13,18) and (14,18) on layers 17–18, with dark oak logs at (11,18) and (15,18) on layers 17–19 and 3 dark oak planks across the top at (12,18), (13,18) and (14,18) on layer 19. On layer 20 start the second doorway, door C, with 3 gaps at (8,15), (9,16) and (10,17) and a dark oak log either side, at (8,14) and (11,18). Count: 16 dark oak logs and 13 dark oak planks in the frames. Check: from the front you can see the tall bay opening above the door, and 5 small windows are scattered round the sides.",[]],
["Lay the second floor","Lay the floor of the middle room on layer 9, inside the wall you just raised: the same pattern as the ground floor. It is a circle 9 across, rows from the back 5, 7, then five rows of 9, then 7, 5, 69 blocks, in 4 rings: a 3 by 3 square of dark oak planks in the middle, a ring of spruce planks to 5 across, a ring of dark oak planks to 7 across and an outer ring of spruce planks that touches the wall, 25 dark oak and 44 spruce planks. Check: the floor is level with the bottom of the second doorway and has the same rings as the floor below.",[]],
["Wind the stair: second flight","The staircase winds round the outside of the stem, counter-clockwise when you look down on it, and every layer is a short tread 2 blocks wide, the inside block touching the stem and the outside block next to it. Each tread starts with stairs that you climb by walking in the direction named (place each stair while looking that way), then planks, spruce planks on even layers and dark oak planks on odd ones. The second flight carries on from where the first one ended, at the front left, and runs from layer 9 to layer 14, 48 blocks: 13 stairs, 18 spruce planks and 17 dark oak planks. It passes in front of door B, which is the doorway you left in the wall at layer 10, then crosses the front above the awning, goes up the right side and starts curving round the back. Layer 9: stairs at (6,15), (7,15) facing toward the front; dark oak planks at (7,16), (7,17), (8,16), (8,17), (8,18), (9,17). Layer 10: stairs at (9,18), (9,19) facing right; spruce planks at (10,18), (10,19), (11,19), (11,20), (12,19), (12,20). Layer 11: stairs at (13,19), (13,20) facing right; dark oak planks at (14,19), (14,20), (15,19), (15,20), (16,19). Layer 12: stairs at (16,18), (17,18), (17,19) facing right; spruce planks at (17,17), (18,16), (18,17), (18,18), (19,16), (19,17). Layer 13: stairs at (19,15), (20,15) facing toward the back; dark oak planks at (19,12), (19,13), (19,14), (20,12), (20,13), (20,14). Layer 14: stairs at (19,11), (20,11) facing toward the back; spruce planks at (17,9), (18,8), (18,9), (18,10), (19,9), (19,10). Check: the layer 9 tread is level with the floor of the second room, so you can walk straight from it into door B.",[]],
["Fence the second flight","Put a fence on top of the outside block of every tread of the second flight, so the stair has a handrail on its outer edge, 24 fences in all. Each tread has 4 outer blocks, and each fence stands 1 layer above its tread, on layers 10–15. Layer 10: (6,15), (7,16), (7,17), (8,18). Layer 11: (9,19), (10,19), (11,20), (12,20). Layer 12: (13,20), (14,20), (15,20), (16,19). Layer 13: (17,19), (18,18), (19,17), (19,16). Layer 14: (20,15), (20,14), (20,13), (20,12). Layer 15: (20,11), (19,10), (19,9), (18,8). Then stand 3 lanterns on top of fence posts, so the stair is lit as you climb: (8,18) on layer 11, (15,20) on layer 13, (20,14) on layer 15. Check: a continuous fence runs along the outside of the stair, never on the inside edge against the stem, and a lantern glows every few treads.",[]],
["Frame and roof door B","Finish door B, the doorway on the front left at layers 10–12. The walkway of layer 9 runs past it, but the tread does not reach the middle of the door, so put 1 dark oak plank on layer 9 at (7,14) as a doorstep. Then give the door a little hood, a curved row of 4 dark oak stairs on layer 13, one block out from the lintel, at (7,13), (7,14), (7,15) and (10,18), each with its high side against the wall. Lay a dark oak slab on top of each stair on layer 14: 4 slabs. The two outside corners of the lintel, (8,16) and (9,17), are already planks, so the hood has a gap there. Hang a lantern under each end of the hood, on layer 12 at (7,13) and (10,18): 2 lanterns. Check: the doorway has a small dark roof with a lantern at each end, and you can step from the stair straight into it without jumping.",[]],
["Glaze the middle windows","Glaze the 5 windows of the middle storey. In each gap put a column of 2 glass panes, 10 panes in all: at (14,8) on layers 13–14, at (11,8), (13,18) and (18,14) on layers 14–15 and at (18,11) on layers 15–16. Now dress the outside of each window. Lay a dark oak plank sill on the layer below the gap, just outside the wall, at (14,7) on layer 12, at (11,7) on layer 13 and at (13,19) on layer 13: 3 planks. The two windows on the right, at (18,14) and (18,11), already have the stair tread of the second flight right below them, so they need no sill. Put a flower pot 1 block out from the glass on each window, at (14,7) on layer 13, at (11,7) and (19,14) and (13,19) on layer 14, and at (19,11) on layer 15: 5 pots in all. Check: 5 windows, three on planks with a pot, and two on the right that sit above the stair with a pot on the tread.",[]],
["Build the front balcony","Make the little balcony above the door, in the bay opening you left in the front wall at columns 12–14 on layers 17–18. First put glass panes in the two outer gaps, (12,18) and (14,18), on layers 17 and 18, 4 panes, and leave the middle gap (13,18) open: it is the balcony door. Then lay the floor of the balcony on layer 16, 5 blocks wide and 2 deep, with dark oak planks at columns 11–15 of rows 19 and 20: 10 planks. Fence the edge on layer 17: a fence at (11,19) and (11,20), at (12,20), (13,20) and (14,20), and at (15,19) and (15,20), 7 fences. Stand a lantern on the two corner posts, on layer 18 at (11,20) and (15,20), and a flower pot on the floor in front of each pane, on layer 17 at (12,19) and (14,19). That is 2 lanterns and 2 pots. Check: a small railed balcony with a clear doorway in the middle and two glazed panels either side.",[]],
["Wind the stair: third flight","The staircase winds round the outside of the stem, counter-clockwise when you look down on it, and every layer is a short tread 2 blocks wide, the inside block touching the stem and the outside block next to it. Each tread starts with stairs that you climb by walking in the direction named (place each stair while looking that way), then planks, spruce planks on even layers and dark oak planks on odd ones. The third flight carries on at the back right and runs from layer 15 to layer 19, 43 blocks: 11 stairs, 11 spruce planks and 21 dark oak planks. It goes round the back and along the left side to the front left again, directly above the first turn, and ends in a longer landing in front of door C. Layer 15: stairs at (17,7), (17,8) facing left; dark oak planks at (14,6), (14,7), (15,6), (15,7), (16,7), (16,8). Layer 16: stairs at (13,6), (13,7) facing left; spruce planks at (10,7), (11,6), (11,7), (12,6), (12,7). Layer 17: stairs at (9,7), (9,8), (10,8) facing left; dark oak planks at (7,9), (7,10), (8,8), (8,9), (8,10), (9,9). Layer 18: stairs at (6,11), (7,11) facing toward the front; spruce planks at (6,12), (6,13), (6,14), (7,12), (7,13), (7,14). Layer 19: stairs at (6,15), (7,15) facing toward the front; dark oak planks at (7,16), (7,17), (8,16), (8,17), (8,18), (9,17), (9,18), (9,19), (10,18). Check: the stair has now made two full turns round the stem and the landing of layer 19 is level with the bottom of door C.",[]],
["Fence the third flight","Put a fence on top of the outside block of every tread of the third flight, so the stair has a handrail on its outer edge, 21 fences in all. Each tread has 4 outer blocks (the last one, the landing, has 5), and each fence stands 1 layer above its tread, on layers 16–20. Layer 16: (17,7), (16,7), (15,6), (14,6). Layer 17: (13,6), (12,6), (11,6), (10,7). Layer 18: (9,7), (8,8), (7,9), (7,10). Layer 19: (6,11), (6,12), (6,13), (6,14). Layer 20: (6,15), (7,16), (7,17), (8,18), (9,19). Then stand 3 lanterns on top of fence posts, so the stair is lit as you climb: (17,7) on layer 17, (10,7) on layer 18, (6,13) on layer 20. Check: a continuous fence runs along the outside of the stair, never on the inside edge against the stem, and a lantern glows every few treads.",[]],
["Lay the third floor","Lay the floor of the top room on layer 19, inside the wall: the same four-ring pattern as the floors below. It is a circle 9 across, rows from the back 5, 7, then five rows of 9, then 7, 5, 69 blocks: a 3 by 3 square of dark oak planks in the middle, a ring of spruce planks to 5 across, a ring of dark oak planks to 7 across and an outer ring of spruce planks, 25 dark oak and 44 spruce planks. Check: the floor is level with the landing outside and with the bottom of door C.",[]],
["Raise the top stem","Raise the top of the stem, layers 21 to 27, 7 layers. The ring is the same 11-across shape, 28 blocks on a full layer: 5; 1, gap of 5, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 9, 1; 1, gap of 7, 1; 1, gap of 5, 1; 5. Use the same mix, 97 mushroom stem, 51 white concrete, 21 smooth sandstone. On layers 21–22 carry door C on up: leave the 3 gaps at (8,15), (9,16) and (10,17), with dark oak logs at (8,14) and (11,18) on layers 21–23, and lay the lintel on layer 23 with dark oak planks at (8,15), (9,16) and (10,17), plus (8,16), (9,17) and (10,18) in the outside corners so that the lintel is one line. Leave 4 window gaps, 1 block wide and 2 high on layers 21–22, at (11,8) and (15,8) at the back, at (18,15) on the right and at (14,18) at the front, with a dark oak plank above each on layer 23. Layers 24 to 27 are a plain ring all the way round. Count: 6 dark oak logs and 10 dark oak planks in the frames. Check: the stem rises 27 layers in all and finishes level, with no gaps above layer 23.",[]],
["Roof door C","Give door C, on the front left at layers 20–22, a small hood and lanterns like door B. Place 3 dark oak stairs on layer 23, one block out from the lintel, at (7,14), (7,15) and (11,19), with the high side against the wall, and hang a lantern under the two end stairs, on layer 22 at (7,14) and (11,19): 2 lanterns. There is no slab on top this time, because the glowing gills sit just above. Check: door C has a tiny dark hood with a lantern at each end, level with the landing of the stair.",[]],
["Glaze the top windows","Glaze the 4 windows of the top storey. Put 2 glass panes in each gap, on layers 21 and 22, 8 panes: at (11,8), (15,8), (18,15) and (14,18). Lay a dark oak plank sill on layer 20 just outside each window, at (11,7), (15,7), (19,15) and (14,19): 4 planks. Put a flower pot on each sill, on layer 21, in the same 4 places: 4 pots. Check: 4 windows in the top storey, two at the back, one on the right and one at the front, each with a pot on a plank sill.",[]],
["Close the stem with a ceiling","Close the top of the stem on layer 28 with a circle 11 across, rows from the back 5, 7, 9, then five rows of 11, then 9, 7, 5, 97 blocks, on top of the wall. Fill it with dark oak planks, but make 4 of the blocks shroomlight, 2 blocks from the centre block (13,13): at (11,13), (15,13), (13,11) and (13,15). That is 93 planks and 4 shroomlight. Those four glowing blocks light the top room from above. Check: a round dark ceiling with a diamond of 4 lights, closing the stem like a lid.",[]],
["Furnish the ground room","Furnish the ground room, between the floor on layer 1 and the second floor on layer 9. In the centre put a table: a dark oak plank at (13,13) on layer 2 with a lantern on top of it, on layer 3, and a dark oak stair on each of its 4 sides, at (13,12), (12,13), (14,13) and (13,14), each with its high side toward the plank: 4 stairs and 1 plank. Round it lay a red carpet rug on layer 2 on every free block of the 5-across circle: 16 carpets. Line the back half of the room with bookshelves against the wall, from about half past nine round through twelve to half past two on the clock face, skipping any block close to a window or doorway: on layer 2 at (11,9), (12,9), (13,9), (14,9), (15,9), (10,10), (11,10), (15,10), (16,10), (9,11), (10,11), (16,11), (17,11), and on layer 3 at (16,10), (16,11), (17,11), 16 bookshelves. Stand 2 barrels on layer 2 at (16,14) and (10,15), and a flower pot on top of the left one, on layer 3. Finally hang a chandelier over the table: a chain of 3 on layers 6–8, hung from the floor above, at (13,13), with a lantern on its lower end on layer 5. Count: 3 chains, 2 lanterns, 2 barrels. Check: a cosy round reading room with a red rug, bookshelves in the back, and a lantern table.",[]],
["Furnish the middle room","Furnish the middle room, between the second floor on layer 9 and the third floor on layer 19. In the middle make a low table: a 3 by 3 square of dark oak slabs on layer 10, columns 12–14 and rows 12–14, 9 slabs. Put a barrel as a stool on each of the 4 sides, at (13,11), (11,13), (15,13) and (13,15), 4 barrels, with a lantern on top of each, on layer 11. Cover the floor with moss carpet on every free block of the 7-across circle, on layer 10: 24 carpets. Stack the bookshelves against the back wall, from about half past nine round through twelve to half past two, skipping any block near a window or doorway: 13 on layer 10, 13 on layer 11, 7 on layer 12 and 4 on layer 13, 37 in all, at the same blocks as the ground room on layers 10 and 11, then (11,9), (10,10), (11,10), (9,11), (10,11), (16,11), (17,11) on layer 12 and (9,11), (10,11), (16,11), (17,11) on layer 13. Finally hang 5 chandeliers from the ceiling: one in the middle, at (13,13), with a chain of 4 on layers 15–18 and a lantern on layer 14, and four smaller ones at (11,11), (15,11), (11,15) and (15,15), each with a chain of 2 on layers 17–18 and a lantern on layer 16. Count: 12 chains and 9 lanterns in all, 4 on the stools and 5 on the chandeliers.",[]],
["Furnish the top room","Furnish the top room, the bedroom, between the floor on layer 19 and the ceiling on layer 28. Make the bed at the back: dark oak planks on layer 20 in a block 2 wide and 3 long, columns 12–13 and rows 10–12, 6 planks, with 2 birch slabs as pillows on layer 21 at (12,10) and (13,10), and a red carpet blanket on top at (12,11), (13,11), (12,12) and (13,12) on layer 21: 4 carpets. Lay moss carpet on the free blocks of the 5-across circle on layer 20: 17 carpets. Put 2 barrels as a bedside shelf at (16,14) and (16,15) on layer 20 with a flower pot on the second, at (16,15) on layer 21. Place the bookshelves on the left side of the room: at (9,11), (10,11) and (9,12) on layers 20 and 21, 6 bookshelves. Hang a lantern in the middle of the room, at (13,13), on layer 23, from a chain of 4 on layers 24–27. Check: a small bedroom with a red bed, a green rug and a lamp hanging from the ceiling, with a clear path from door C to the bed.",[]],
["Lay the glowing gills","Under the cap of a mushroom you see the gills, so make a ring of them on layer 24, right outside the stem. The ring is a circle 23 across with a hole 11 across in the middle, where the stem rises. Counting its outer shape, the circle has rows from the back of 7, 11, 15, 17, 19, 19, 21, 21, then seven rows of 23, then 21, 21, 19, 19, 17, 15, 11, 7, and the hole has rows of 5, 7, 9, then five rows of 11, then 9, 7, 5. That is 324 blocks. Fill it with mushroom stem, but make 8 wedges of shroomlight, each about 15 degrees wide, one starting at each of the 8 compass points (the 4 sides and the 4 diagonals) and about 3 blocks wide at the outer edge, so the gills glow like a ring of lamps: 128 shroomlight and 196 mushroom stem. Check: a pale disc 6 blocks wide all round the stem, with 8 glowing wedges, and the stem passing through the middle.",[]],
["Hang the fairy lights","Hang 20 lanterns under the gills, like fairy lights. Each hangs from a chain: put a chain on layer 23 directly under a gill block, and a lantern on layer 22 under the chain. There are 12 round the outside, 30 degrees apart, and 8 on an inner ring, 45 degrees apart. Place them at (14,3), (9,4), (19,5), (10,6), (16,6), (5,7), (22,9), (6,10), (20,10), (3,12), (23,14), (6,16), (20,16), (4,17), (21,19), (10,20), (16,20), (7,21), (17,22) and (12,23). That is 20 chains and 20 lanterns. Check: from the ground you see a ring of warm lights hanging under the cap, the way fairy lights hang from a tree.",[]],
["Start the red cap: the rim","Now for the great red cap. It is built layer by layer as a hollow dome, a ring 2 blocks thick, and each layer is narrower than the one below. The first three layers, 25 to 27, are the widest: each is a ring 23 across on the outside, with rows from the back of 7, 11, 15, 17, 19, 19, 21, 21, then seven rows of 23, then 21, 21, 19, 19, 17, 15, 11, 7, and a hole 19 across, with rows of 7, 11, 13, 15, 17, 17, then seven rows of 19, then 17, 17, 15, 13, 11, 7. That is 128 blocks per layer, 384 in all. The ring sits on the gill disc, covering its outer 2 blocks. Make most of it red concrete and scatter white concrete spots over the outside, about 1 block in 12 on the outer face and on any block you can see from above: 368 red concrete and 16 white concrete in all. Check: a thick red rim like the brim of a hat, 3 layers high, with the gills showing under it.",[]],
["Shape the lower cap","Raise the lower part of the cap, layers 28 to 30. Each layer is a ring 21 across on the outside, rows from the back of 7, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 19, then seven rows of 21, then 19, 19, 17, 15, 13, 11, 7, with a hole 17 across, rows of 5, 9, 13, 13, 15, 15, then five rows of 17, then 15, 15, 13, 13, 9, 5: 124 blocks per layer. The ring sits 1 block in from the one below, so the dome starts to curve in. On layer 30 leave 2 gaps for the side dormers: at column 8 and at column 18, 3 blocks deep each, in rows 20, 21 and 22, 6 blocks left out, so that layer has 118. Make most of it red concrete and scatter white concrete spots over the outside, about 1 block in 12 on the outer face and on any block you can see from above. Total: 366 blocks, 347 red and 19 white. Check: the dome narrows steadily, and 2 small gaps show on the front left and front right of layer 30.",[]],
["Shape the middle cap","Raise the middle of the cap, layers 31 to 34. Layers 31 and 32 are a ring 19 across, rows from the back of 7, 11, 13, 15, 17, 17, then seven rows of 19, then 17, 17, 15, 13, 11, 7, with a hole 15 across, rows of 5, 9, 11, 13, 13, then five rows of 15, then 13, 13, 11, 9, 5: 116 blocks per layer. Layers 33 and 34 are a ring 17 across, rows of 5, 9, 13, 13, 15, 15, then five rows of 17, then 15, 15, 13, 13, 9, 5, with a hole 13 across, rows of 5, 9, 11, 11, then five rows of 13, then 11, 11, 9, 5: 88 blocks per layer. The dormer gaps carry on up through layer 31: leave rows 19, 20 and 21 of columns 8 and 18 empty there, so layer 31 has 110 blocks. Make most of it red concrete and scatter white concrete spots over the outside, about 1 block in 12 on the outer face and on any block you can see from above. Total: 402 blocks, 379 red and 23 white. Check: the cap is now clearly domed, with the dormer gaps 2 layers tall on both front corners.",[]],
["Shape the upper cap","Raise the shoulders of the cap, layers 35 to 38, each a smaller ring. Layer 35 is a ring 15 across, rows of 5, 9, 11, 13, 13, then five rows of 15, then 13, 13, 11, 9, 5, with a hole 11 across (80 blocks); layer 36 is 13 across, rows of 5, 9, 11, 11, then five rows of 13, then 11, 11, 9, 5, with a hole 9 across (68 blocks); layer 37 is 11 across, rows of 5, 7, 9, then five rows of 11, then 9, 7, 5, with a hole 7 across (60 blocks); and layer 38 is 9 across, rows of 5, 7, then five rows of 9, then 7, 5, with a hole 5 across (48 blocks). Leave a gap for the top dormer in the middle of the front, at column 13: on layer 35 leave rows 19 and 20 empty, and on layer 36 rows 18 and 19, so those layers have 78 and 66 blocks. Make most of it red concrete and scatter white concrete spots over the outside, about 1 block in 12 on the outer face and on any block you can see from above. Total: 252 blocks, 236 red and 16 white. Check: the top of the cap curves in quickly, like the shoulders of a real mushroom.",[]],
["Close the top of the cap","Close the cap with solid discs, no hole, on layers 39 to 41. Layer 39 is a disc 7 across, rows from the back of 3, 5, then three rows of 7, then 5, 3, 37 blocks. Layer 40 is a disc 5 across, rows of 3, then three rows of 5, then 3, 21 blocks. Layer 41 is a 3 by 3 square, 9 blocks. That is 67 blocks, mostly red concrete with 6 white spots. Scatter the white spots at random, with no two touching. Check: a smooth rounded red dome whose top reaches layer 41.",[]],
["Build the left dormer","Build the left dormer, the little window in the front left of the cap, in the gap you left at column 8. On layer 30 put a glass pane in the outermost block of the gap, at (8,22), and on layer 31 another at (8,21), 2 panes. Behind the lower pane, in the innermost block of the gap on layer 30, set a lantern at (8,20), so the window glows. Now the little balcony: on layer 29, just outside the cap, lay dark oak planks at (6,23), (7,23), (8,23), (9,23), (6,24), (7,24), (8,24), (9,24), (10,24), 9 planks. Fence its edge on layer 30 at (6,23), (6,24), (7,24), (8,24), (9,24), (10,24), 6 fences. Finally roof the window on layer 32, where the cap curves: place dark oak stairs at (7,21), (8,22), (9,22), with their high sides toward the cap, and dark oak slabs at (7,22), (8,23), (9,23): 3 stairs and 3 slabs. Check: a round-topped window with a glowing lantern behind it, a railed balcony under it and a small brown roof over it.",[]],
["Build the right dormer","Build the right dormer, the little window in the front right of the cap, in the gap you left at column 18. On layer 30 put a glass pane in the outermost block of the gap, at (18,22), and on layer 31 another at (18,21), 2 panes. Behind the lower pane, in the innermost block of the gap on layer 30, set a lantern at (18,20), so the window glows. Now the little balcony: on layer 29, just outside the cap, lay dark oak planks at (17,23), (18,23), (19,23), (20,23), (16,24), (17,24), (18,24), (19,24), (20,24), 9 planks. Fence its edge on layer 30 at (20,23), (16,24), (17,24), (18,24), (19,24), (20,24), 6 fences. Finally roof the window on layer 32, where the cap curves: place dark oak stairs at (19,21), (17,22), (18,22), with their high sides toward the cap, and dark oak slabs at (19,22), (17,23), (18,23): 3 stairs and 3 slabs. Check: a round-topped window with a glowing lantern behind it, a railed balcony under it and a small brown roof over it.",[]],
["Build the top dormer","Build the small dormer in the middle of the front of the cap, in the gap you left at column 13 on layers 35 and 36. Put a glass pane in the outermost block of each gap row: at (13,20) on layer 35 and at (13,19) on layer 36, 2 panes. Lay a little ledge under the window on layer 34 with 3 dark oak planks at (12,22), (13,22) and (14,22), 3 planks. Roof it on layer 37 with dark oak stairs at (12,19), (13,19) and (14,19), their high sides toward the cap, and a dark oak slab on the next row out, at (12,20), (13,20) and (14,20): 3 stairs and 3 slabs. There is no balcony or lantern on this one, because it is too small. Check: a tiny window high in the middle of the cap, with a brown roof, above the front balcony of the tower.",[]],
["Add the mushroom chimney","On the right of the top of the cap, where it curves in, add a small chimney in the shape of a toadstool. It stands on the shoulder of the cap at (16,11), 2 blocks back and 3 to the right of the centre block. Stack 3 mushroom stems for the stalk, on layers 39–41: 3 stems. On layer 42 make the little cap, a plus shape of 5 brown mushroom blocks: one on the stalk and one on each of its 4 sides, at (16,11), (15,11), (17,11), (16,10) and (16,12). Put a sixth brown mushroom block on top, on layer 43, at (16,11). That is 6 brown mushroom blocks. Check: a small brown toadstool sitting on the red cap like a chimney pot, rising above the top of the dome.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Plant the bushes in a loose ring round the tower, between 7 and 11 blocks from the centre block, about 1 block in 8 of that band, so the garden looks planted rather than laid out. Keep the front clear: leave the strip in front of the tower, 5 blocks wide, and the pond and path free, and keep the 3 by 3 patch around (4,7) at the back left free for the tree. Use 24 oak leaves and 22 flowering azalea leaves, 46 blocks in all, mixed at random. Most are 1 block high, on layer 2; stack a second leaf block on about 2 bushes in 5, on layer 3, 14 blocks on layer 3 and 32 on layer 2. Check: dozens of round little bushes, some pink with azalea flowers, never in a straight line.",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","Scatter the flowers and grass in the same band as the bushes, on layer 2, on free ground only: 24 short grass, 11 alliums, 8 oxeye daisies, 7 dandelions, 5 pink tulips and 3 poppies, 58 blocks in all. Keep them off the path, the pond and the bushes, and away from the front strip and the tree patch, and mix the kinds at random. Check: spots of purple, white, yellow, pink and red in the grass between the bushes, with grass tufts filling the gaps.",[]],
["Plant the toadstools","Add the toadstools, which make the garden look fairy-like. Make 3 toadstools and 2 tiny mushrooms. On the right, at (21,16), build a tall one: a stem of 2 mushroom stems on layers 2–3, and a red mushroom block plus shape for its cap on layer 4, one block on the stem and one on each side, at (21,16), (21,15), (21,17), (20,16) and (22,16). At the back right, at (18,4), build a short one: 1 mushroom stem on layer 2 and a brown mushroom block plus shape on layer 3, at (18,4), (17,4), (19,4), (18,3) and (18,5). On the left, at (3,12), build another short one: 1 stem on layer 2 and a brown plus on layer 3 at (3,12), (2,12), (4,12), (3,11) and (3,13). Finally plant 2 small red mushrooms on layer 2, at (3,10) and (10,24). Count: 4 stems, 5 red and 10 brown mushroom blocks and 2 red mushrooms. Check: one tall red toadstool and two short brown ones standing round the garden.",[]],
["Plant the fairy tree","Add a little tree at the back left to frame the tower. Raise a trunk of 6 oak logs on layers 2–7 at (4,7). The crown is a rounded blob of leaves: on layers 6 and 7 a 5 by 5 square without its 4 corners (21 blocks per layer, the trunk takes the middle one), on layer 8 a 3 by 3 square (9 blocks) and on layer 9 one block on top. Use oak leaves, with about 1 block in 4 as flowering azalea, 36 oak leaves and 14 azalea leaves. Now hang 5 lanterns under the crown, each on a chain: put a chain on layer 5 and a lantern on layer 4 under the leaves, at (3,5), (5,5), (6,6), (2,8) and (6,8). That is 5 chains and 5 lanterns. Check: a round little tree with pink blossoms and 5 lanterns glowing under it.",[]],
["Light the path","Finish with 2 lamp posts beside the path, in front of the tower. Each is a post of 3 dark oak fences on layers 2–4 with a lantern on top, on layer 5: one at (11,22), on the left of the path, and one at (16,23), on the right. That is 6 fences and 2 lanterns. Check: the two posts stand where the path starts to curve, the same height as the awning, and from the pond you can see the whole fairy tower lit up behind them.",[]]]},
{id:"dairy-barn-pasture",name:"Dairy Barn & Pasture",emoji:"🐄",tags:["Farm", "Nature", "Cozy"],diff:"Hard",time:"4 hr",size:"31×27",photos:[P18],pr:[1.25],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A cream-plastered dairy barn with a dark timber frame and a big arched spruce roof, built to house cows. Inside are six fenced stalls with hay and feed barrels, a lantern-lit aisle and a loft stacked with hay. Outside, a coarse-dirt farmyard with milk churns and a hay stack, a fenced pasture with a water trough and a hay pile, three oaks and a weathervane on the ridge.",steps:[["Lay the field","Everything stands on a flat green field, so start there. Mark out a rectangle 31 blocks wide and 27 deep. Columns are counted from the left and rows from the back, as you stand at the front, so it covers columns 1–31 and rows 1–27. All of this is on layer 1, the ground layer. Fill it with grass blocks, but leave three areas empty for later: the barn's footprint (columns 3–17, rows 3–15, a 15 by 13 rectangle), the farmyard in front of it (columns 6–14, rows 16–19, 9 by 4) and the lane that runs away from the yard (columns 9–11, rows 20–27, 3 wide and 8 long). That is 582 grass blocks.",[]],
["Pack the yard and lane","Fill the two empty patches in front of the barn with coarse dirt, still on layer 1. The yard is columns 6–14, rows 16–19, a 9 by 4 rectangle of 36 blocks, and the lane is columns 9–11, rows 20–27, 3 wide and 8 long, 24 blocks, running right to the front edge of the field. That makes 60 coarse dirt in all. Coarse dirt is a full block, so it sits level with the grass and gives the cows a worn track to walk on.",[]],
["Lay the barn floor","Fill the 15 by 13 hole you left for the barn, columns 3–17, rows 3–15, on layer 1. First lay the outer ring, 1 block thick, where the walls will stand: cobblestone with a few mossy cobblestones mixed in at random, 36 cobblestone and 11 mossy cobblestone, 47 blocks. The front row (row 15) has a gap of 5 in the middle, columns 8–12, which is the doorway. Next lay the aisle, a strip of spruce planks 5 wide down the middle of the barn: columns 8–12, rows 4–14, 55 blocks. Add 5 more spruce planks in the doorway itself, row 15, columns 8–12, as a sill. That is 60 spruce planks. Last, the stall floors: coarse dirt in two rectangles 4 wide and 11 long, columns 4–7 and columns 13–16, both rows 4–14, 88 blocks. The whole floor is 195 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the timber posts","Now put up the dark oak frame that makes the barn look like a timber-framed barn. Build 12 posts, each 6 blocks tall (layers 2–7), from dark oak logs, 72 logs in all. Stand them on the cobblestone ring at the four corners ((3,3), (17,3), (3,15), (17,15)), beside the doorway and the back wall ((7,3), (13,3), (7,15), (13,15)), and halfway along each long side wall ((3,7), (3,11), (17,7), (17,11)). Between the posts are bays 3 blocks wide, and that is where the walls will go.",[]],
["Fill the plaster walls","Fill in the walls between the posts. On layers 2–6 (5 layers) use smooth sandstone, which looks like cream plaster, 153 blocks. On layer 7, on top of every wall between the posts, lay a beam of dark oak planks, 40 blocks, so the frame has a dark band all the way round. Leave these gaps in the plaster: the doorway in the front wall (row 15, columns 8–12, layers 2–6, empty so far); the back door (row 3, column 10, layers 2–3); and ten window holes 2 blocks tall on layers 4–5, at rows 5, 9 and 13 of columns 3 and 17 on the long walls, and at columns 5 and 15 on the front wall (row 15) and the back wall (row 3).",[]],
["Step the doorway into an arch","The front doorway is 5 wide and 5 tall, which is a little square, so round off its top corners to make a stepped arch. On layer 6, in the front wall (row 15), put a dark oak plank at column 8 and another at column 12, 2 planks. The doorway is now 5 blocks wide up to layer 5 and only 3 wide (columns 9–11) on layer 6, just under the beam.",[]],
["Glaze the windows, add planters","Fill the ten window holes with glass panes, 2 high on layers 4–5, 20 panes. Then give each window a planter, one block outside the wall: a spruce plank on layer 3 directly under the window, and a flower pot on top of it on layer 4, 10 planks and 10 pots. On the long walls the planters are in column 2 (left wall) and column 18 (right wall), at rows 5, 9 and 13; on the front wall they are in row 16, and on the back wall in row 2, both at columns 5 and 15.",[]],
["Lay the hay loft floor","Build the hay loft's floor over each row of stalls, with spruce planks on layer 7, level with the beam. It is two rectangles 4 wide and 11 long, columns 4–7, rows 4–14 and columns 13–16, rows 4–14, 88 planks in all. Leave the aisle between them (columns 8–12) open all the way up to the roof, like the open arch in the reference.",[]],
["Close the gable ends","Fill the two end walls above the beam, the front (row 15) and the back (row 3), so the roof has something to sit on. Start on layer 8, just above the beam, and fill with smooth sandstone a stepped triangle that is this many blocks tall for columns 3 to 17 in order: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 5, 4, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0. Do the same on both rows. Then swap in dark oak for the framing: the posts at columns 7 and 13 carry on up through the gable as dark oak logs (4 high, layers 8–11); and make the hay-loft door, a hole 3 wide and 2 high at columns 9–11, layers 9–10, with a dark oak plank sill under it (layer 8) and lintel over it (layer 11), and dark oak logs at columns 8 and 12 on layers 9–10 as its sides. That is 40 sandstone, 24 logs and 12 planks.",[]],
["Raise the steep lower roof","Now the big spruce roof, which is the barn's arch. Every slice of the roof is the same cross-section across the barn, and you repeat it along rows 2–16, 15 rows in all, 1 more than the walls at each end to give the roof an overhang. Build the left side from the outside in: a spruce stair at column 2 on layer 8; at column 3 a spruce plank on layer 8 and a stair on layer 9; at column 4 a plank on layer 9 and a stair on layer 10; at column 5 a plank on layer 10 and a stair on layer 11; at column 6 a plank on layer 11 and a stair on layer 12. Every stair faces right, toward the middle of the barn, so its high side is on the inside. The right side is the mirror image, in columns 18, 17, 16, 15 and 14, with the stairs facing left. That is 18 blocks per row, 270 in all: 150 stairs and 120 planks. This part is steep, like the sides of a barn roof.",[]],
["Round off the arch","Finish the top of the roof, which is gentler and flatter, so the whole roof looks curved. Again repeat one slice along rows 2–16. On the left side: column 7 has a spruce plank on layer 12 and a spruce slab on layer 13 (bottom half); column 8 has a plank on layer 12 and a stair on layer 13, facing right; column 9 has a plank on layer 13 and a slab on layer 14. In the middle, column 10, put a single slab on layer 14. The right side mirrors the left, in columns 13, 12 and 11, with the stair at column 12 facing left. That is 13 blocks per row, 195 in all: 90 planks, 75 slabs and 30 stairs.",[]],
["Fence the stalls","Divide the stalls with dark oak fences, all on layer 2. Run the stall fronts along the aisle: columns 7 and 13, rows 4–14, with an oak fence gate at rows 5, 9 and 13 in each, and fence everywhere else (face each gate across the aisle). Then add the dividers between stalls: fences at rows 7 and 11, columns 4–6 on the left and columns 14–16 on the right. You now have 6 stalls, each 3 by 3. That is 28 fences and 6 gates.",[]],
["Furnish the stalls","Put a hay bale at the back of each stall, on layer 2 against the wall: column 4 on the left and column 16 on the right, at rows 5, 9 and 13, 6 bales. Put a barrel for feed in the corner of each stall: column 4 and column 16, at rows 4, 8 and 12, 6 barrels. Finish with 4 lanterns standing on top of the fence posts where the dividers meet the stall fronts, on layer 3, at columns 7 and 13, rows 7 and 11.",[]],
["Stack the loft with hay","Fill the loft with hay. On the left, layer 8: hay bales over columns 4–6, rows 4–8, 15 bales; then on layer 9, columns 5–6, rows 4–7, 8 more. On the right, layer 8: columns 14–16, rows 10–14, 15 bales; and layer 9, columns 14–15, rows 11–14, 8 more. That is 46 bales. The pile is stepped so it slides in under the sloping roof.",[]],
["Fence the pasture","Build the fenced pasture to the right of the barn, all on layer 2. Run oak fence round the edge of the rectangle columns 21–30, rows 3–25: that is rows 3 and 25 and columns 21 and 30, 10 wide and 23 long. Make a double gate on the left side, at column 21, rows 18 and 19, using oak fence gates facing left and right. That is 60 fences and 2 gates.",[]],
["Add a trough and a hay pile","Make the water trough inside the pasture: a ring of spruce planks on layer 2, columns 23–27, rows 8–10, 12 planks, with water filling the 3 blocks in the middle, row 9, columns 24–26. Then make the hay pile near the gate: hay bales on layer 2 over columns 26–28, rows 19–20 (6 bales), and a second layer on layer 3 over columns 27–28, rows 19–20 (4 bales). That is 10 bales in all.",[]],
["Plant three oak trees","Plant three oak trees: one in the pasture at (24,15), one at (28,5), and one in the front-left corner at (3,22). Each tree is the same: a trunk of 5 oak logs on layers 2–6; then leaves. On layers 4 and 5, put leaves in the 5 by 5 square around the trunk without its 4 corners, 20 leaves each; on layer 6, a 3 by 3 square around the trunk, 8 leaves; and on layer 7, a plus shape of 5 leaves on top. That is 15 logs and 159 leaves for the three.",[]],
["Dress the farmyard","Add the clutter of a working farmyard in front of the barn. A hay stack at columns 3–4, rows 17–18, 2 bales high (layers 2–3), 8 bales. Three barrels as milk churns on layer 2 at (16,17), (17,17) and (16,18). A little table of 2 spruce planks at (16,19) and (17,19) on layer 2, with a flower pot on top of each on layer 3.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns and weathervane","Light the yard with 4 lamp posts at (5,18), (15,18), (8,23) and (12,23): each is 3 dark oak fences on layers 2–4 with a lantern on top, layer 5. Then add a wall bracket by each side of the doorway, at columns 7 and 13 in row 16 (in front of the wall): a dark oak fence on layer 4 with a lantern on top, layer 5. Finally, put a weathervane on the middle of the roof at (10,9): a column of 3 oak fences on layers 15–17, with 4 more oak fences sticking out to each side on layer 16, at (9,9), (11,9), (10,8) and (10,10). That is 14 dark oak fences, 7 oak fences and 6 lanterns.",[]],
["Scatter the grass and flowers","Finish the farm by making the field look lived in. On layer 2, put short grass, poppies, dandelions and oxeye daisies on the grass blocks that are still bare, about 1 block in 7, anywhere except the paths, the fences and the barn's doors. That is 36 short grass, 11 poppies, 12 dandelions and 10 daisies, 69 plants; the exact places do not matter.",[]]]},
{id:"sheep-stable-yard",name:"Sheep Stable & Shearing Yard",emoji:"🐑",tags:["Farm", "Nature", "Cozy"],diff:"Hard",time:"4 hr",size:"33×26",photos:[P19],pr:[1.273],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A long open-front stable for a flock of sheep: stone-brick and plaster walls, dark oak posts, a spruce roof with grey stone eaves and a little glass cupola. Under the roof are four hay-lined pens, a stone walkway and a wool bay with a stepped pile of white wool and a shearing table. In front, a big fenced pasture with two water troughs, a hay rack, a shearing platform and three oaks.",steps:[["Lay the field","Start with a flat grass field 33 blocks wide and 26 deep. Columns are counted from the left and rows from the back, as you stand at the front, so it covers columns 1–33 and rows 1–26, all on layer 1. Fill it with grass blocks, but leave two areas empty: the stable's footprint (columns 4–28, rows 3–13, 25 wide and 11 deep) and a short lane in front of it (columns 13–19, rows 14–15, 7 wide and 2 deep). That is 569 grass blocks.",[]],
["Lay the stable floor","Fill the stable's footprint, columns 4–28, rows 3–13, on layer 1. First the stone-brick edge, which is the base of the back wall and both end walls: stone bricks along row 3 (columns 4–28) and down columns 4 and 28 (rows 4–13), 45 blocks. Then the pen floors: coarse dirt over columns 5–27, rows 4–9, 23 wide and 6 deep. The front 4 rows, columns 5–27, rows 10–13, are the walkway: cobblestone with about one block in four mossy cobblestone, 65 cobblestone and 27 mossy. Finally fill the lane in front, columns 13–19, rows 14–15, with 14 coarse dirt. That is 152 coarse dirt in total.",[]],
["Raise the timber posts","Put up the stable's frame: dark oak log posts 5 tall (layers 2–6). Stand 7 along the back wall, in row 3, and 7 along the open front, in row 13, at columns 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24 and 28 (the front ones line up with the back ones). Add 1 more halfway along each end wall, at (4,8) and (28,8). That is 16 posts, 80 logs. Notice there is a post at column 16, the middle of the front; the two entrances will be either side of it.",[]],
["Wall in the back and ends","Build the three closed walls between the posts: the back wall (row 3) and the two end walls (columns 4 and 28). Each is 2 layers of stone bricks (layers 2–3) with 2 layers of smooth sandstone on top (layers 4–5): 68 stone bricks and 56 sandstone. Leave a window hole 2 tall (layers 4–5) in the back wall at columns 6, 10, 14, 18, 22 and 26. Then run a beam of dark oak planks on layer 6 along all four sides, between the posts, including the open front (row 13): 52 planks.",[]],
["Rail the open front","The front wall (row 13) is open between the posts, like the stables in your reference. Put a low rail of oak fence on layer 2 across 4 of the 6 bays, 3 blocks each: columns 5–7, 9–11, 21–23 and 25–27, 12 fences. Leave the two bays either side of the middle post, columns 13–15 and 17–19, open as entrances for the sheep.",[]],
["Close the gable ends","Fill the two ends above the beam so the roof has a wall to sit against. On column 4 (left end) and again on column 28 (right end), working along rows 3–13, build a stepped triangle starting on layer 7, this many blocks tall in order: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0. Use smooth sandstone, except the tallest column, row 8, which is dark oak logs (5 high). That is 40 sandstone and 10 logs.",[]],
["Raise the lower roof","Now the long spruce roof. Every slice of the roof is the same cross-section from back to front, and you repeat it along columns 3–29, 27 slices (one more than the walls at each end, for an overhang). The back half of the slice, from the outside in: a stone brick stair at row 2 on layer 7; at row 3 a spruce plank on layer 7 and a spruce stair on layer 8; at row 4 a plank on layer 8 and a stair on layer 9; at row 5 a plank on layer 9 and a stair on layer 10. Every stair faces the front half of the roof, so the high side is on the inside; the front half mirrors it, at rows 14, 13, 12 and 11, with the stairs facing the back. That is 14 blocks a slice, 378 in all: 54 stone brick stairs for the grey eave, 162 spruce stairs and 162 spruce planks.",[]],
["Close the ridge","Finish the roof with the upper slopes and ridge, again repeating one slice along columns 3–29. On the back half: at row 6 a spruce plank on layer 10 and a stair on layer 11; at row 7 a plank on layer 11 and a stair on layer 12. In the middle, row 8, put a single spruce plank on layer 12, the ridge. The front half mirrors it at rows 10 and 9, with the stairs facing the back. That is 9 blocks a slice, 243 in all: 135 planks and 108 stairs.",[]],
["Top it with a cupola","A cupola lets the hay breathe and gives the long roof a landmark. It stands on the flat top of the ridge, on columns 15–17, rows 7–9. On layers 13–14, build a dark oak log at each of its 4 corners, (15,7), (17,7), (15,9) and (17,9), 2 high, 8 logs; and put glass panes, also 2 high, in the middle of each side, at (16,7), (16,9), (15,8) and (17,8), 8 panes. Roof it on layer 15 with a 3 by 3 square of spruce planks, 9 planks, and finish with a single spruce slab on top at (16,8), layer 16.",[]],
["Fence the sheep pens","Divide the back of the stable into 4 pens, all in dark oak fence on layer 2. Run 3 dividers down columns 9, 14 and 19, rows 4–8, column 9, rows 4–8 and so on, 15 fences. Run a front rail along row 9 from column 5 to column 23 with a gate in each pen, oak fence gates at columns 6, 11, 16 and 21 (facing front and back), and fence everywhere else, 15 fences. Close off the end of the pens with a partition on column 24, rows 4–9, 6 fences. That is 36 fences and 4 gates.",[]],
["Furnish the pens","Give each pen hay and feed. On layer 2 against the back wall (row 4), put 2 hay bales in each pen, at columns 6–7, 11–12, 16–17 and 21–22, 8 bales; and a barrel at the end of each, at columns 8, 13, 18 and 23, 4 barrels. Light the pens with 4 lanterns standing on the fence posts at the top of each divider, on layer 3: at (9,9), (14,9), (19,9) and (24,9).",[]],
["Build the wool bay","The last bay, at the right end, is for shorn wool. Heap up white wool against the back wall like a stepped pile: on layer 2, columns 25–27, rows 4–6 (9 blocks); on layer 3, columns 26–27, rows 4–6 (6 blocks); on layer 4, column 27, rows 4–6 (3 blocks), 18 wool in all. Make a shearing table in front of it, 3 spruce planks on layer 2 at columns 25–27, row 8, with a lantern on each end of it, on layer 3 at (25,8) and (27,8); and put 2 barrels beside it at (26,9) and (27,9).",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Fill the six back-wall window holes with glass panes, 2 high on layers 4–5, at columns 6, 10, 14, 18, 22 and 26, 12 panes. Outside each window, in row 2, put a planter: a spruce plank on layer 3 and a flower pot on top on layer 4, 6 planks and 6 pots.",[]],
["Fence the pasture","Build the big pasture in front, with oak fence on layer 2 round the edge of the rectangle columns 3–31, rows 16–25: rows 16 and 25 and columns 3 and 31, 29 wide and 10 deep. Make the gates in the back fence (row 16) at columns 14 and 18, oak fence gates, in line with the stable's entrances (the lane in front of the stable runs up to them). That is 72 fences and 2 gates.",[]],
["Add troughs and a hay rack","Build two water troughs in the pasture, each a ring of spruce planks on layer 2 with water in the middle: columns 8–12, rows 19–21 on the left and columns 22–26, rows 19–21 on the right. Each ring is 5 wide and 3 deep, 12 planks, and the 3 blocks in the middle (row 20, columns 9–11 and 23–25) are water. Then make a hay rack between them: hay bales on layer 2 at columns 15–17, row 23 (3 bales), with 1 more on top at (16,23) on layer 3. That is 24 planks, 6 water and 4 bales.",[]],
["Plant three oak trees","Plant three oak trees: two in the pasture at (6,22) and (28,23), and one beside the stable at (31,9). Each tree has a trunk of 5 oak logs on layers 2–6. On layers 4 and 5, put leaves in the 5 by 5 square around the trunk without its 4 corners, 20 leaves each; on layer 6, a 3 by 3 square around the trunk, 8 leaves; on layer 7, a plus of 5 leaves. That is 15 logs and 159 leaves for the three.",[]],
["Dress the shearing yard","Make the yard in front of the stable's right end look used. A pile of white wool 2 by 2 and 2 high at columns 21–22, rows 14–15, layers 2–3, 8 blocks. A low shearing platform of spruce slabs on layer 2 at columns 24–26, rows 14–15, 6 slabs.",[]],
["Light the entrances","Put a lamp post on each side of the lane at (12,14) and (20,14): 3 dark oak fences, layers 2–4, with a lantern on top at layer 5. Then add a wall lantern on each of the front posts at columns 8 and 24, in row 14 (just in front of the stable): a dark oak fence on layer 4 with a lantern on top on layer 5. That is 8 fences and 4 lanterns.",[]],
["Scatter the grass and flowers","Finish by roughing up the grass. On layer 2, put short grass, poppies, dandelions, oxeye daisies and a few pink tulips on the bare grass blocks, about 1 block in 8, avoiding the paths and fences. That is 31 short grass, 14 poppies, 10 dandelions, 6 daisies and 6 tulips, 67 plants; the exact places do not matter.",[]]]},
{id:"wheat-terrace-farm",name:"Wheat Terraces & Granary",emoji:"🌾",tags:["Farm", "Nature", "Showpiece"],diff:"Hard",time:"4 hr",size:"35×27",photos:[P20],pr:[1.384],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A hillside wheat farm built as three stone-walled terraces joined by a stair, with water channels watering rows of wheat, carrots, pumpkins and melons, and a scarecrow on every level. A timber-framed granary with a spruce roof stands on the top terrace, and a stone well, a farm stall, a hay cart, a fenced field and a few oaks fill the yard.",steps:[["Lay the field","This farm climbs three terraces, so first mark out the site: a field 35 blocks wide and 27 deep. Columns are counted from the left and rows from the back, as you stand at the front, so it covers columns 1–35 and rows 1–27. On layer 1, fill it with grass blocks, but leave three areas empty for the fields: the two upper terraces (columns 3–28, rows 2–16, 26 wide and 15 deep, which you will build up with walls) and the front field (columns 4–27, rows 17–24, 24 wide and 8 deep). That is 363 grass blocks, in an L shape round the fields.",[]],
["Wall the middle terrace","Build the retaining wall of the middle terrace, which is 2 blocks high, from stone bricks with about 1 in 4 mossy stone bricks mixed in. The terrace is columns 3–28, rows 10–16, and its wall runs round the front and both sides, only: along the front, row 16, columns 3–28; and down the two sides, columns 3 and 28, rows 10–15. Make the wall 2 blocks high (layers 1–2) everywhere, except at columns 14–16 of the front row, where it is only 1 high (layer 1), because the stairs will go there. That is 73 blocks, 52 stone bricks and 21 mossy.",[]],
["Wall the top terrace","Do the same for the top terrace, columns 3–28, rows 2–9, but make its wall 3 high (layers 1–3). Along the front, row 9, columns 3–28, build it 3 high, except at columns 14–16, which are only 2 high (layers 1–2) for the stairs. Then run the side walls, 3 high, down columns 3 and 28, rows 2–8. There is no wall at the back, because that is the edge of the world. That is 117 blocks, 92 stone bricks and 25 mossy.",[]],
["Add the stairs","Join the three levels with stone brick stairs, 3 wide in columns 14–16. On layer 2 at row 16, set 3 stairs on top of the low part of the middle terrace's wall, and on layer 3 at row 9, set 3 more on top of the top terrace's wall. Every stair faces the back (the high side toward the back), so you can walk up from the front. That is 6 stairs. The path in front of them climbs from the front field to the middle terrace to the top terrace.",[]],
["Dig the top terrace","Lay out the soil of the top terrace on layer 3, the layer on top of its wall. The terrace's inside is columns 4–27, rows 2–8. Make a path of coarse dirt 3 wide, columns 14–16, rows 2–8, 21 blocks. To the left of it, columns 4–13, rows 2–8, is the yard of the granary you will build later: fill it with coarse dirt, except for the granary's footprint (columns 5–11, rows 3–8), which you leave empty, 28 blocks. To the right, columns 17–27, build the field: water along row 5, 11 blocks, and farmland on the other rows (2–4 and 6–8), 66 blocks. That is 49 coarse dirt, 11 water and 66 farmland.",[]],
["Dig the middle terrace","Lay out the middle terrace on layer 2: the inside is columns 4–27, rows 10–15. Make a coarse dirt path 3 wide, columns 14–16, rows 10–15, 18 blocks, continuing the stairs. Put a water channel along row 13 on either side of the path: columns 4–13 and columns 17–27, 21 blocks of water. Fill all the rest with farmland: rows 10–12 and 14–15, columns 4–13 and 17–27, 105 blocks. Water hydrates farmland up to 4 blocks away, so every row is irrigated.",[]],
["Dig the front field","Lay out the front field on layer 1, in the hole you left: columns 4–27, rows 17–24. Again make a coarse dirt path 3 wide, columns 14–16, rows 17–24, 24 blocks, and a water channel along row 21 on both sides (columns 4–13 and 17–27), 21 water. Fill the rest with farmland, rows 17–20 and 22–24, 147 blocks.",[]],
["Stand the scarecrows","Make 3 scarecrows, one for each terrace, standing on the farmland: top terrace at (22,7), middle at (9,11), front at (8,19). Each is a post of 2 oak fences on top of the soil, then a hay bale for the body on top of those, with an oak fence either side of the hay bale as arms, and a pumpkin for a head on top. For the top terrace, the soil is layer 3, so the fences are on layers 4–5, the hay and arms on layer 6 and the pumpkin on layer 7; the middle one is one layer lower and the front one two lower. That is 12 fences, 3 hay bales and 3 pumpkins.",[]],
["Sow the top terrace","Plant wheat on every bare farmland block of the top terrace, on layer 4, on top of the farmland: columns 17–27, rows 2–4 and 6–8, except where the scarecrow stands, 65 wheat. Leave the water row alone.",[]],
["Sow the middle terrace","On the middle terrace, plant wheat on layer 3 over the farmland of rows 10–12, columns 4–13 and 17–27, except for the scarecrow's block, 62 wheat. Then plant carrots in the same way on rows 14–15, the 2 rows in front of the water, 42 blocks, 42 carrots in all. Leave the paths and water bare.",[]],
["Sow the front field","On the front field, plant on layer 2. Wheat on rows 17–20, columns 4–13 and 17–27, except for the scarecrow's block, and on rows 22–24, columns 4–13: 113 wheat. Then fill rows 22–24 of the right-hand side with a pumpkin patch at columns 17–21 (15 pumpkins) and a melon patch at columns 22–27 (18 melons). Pumpkins and melons are full blocks, so the patches rise like little hills.",[]],
["Floor the granary","The granary, a little timber-framed barn, stands on the top terrace's left side, on columns 5–11, rows 3–8, 7 wide and 6 deep, in the hole you left. On layer 3, lay a floor of spruce planks, 42 planks. Then raise 5 posts of dark oak logs, 4 tall (layers 4–7): at the 4 corners, (5,3), (11,3), (5,8) and (11,8), and one in the middle of the back wall, at (8,3). That is 20 logs.",[]],
["Wall the granary","Between the posts, build the walls of smooth sandstone on layers 4–6, 3 layers, and a beam of dark oak planks on layer 7 along the top. Leave a doorway 3 wide and 2 tall in the front wall (row 8), at columns 7–9, layers 4–5, with plaster above it. Then raise the two gable ends, on rows 3 and 8, with a stepped triangle of sandstone from layer 8, this tall for columns 5 to 11 in order: 0, 1, 2, 3, 2, 1, 0. That is 63 sandstone and 17 planks.",[]],
["Roof the granary","Roof it in spruce, in slices along rows 2–9, 8 slices (1 more than the walls at each end). Each slice, from the left: a spruce stair at column 4 on layer 8; at column 5 a plank on layer 8 and a stair on layer 9; at column 6 a plank on layer 9 and a stair on layer 10; at column 7 a plank on layer 10 and a stair on layer 11. In the middle, column 8, a plank on layer 11. The right half mirrors it, at columns 12, 11, 10 and 9, with the stairs facing left. Every stair on the left half faces right. That is 15 blocks a slice, 120 in all: 64 stairs and 56 planks.",[]],
["Stock the granary","Fill the granary. On layer 4, just inside the door, put hay bales at (7,7), (8,7) and (9,7), and 2 more on top of the outer two on layer 5 (5 bales). Put 3 barrels of grain against the left wall at (6,4), (6,5) and (6,6), on layer 4, and a lantern on the floor at (10,4) on layer 4.",[]],
["Dig the well","At the right of the front field, build a well on columns 31–33, rows 19–21. On layer 2, make a ring of stone bricks, 8 blocks, with a water block in the middle at (32,20). Put a dark oak fence post 2 high on each corner of the ring, at (31,19), (33,19), (31,21) and (33,21), layers 3–4, and roof it with a 3 by 3 square of spruce slabs on layer 5. That is 8 stone bricks, 8 fences and 9 slabs.",[]],
["Set up the market table","By the middle terrace, set up a farm stall. On layer 2 put 3 spruce planks as a table at columns 30–32, row 11, and on top of them on layer 3 a pumpkin at (30,11), a melon at (31,11) and a pumpkin at (32,11). In front of the table, at columns 30–32, row 12 on layer 2, put 3 barrels for the harvest.",[]],
["Park the hay cart","Park a cart by the well. For its bed, put spruce planks on layer 2 at columns 31–33, rows 23–24, 6 planks. Stack hay bales on top on layer 3 at columns 31–32, rows 23–24, 4 bales. The 4 wheels are oak fences on layer 2, in front of and behind the bed, at (31,22), (33,22), (31,25) and (33,25).",[]],
["Fence the front field","Fence the front field with oak fence on layer 2 along its two sides and its front: columns 3 and 28, rows 17–25, and row 25, columns 3–28, 41 fences. Put an oak fence gate in the middle of the front row, at (15,25), facing front and back, so you can walk in. That is 1 gate. The back is open: the middle terrace's wall makes that side.",[]],
["Plant three oak trees","Plant three oak trees along the right edge: at (31,5), (33,8) and (33,15). Each has a trunk of 5 oak logs on layers 2–6. On layers 4 and 5, put leaves in the 5 by 5 square around the trunk without its 4 corners, 20 leaves each; on layer 6, a 3 by 3 square, 8 leaves; on layer 7, a plus of 5 leaves. That is 15 logs and 151 leaves.",[]],
["Light the terraces","Put a lantern post on each side of the stairs, standing on the tops of the walls. On the middle terrace's wall at (13,16) and (17,16): 2 dark oak fences on layers 3–4, a lantern on top on layer 5. On the top terrace's wall at (13,9) and (17,9): fences on layers 4–5, lantern on layer 6. Near the well, at (30,18) and (34,18), put 2 lamp posts of 3 fences on layers 2–4, with a lantern on layer 5. That is 14 fences and 6 lanterns.",[]],
["Scatter the grass and flowers","Finish by dressing the grass. On layer 2, put short grass, poppies, dandelions, oxeye daisies and a few pink tulips on the bare grass blocks, about 1 block in 8, avoiding the fences and the paths. That is 23 short grass, 8 poppies, 3 dandelions, 3 daisies and 5 tulips, 42 plants; the exact places do not matter.",[]]]},
{id:"overgrown-stone-bridge",name:"Overgrown Stone Arch Bridge",emoji:"🌉",tags:["Bridges", "Nature", "Medieval"],diff:"Hard",time:"5 hr",size:"25×15",photos:[P13],pr:[1.196],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A mossy stone bridge with a house on top: a brick-ringed arch over a lily-pad stream, a stone-brick house with log posts, glass slits and a stepped grass roof, hanging vines, lantern posts and grassy hills rising on both banks.",steps:[["Lay the bed","Find flat ground and mark a rectangle 25 blocks wide and 15 deep. The long side is the front, and left and right are as you face it. The stream will run from the back to the front through the middle. Cover the whole rectangle on layer 1 (375 blocks) with 282 coarse dirt, 70 mossy cobblestone and 23 cobblestone. The banks on either side are mostly coarse dirt with a few mossy stones. In the stream strip, 9 blocks wide, from the 9th to the 17th block from the left, use a rockier mix so the stream bed looks stony.",[]],
["Fill the stream","Fill the stream strip with water on layer 2, from the back edge to the front edge. The strip is 9 wide and 15 long, so that is 135 water blocks. The banks on each side stay empty for now. Check that the water reaches every spot, because the arch will stand right over it.",[]],
["Wall the stream edge","Behind where the bridge will stand, the stream runs between rock walls. On each side of the stream, in the back 3 rows, stack mossy cobblestone from layer 2: 9 tall in the very back row, 8 in the next row and 7 in the third. That is 24 blocks on each side, 48 in all: 34 mossy cobblestone and 14 plain cobblestone scattered through it to break up the green. The hills will grow up against these walls.",[]],
["Raise the left hill","Raise the left hill. It is 8 blocks wide and 15 deep, and it sits on the left bank. Build it from coarse dirt with a few mossy cobblestones in it (277 dirt and 48 mossy cobblestone), and put 1 grass block on top of every column (80 grass). Heights below count the blocks stacked from layer 2, grass included. In the middle 9 rows, leave the 4 columns next to the stream empty, because the stone bridge fills that space. The 4 columns from the outer edge form a ramp up to the bridge: 4, 5, 6 and 7 tall, counting in from the outer edge. In the 3 rows at the front, the ground steps down. The row right in front of the ramp is 2, 3, 4, 5 tall, the next row is 1, 1, 2, 3 and the very front row is all 1. The 4 columns beside the stream are only 1 tall in all 3 rows, a flat lawn. In the 3 rows at the back, the hill climbs 1 block per row. The first back row is 5, 6, 7, 8 tall on the ramp columns, the next is 6, 7, 8, 9 and the very back row is 7, 8, 9, 10. The 4 columns beside the stream are 8, 9 and 10 tall in those 3 rows. The stream-edge column of those rows is already the mossy wall, so just add the grass block on top of it. On the middle row of the ramp, leave the top block empty: the dirt path goes there later.",[]],
["Raise the right hill","Raise the right hill as the mirror image of the left one: 8 wide and 15 deep, with the ramp on the outer edge, the 4 lawn columns beside the stream, the climb at the back and the step-down at the front, all the same as before. Use 279 coarse dirt, 46 mossy cobblestone and 80 grass blocks on top, and leave the top block of the ramp's middle row empty for the path. Put the grass on top of the mossy wall too.",[]],
["Build the stone body","Between the two hills, build the stone body of the bridge, from layer 2 to layer 7. It is 17 blocks long, the 5th to the 21st block from the left, and 9 deep, the 4th to the 12th row from the back, so it fills the gap between the hills and crosses the stream. Cut the arch through it from the front to the back: the opening is 9 wide, the width of the stream, and starts above the water on layer 3. The 3 middle columns are 5 tall (layers 3–7), the 2 columns next to them on each side are 4 tall (layers 3–6), and the outer column on each side is 3 tall (layers 3–5). Face everything you can see from outside, the front, the inside of the arch and the back above the stream, with the stone mix (55 stone bricks, 51 mossy stone bricks and 16 cracked stone bricks), and fill everything buried inside with 358 cobblestone. Leave the blocks that touch the arch opening on the front and back faces empty, because the brick ring goes there next. That is 480 blocks.",[]],
["Ring the arch with bricks","Frame each arch mouth with a ring of bricks 1 block thick, on the front and the back. On each face, follow the curve of the opening: 3 bricks up the outside of each side (layers 3–5), 1 brick above each outer column (layer 6), and 2 bricks above the next two columns on each side (layer 7). That is 12 bricks on each face, 24 in all. The 3 bricks above the crown come with the top course in the next step.",[]],
["Lay the top course","On layer 8, cover the whole top of the body, 17 by 9, with the stone mix: 70 stone bricks, 56 mossy stone bricks and 21 cracked stone bricks. In the middle of the front edge and the back edge, the 3 blocks above the crown of the arch are bricks, the last 6 bricks of the ring (6 in all). This course is the floor of the house, and the 1-block ledge around it.",[]],
["Stand the log posts","The house sits on the top course, 1 block in from the edge all round, so a ledge is left outside its walls. It is 15 long and 7 deep. Stand oak log posts 3 tall (layers 9–11) at its 4 corners, and 2 more on the front and 2 on the back: the 5th and the 11th block from the left of the house. That is 8 posts, 24 logs. They split each long wall into 3 bays: 3 blocks, 5 blocks and 3 blocks wide.",[]],
["Wall the house","Build the house walls with the stone mix, 3 tall (layers 9–11), between the posts: 50 blocks in all. Leave window gaps 2 tall, starting on layer 10 so the bottom block stays as a sill. On each long wall, leave a gap 1 wide in the middle of each 3-wide bay, and a gap 3 wide in the middle of the 5-wide bay. On each end wall, leave a gap 1 wide beside each corner post. Keep the middle 3 blocks of each end wall clear as well, because the brick door frame goes there.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Fill every window gap with glass panes: 28 in all. That is 2 panes in each of the 4 narrow gaps on the long walls, 6 in each of the two big windows, and 2 in each of the 4 slits on the end walls (layers 10–11). From outside they look like narrow arrow slits in the old stone.",[]],
["Frame the doors","Frame the door in the middle of each end wall with bricks: a post 2 tall on each side of the door gap (layers 9–10) and a lintel of 3 bricks above (layer 11). That is 7 bricks on each end, 14 in all. The door gap itself is left open, 1 wide and 2 tall, so you can walk straight through the house along the bridge.",[]],
["Roof the house","Cover the house with a ceiling of stone bricks on layer 12, 15 long and 7 deep: 105 blocks (49 stone bricks and 56 mossy stone bricks). It sits on top of the walls and closes the room.",[]],
["Add the eaves","Round the ceiling, on the ledge, set a ring of eaves on layer 12, 17 long and 9 deep: 48 blocks. Use stone brick stairs with the high side toward the house (44 stairs) and a mossy stone brick on each of the 4 corners. The ring roofs over the ledge, so the lanterns sit under cover.",[]],
["Step the roof","On layer 13, make the second step: a ring of stone brick stairs on the edge of the ceiling, 15 long and 7 deep, high side inward (36 stairs), with a mossy stone brick on each corner. Fill the inside, 13 by 5, with 65 grass blocks. That is 105 blocks, and the roof now has a green top.",[]],
["Pile up the roof mound","On layer 14, in the middle of the grass roof, pile a mound of moss blocks 9 long and 3 deep, leaving out the 4 corner blocks: 23 moss blocks. It makes the roof look like part of the hillside that has grown over the house.",[]],
["Light the ledge","On the front ledge, in front of the house wall, stand oak fences on layer 9 at the 1st, 5th, 9th, 13th and 17th blocks from the left of the body, and put a lantern on top of each (layer 10): 5 fences and 5 lanterns. They glow under the eaves and light the arch at night.",[]],
["Scatter moss carpet","Scatter 25 moss carpets on layer 9: on the ledge outside the walls and on the house floor inside. Leave gaps between them, and keep them out of the spots where the lamps stand. They make the stone look damp and old.",[]],
["Hang the vines","Hang strands of oak leaves from the stone. On the front face of the body, hang them down from the top course (layer 8): at each end of the front, a strand 3 long on the end block, 2 long on the next block and 4 long on the 4th block, counting the end block as the 1st. That is 18 leaves. Then, from the eaves above the front ledge, hang 6 more: 1 at the 3rd block from the left, 2 at the 7th, 1 at the 11th and 2 at the 15th. That is 24 leaves in all.",[]],
["Lay the dirt paths","On the top of the middle row of each ramp, put dirt path in the 4 empty spots, climbing up to the ledge: 4 on the left and 4 on the right, 8 in all (layers 5–8). They show you the way to the house doors.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Plant 12 small bushes on the hills, each a plus shape of 5 oak leaves with 1 more on top in the middle of a few of them, and swap some of the side leaves for flowering azalea leaves. Put them near the ends of the hills, up the back slope and around the lawns. On the roof mound, add 4 leaves: 3 oak and 1 flowering azalea. That is 64 leaves in all: 49 oak and 15 flowering azalea.",[]],
["Scatter flowers and grass","Dot the grass on the hills with 16 small plants: 11 short grass, 3 dandelions and 2 oxeye daisies. Put them on the top of the grass blocks, away from the path.",[]],
["Float the lily pads","Finish the pool in front of the arch with 8 lily pads, floating on the water in the 3 rows at the front of the stream (layer 3). Leave gaps between them, so the pool looks natural.",[]]]},
{id:"lantern-pergola-bridge",name:"Lantern Pergola Bridge",emoji:"🏮",tags:["Bridges", "Cozy", "Nature"],diff:"Medium",time:"3 hr",size:"27×14",photos:[P14],pr:[1.3],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A wooden bridge over a lily-pad pond, with a stepped birch-plank pergola roof held up by stone-brick and log pillars, rows of purple and pink flowers along both edges, lanterns hanging from every beam, log piers and cobblestone paths at each end.",steps:[["Lay the ground and pond bed","Find flat ground and mark a rectangle 27 blocks wide and 14 deep. The long side is the front, and left and right are as you face it. Cover all of it on layer 1 (378 blocks) with 239 coarse dirt, 76 mossy cobblestone and 63 cobblestone. The pond takes the middle: it is 19 wide and 12 deep, which leaves 4 blocks of land on the left and right, 1 row behind and 1 row in front. The 4 corner blocks of the pond stay land, so the pond has rounded corners. Use mostly mossy cobblestone and cobblestone in the pond bed so it looks stony, and coarse dirt on the land.",[]],
["Raise the grass banks","Build the banks on every block outside the pond. Most of the bank is 2 blocks high: coarse dirt on layer 2 and grass on layer 3. Where a block on layer 2 touches the pond water, use mossy cobblestone or cobblestone instead, so the edge looks like old stone. The back row is 1 block higher, with dirt on layers 2–3 and grass on layer 4, and the front row is only 1 block, grass on layer 2, so you can see the pond from the front. Leave the top block empty on the two 3 by 3 patches in the middle of the left and right ends: the path goes there. That is 290 blocks: 136 grass, 100 coarse dirt, 47 mossy cobblestone and 7 cobblestone.",[]],
["Fill the pond","Fill the pond with water on layer 2: 212 blocks. Leave out the 12 spots where the log piers stand. They are at the 8th, 12th, 16th and 20th blocks from the left, in the 3rd, 6th and 9th rows from the back. The water sits 1 block below the grass on the sides and the back, with the mossy stone showing at the edge.",[]],
["Lay the cobblestone paths","At each end of the bridge, lay a path of cobblestone on layer 3 in the middle of the bank, 3 blocks wide and 3 long, running from the edge of the build to the bridge. Use the 5th, 6th and 7th rows from the back. That is 9 blocks on each side, 18 in all: 14 cobblestone with 4 mossy cobblestone scattered in.",[]],
["Sink the log piers","Stand the 12 piers in the pond: oak logs 2 tall on layers 2–3, in the 12 spots you left empty in the water. They rest on the pond bed and hold up the deck. That is 24 logs. The tops sit exactly level with the grass banks.",[]],
["Lay the deck","On layer 4, build the deck, 21 long and 7 deep. It runs from the 4th block from the left to the 24th, so it rests on the grass bank for 1 block at each end and stands on the piers in between. Lay oak logs along both long edges, the front row and the back row (42 logs) and oak planks in the 5 rows between (105 planks). That is 147 blocks.",[]],
["Add the plank steps","At each end of the deck, stand 3 oak stairs on layer 4 in the middle rows, on the last block of the path, the 3rd block in from the end. The high side faces the deck, so you climb up from the path onto the bridge. That is 6 stairs.",[]],
["Raise the pillars","Along the front and the back edge of the deck, stand 7 pillars 4 tall (layers 5–8), every 3rd block, from the 5th block from the left to the 23rd. The pillars at the 5th, 11th, 17th and 23rd blocks are stone: mix 12 stone bricks, 13 mossy stone bricks and 7 cracked stone bricks. The ones at the 8th, 14th and 20th blocks are oak logs (24 logs). That is 14 pillars, 56 blocks. Each pillar stands on the log rim of the deck.",[]],
["Rail the edges","On layer 5, along the front edge and the back edge of the deck, fill every gap between the pillars with oak fences, from the 1st block of the deck to the last. That is 14 fences on each side, 28 in all.",[]],
["Lay the roof beams","On layer 9, on top of the pillars, run an edge beam of oak planks along the front and along the back, from the first pillar to the last: 19 blocks each, 38 in all. Then, above each of the 7 pillar positions, lay a cross beam of birch planks across the deck, the 5 blocks between the two edge beams (35 planks). The cross beams are what give the roof its frame. That is 73 planks.",[]],
["Step the roof up","On layer 10, on each of the 7 cross beams, stack 3 birch planks in the middle, the 3 middle blocks of the 5. That is 21 planks. The roof now starts to rise in the middle, like a shallow step.",[]],
["Lay the ridge","On layer 11, run a ridge of birch planks along the middle of the roof, from the first cross beam to the last: 19 planks. It ties the stepped cross beams together, and it completes the open, airy roof. The gaps between the beams stay open, so light falls onto the flowers.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang lanterns from chains under the beams: each is a chain on layer 8 with a lantern under it on layer 7. First, 1 under each of the 7 cross beams, alternating between the 2nd block and the 4th block of the beam, counting from the back, starting with the 2nd on the leftmost beam. Then 6 along the front edge beam, on its 3rd, 6th, 9th, 12th, 15th and 18th blocks, and 3 along the back edge beam, on its 3rd, 9th and 15th blocks. That is 16 chains and 16 lanterns.",[]],
["Build the planters","On layer 5, build 2 long planters of moss blocks along the deck, in the 4th and the 8th rows from the back, from the 6th to the 22nd block from the left: 17 moss blocks in each row, 34 in all. The 3 middle rows of the deck stay clear as the walkway, 3 blocks wide.",[]],
["Plant the flowers","On top of the planters, on layer 6, plant a mixed row of flowers: 34 plants in all, with 16 alliums for the purple, 8 flowering azalea leaves, 3 pink tulips, 2 poppies, 2 oxeye daisies and 3 dandelions. Mix them up, so no 2 of the same stand side by side too often.",[]],
["Light the entrances","On each bank, 1 block in from the end of the build and in line with the front and back edge of the deck, stand an oak fence on layer 4 and put a lantern on top of it (layer 5). That is 4 fences and 4 lanterns: a lamp on each side of both entrances.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Plant about 13 small bushes on the banks, each a plus shape of 5 leaves, with 1 more on top in the middle of a few of them. Put them in the corners of the banks, along the back row and on the front lawn. Use oak leaves and swap in flowering azalea leaves on the sides of some of them. That is 58 leaves: 49 oak and 9 flowering azalea.",[]],
["Scatter grass and blooms","On the grass of the banks, scatter 17 small plants: 12 short grass, 2 poppies, 1 pink tulip, 1 dandelion and 1 allium. Keep them off the path.",[]],
["Float the lily pads","Finish the pond with 40 lily pads floating on the water, on layer 3. Put them in the rows behind and in front of the bridge, in loose groups with gaps between them, and leave the water under the deck clear. They should look like they have been growing there for years.",[]]]},
{id:"terrace-farm",name:"Terraced Harvest Hall",emoji:"🌾",tags:["Farm","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"8 hr",size:"27×22",photos:[P12],pr:[1.367],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A ring-shaped farm hall built around a terraced garden courtyard: log pillars and lantern-lit wings, a wheat field and irrigation channel on the roof, a staircase up to it, stone-rimmed crop beds with a scarecrow, two ponds and hedged borders.",steps:[["Lay the stone border","On flat ground, mark out a rectangle 27 blocks wide and 19 deep. Trace its edge with stone brick slabs, bottom half, one block wide, all the way round. Leave a gap of 3 blocks in the middle of one of the long edges: this is the front, and the gap is where the grand stairs will go. That is 85 slabs. The border will carry the hedge later.",[]],
["Lay the ground floor","Fill the space inside the border with oak planks, 25 blocks wide and 17 deep, on the same layer as the slabs. This is the floor of the ground-floor hall. That is 425 planks.",[]],
["Stand the outer pillars","The building is a grid of pillars, 7 columns across and 5 rows deep, every fourth block, starting on the first block of the floor. First stand the outer ones: oak logs 3 tall (layers 2–4) on the four corners, then every fourth block along each edge: 7 along the back, 7 along the front, and 3 more on each side between the corners. That is 20 pillars, 60 logs.",[]],
["Stand the inner pillars","Fill in the rest of the grid inside: 5 columns by 3 rows, every fourth block, each 3 oak logs tall (layers 2–4). They hold up the deck above. That is 15 pillars, 45 logs.",[]],
["Wall the back and sides","Between the pillars there are gaps 3 blocks wide, called bays. On layer 2, fill every bay along the back wall (6 bays) and both side walls (4 bays each) with oak planks. Leave the front open, because it is an arcade. That is 14 bays, 42 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Above each wall plank, put 2 glass panes (layers 3–4), so every back and side bay is a tall window. That is 84 panes.",[]],
["Rail the front arcade","Along the open front, fill each of the 6 bays between the pillars with oak fences on layer 2, as a low rail. That is 18 fences. The space above stays open, so you can see into the hall.",[]],
["Stock the storerooms","Number the bays: along the back wall from the left, 1 to 6, and along each side wall from the back, 1 to 4, as you face the house. Everything goes against the wall, 2 high (layers 2–3). Along the back wall, stack barrels in bays 2, 4 and 6 (18 barrels). Along the left wall, stack hay bales in side bays 2 and 4 (12 hay bales). On both side walls, stack barrels in side bay 3 (12 barrels). That is 30 barrels and 12 hay bales.",[]],
["Fill the bookshelves","Along the back wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in bays 1, 3 and 5 (18 shelves). Along the right wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 shelves). That is 30 bookshelves, on layers 2–3.",[]],
["Set out the tables","Along the middle row of pillars, set out two tables: 2 oak slabs side by side between the second and third pillars from the left, and 2 more between the fifth and sixth. Give each table 4 chairs: an oak stair on each side of each slab, with the high side facing away from the table. That is 4 slabs and 8 stairs, all on layer 2.",[]],
["Lay the courtyard deck","The upper floor is U-shaped: a back wing 5 deep and two side wings 5 wide, around a courtyard 15 blocks wide and 12 deep that opens to the front. Start with the courtyard: lay oak planks on top of the pillars, on layer 5, over the whole courtyard, but leave out two things: a strip 3 wide in the middle, which is the path, and a hole 2 wide and 3 deep on each side of the path in the back half, one block away from the path and starting in the second row from the back wing. The two holes will be sunken crop beds. That is 132 planks.",[]],
["Floor the wings","Now lay spruce planks on layer 5 for the three wings: the back wing, 25 blocks by 5, and the two side wings, 5 blocks by 12 each. They join up in a U round the courtyard. That is 245 planks. This layer is also the ceiling of the ground-floor hall.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","Down the middle of the courtyard, fill the 3-wide strip you left with stone bricks, from the front edge to the back wing, on layer 5. That is 36 bricks. It lines up with the gap in the border.",[]],
["Hang the ground-floor lanterns","Under the ceiling, hang a lantern in the middle of every bay of the ground floor: 6 across and 4 deep, on layer 4, each one hanging from the layer above. That is 24 lanterns.",[]],
["Build the grand stairs: the base","In the gap in the border, build a solid block of stone bricks, 3 wide, that rises towards the house: 3 tall right against the front edge, 2 tall one block out, and 1 tall two blocks out. That is 18 bricks.",[]],
["Build the grand stairs: the steps","Along the top of that block, put stone brick stairs, rising towards the house with the high side facing it: one row of 3 on top of each column of bricks, plus one more row of 3 on the ground at the front. That makes 4 rows, 4 steps, up to the deck. That is 12 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the upper pillars","On the wing floors, stand pillars of oak logs, 3 tall (layers 6–8), in the same grid as before, but only on the wings. Leave out the middle pillar of the courtyard side of the back wing, where the door will be. That is 25 pillars, 75 logs.",[]],
["Wall the upper hall","Along the outer edge of the wings, which is the back, both outer sides and the two front ends, fill every bay between the pillars with oak planks on layer 6. That is 48 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the upper windows","Above each of those planks, put 2 glass panes (layers 7–8). That is 96 panes.",[]],
["Rail the courtyard side","On the courtyard side of the wings, fill the bays between the pillars with oak fences on layer 6, so the hall is open to the garden. Leave the 3-wide door open in the middle of the back wing. That is 28 fences.",[]],
["Fill the upper bookshelves","Along the back wall of the upper hall, stack bookshelves 2 high (layers 6–7) in bays 1, 2, 5 and 6 (24 shelves). Along the right wall, stack bookshelves 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 shelves). That is 36 bookshelves.",[]],
["Stock the upper hall","Between the bookshelves at the back, in bays 3 and 4, set a row of barrels, 1 high (6 barrels). On the left wall, stack hay bales 2 high in side bays 2 and 4 (12 hay bales), and put one row of barrels in side bay 3 (3 barrels). Put a row of 3 barrels in side bay 3 of the right wall as well. That is 12 barrels and 12 hay bales.",[]],
["Set out the upper tables","In the middle of each side wing, set a table of 2 oak slabs side by side, in line with the middle row of pillars, with 4 chairs: an oak stair on each side of each slab, high side facing away from the table. That is 4 slabs and 8 stairs, on layer 6.",[]],
["Raise the roof posts","Now the roof farm. On top of each upper pillar, stack 2 more oak logs (layers 9–10), so the pillars stand up above the roof as posts. That is 25 posts, 50 logs.",[]],
["Frame the roof farm","The roof is a U-shaped field 5 blocks wide. Frame it with oak planks on layer 9: a border one block wide all the way round the U, on the outside, on the courtyard side, and across the two front ends, in the gaps between the posts. That is 79 planks. The middle of the field, 3 wide, stays empty for now.",[]],
["Fence the roof","On top of every border plank, put an oak fence on layer 10. On the courtyard side of the right wing, leave the bay between the two middle posts open, 3 blocks wide, because the roof stairs will arrive there. That is 76 fences.",[]],
["Plough the roof farm","Inside the border, fill the whole U, 3 blocks wide, with farmland on layer 9, except for a one-block channel down the middle of each strip: along the back wing, and down the middle of each side wing. Farmland also makes the ceiling of the upper hall. That is 92 farmland blocks.",[]],
["Flood the irrigation channel","Fill the channel with water on layer 9. It runs along the back wing from end to end, then turns and runs down the middle of each side wing to the front. One long channel waters every block of farmland on the roof. That is 49 water blocks.",[]],
["Hang the upper lanterns","Under the roof, hang a lantern from the middle of each bay of the upper hall (layer 8): 6 along the back wing and 3 in each side wing. That is 12 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the roof wheat","Plant wheat on every farmland block on the roof, on layer 10. That is 92 wheat.",[]],
["Light the roof","On layer 11, put a lantern on top of each of the 25 posts, and one more on the middle fence of each bay between two posts. That is 50 lanterns, and they give the roof its glow.",[]],
["Build the roof stairs","At the right-hand end of the courtyard, against the right wing, build a staircase 2 blocks wide and 4 rows long, rising towards the back, on top of the courtyard deck. Row 1: 2 oak stairs on layer 6. Row 2: 1 plank under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 7). Row 3: 2 planks under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 8). Row 4: 3 planks under 2 stairs (stairs on layer 9). Every stair has its high side facing the back. The top row is level with the roof, next to the gap in the fence. That is 12 planks and 8 stairs.",[]],
["Dig the two ponds","In each back corner of the courtyard, in the 2-wide strip beside the beds, build a small pond. Close each end with 2 stone bricks (layer 6), then fill the 2-by-4 space between them with water (layer 6). That is 8 stone bricks and 16 water.",[]],
["Build the crop beds","The garden has 4 beds, each a rectangle of stone bricks 4 wide and 5 deep, with a 2-by-3 space inside. Two sit at the back, against the back wing, round the holes you left in the deck, and two at the front, with one empty row between the back beds and the front beds. They stand on either side of the path, touching it. The 2 back beds are terraced higher: 2 bricks high (layers 6–7). The 2 front beds are 1 brick high (layer 6). That is 14 bricks round each bed, 84 in all.",[]],
["Fill the crop beds","Each bed has a 2-by-3 space inside. In each one, put a water block in the middle row of the left-hand column, and fill the other 5 blocks with farmland. In the two back beds this is the floor of the pit, on layer 5; in the two front beds it is on layer 6. That is 4 water and 20 farmland.",[]],
["Plant wheat and carrots","Plant wheat on all 5 farmland blocks of the back-left bed (layer 6), and on the middle block of the right-hand column of the front-left bed (layer 7). Plant carrots on the 4 farmland blocks of the back-right bed that are not in the middle of the right-hand column (layer 6), and on the middle block of the right-hand column of the front-right bed (layer 7). That is 6 wheat and 5 carrots.",[]],
["Plant pumpkins and melons","Put a pumpkin on each of the 4 corner farmland blocks of the front-left bed, and a melon on each of the 4 corner blocks of the front-right bed (layer 7). That is 4 pumpkins and 4 melons.",[]],
["Stand up the scarecrow","In the back-right bed, on the farmland block you left bare, build a scarecrow: 2 oak fences stacked on top of each other (layers 6–7), a hay bale on top of them (layer 8), an oak fence on each side of the bale along the bed as arms (layer 8), and a pumpkin on top as the head (layer 9). That is 4 fences (2 for the post, 2 for the arms), 1 hay bale and 1 pumpkin.",[]],
["Hedge the crop beds","On top of the stone rims, put leaves to make a low hedge (layer 8 on the two raised back beds, layer 7 on the front ones), leaving gaps where you like: about three of every four rim blocks. Mix them: mostly oak leaves with a flowering azalea leaf now and then. That is 31 oak leaves and 10 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Hedge the garden’s front edge","Along the front edge of the courtyard, on layer 6, plant a hedge of leaves on every deck block, except the 3 blocks of the path. That is 10 oak leaves and 2 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Make the farm corner","In the 2-wide strip at the front left of the courtyard, build a corner for the farm: a block of hay bales 2 wide, 2 deep on layer 6 with a second layer of 2 on the back row, then a gap of one block, then 2 rows of barrels, 2 wide (layer 6), with a flower pot on top of each barrel in the front row (layer 7). Then put 2 barrels and 2 flower pots in front of the roof stairs, at the front of the right-hand strip. That is 6 hay bales, 6 barrels and 4 flower pots.",[]],
["Plant the border hedge","Back down at the ground, plant a hedge along the stone border: leaves on layer 2 on top of about four in every five border slabs, with a flowering azalea leaf now and then. Keep the gap at the front clear. That is 54 oak leaves and 14 flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Plant the two trees","Finish with two oak trees outside the border: one on the left, 2 blocks out from the border, level with the front of the house, and one on the right, 2 blocks out, near the back. Each has a trunk of 5 oak logs and a ball of leaves around the top, about 5 blocks across. That is 10 logs and 112 leaves for the pair.",[]]]},
{id:"sakura-teahouse",name:"Sakura Tea House",emoji:"🍵",tags:["Japanese","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Hard",time:"4 hr",size:"21×20",photos:[P11],pr:[1.274],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A Japanese tea house with a cherry-wood veranda and frame, a sweeping dark roof, a torii gate over a stone path, a koi pond with a little bridge, and two blossoming cherry trees.",steps:[["Lay the stone plinth","On flat ground, mark out a rectangle 13 blocks wide and 11 deep, and fill it with stone bricks. This is the raised base of the tea house, 143 blocks. One of the long sides is the front, facing the path you will build later.",[]],
["Trim the plinth with stone slabs","Just outside the plinth, run a ring of stone brick slabs one block wide, all the way round, bottom half. This is a stone footing, and it makes the plinth look like it sits in the ground. The ring is 15 blocks by 13. In the middle of the front edge, leave a gap of 5 blocks: 3 for the front steps and one either side for the lantern posts, which come later. That is 47 slabs.",[]],
["Add the stone front steps","In the middle of the front edge, in the gap of the footing, set 3 stone brick stairs side by side with their high side against the plinth. This is the bottom step up to the house. Leave the blocks either side of them bare for now.",[]],
["Lay the cherry floor","Cover the plinth with cherry planks, one layer up, but leave a 3-wide notch in the middle of the front edge for the next step. That is 140 planks. This floor is also the veranda that runs round the house.",[]],
["Add the cherry front steps","Fill the notch with 3 cherry stairs, high side towards the house. With the stone steps below them they make a two-step staircase up to the veranda.",[]],
["Stand the four corner posts","The house itself is 9 blocks wide and 7 deep, set 2 blocks in from every edge of the plinth. On its four corners, stand posts of cherry logs, 5 blocks tall. That is 20 logs.",[]],
["Stand the wall posts","Along each wall, add more posts 5 tall, one every second block: 3 along the back wall, 2 on the front wall, and 2 on each side wall. Leave the 3 blocks in the middle of the front wall free for the doorway. That is 9 posts, 45 logs.",[]],
["Lay the skirt boards","On the bottom layer of the walls, fill each gap between posts with a cherry plank. The doorway stays open. That is 12 planks.",[]],
["Fill in the paper walls","Above the skirt boards, fill 6 of the 12 gaps with white concrete, like paper screens. These are the solid gaps: the outer gaps of the back wall and the two end gaps of each side wall. In each one stack white concrete (layer 4), a cherry plank as a crossbar (layer 5) and white concrete again (layer 6). The other 6 gaps are the windows, and wait for the next two steps. That is 12 white concrete and 6 cherry planks.",[]],
["Lay the window sills","In each of the 6 window gaps, lay a cherry plank on top of the skirt board as a sill (layer 4). These are the 2 middle gaps of the back wall, the 2 end gaps of the front wall and the middle gap of each side wall. The flower boxes will sit just in front of them. That is 6 planks.",[]],
["Set the window panes","Above each of the 6 sills, fill the window gap with glass panes, 2 tall: the 2 middle gaps of the back wall, the 2 end gaps of the front wall, and the middle gap of each side wall. That is 12 panes.",[]],
["Run the beam round the top","On the top layer, fill the gaps between the posts with cherry planks, including over the doorway so it makes a lintel. That is 15 planks. The walls are now 5 tall, and the doorway is 4 tall.",[]],
["Lay the tatami mats","Inside the house, lay moss carpet on the floor to look like tatami mats. The room is 7 blocks wide and 5 deep. Leave these spaces bare, because the next step fills them: the 3 blocks in the middle of the back row, a patch 3 wide and 2 deep in the middle of the room, 2 spaces on each side of that patch, and one space in each back corner. That is 20 carpets.",[]],
["Furnish the room","Stand 3 bookshelves side by side in the middle of the back row, 2 high. In the bare patch in the middle of the room, lay a tea table of 6 cherry slabs. Put a red carpet cushion in each of the 4 spaces either side of the table, and a lantern on the floor in each back corner. That is 6 bookshelves, 6 slabs, 4 red carpets and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Stand the veranda posts","Around the edge of the plinth, stand more cherry log posts, 5 tall: one on each corner, one in the middle of the back edge, two on each side edge, and two on the front edge, either side of the 3-block gap for the steps. That is 11 posts, 55 logs.",[]],
["Rail the veranda","On the layer above the floor, fill the gaps between the veranda posts with cherry fences, all the way round the plinth edge. Leave the gap in front of the steps open. That is 30 fences.",[]],
["Stand the lantern posts","On either side of the front steps, in the two spots left bare in the footing, stand a post of 3 cherry fences (layers 1–3) and set a lantern on top of each (layer 4). They frame the stairs, like lamp posts at the corners of a railing. That is 6 fences and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Add the roof brackets","Before the roof goes on, tuck cherry stairs against the veranda posts on the top layer (layer 7), with their high side against the post. They are the brackets that will hold up the eaves. Along the front, put 6: one on the inside of each corner post, and one either side of the two middle posts. Along the back, put 4: one on the inside of each corner post, and one either side of the middle post. On each side, put 2: one beside each of the two middle posts, facing the middle of the wall. That is 14 stairs. The lanterns will hang in the gaps between the brackets later.",[]],
["Set the flower boxes","On the veranda floor, in front of the windows, set a row of cherry trapdoors against the wall (layer 3) to be the flower boxes. Under each front window put 3, centred on the window: 6 in all. Under the two back windows put one long row of 5, from one block left of the left window to one block right of the right window. Under each side window put 3, along the wall. Leave the 3 blocks in front of the door free. That is 17 trapdoors.",[]],
["Plant the flowers","On top of each trapdoor, plant a flower (layer 4): pink tulips along the front and back, and alliums along the two sides. That is 11 pink tulips and 6 alliums.",[]],
["Roof: the pink eaves","The roof is built from rings, each ring one block further in than the last. The rings come in pairs, and each pair sits one layer higher than the one before. Start with the pink edge: a ring of cherry slabs, bottom half, sticking out one block past the plinth: 15 blocks by 13. At each of the four corners, lift the tip: use a full cherry plank instead of a slab on the corner block and on the block either side of it (12 planks), then put one slab on top of each corner plank. Just inside the ring, on the same layer, put a ring of cherry stairs resting on top of the veranda posts and brackets, high side facing in: 13 by 11, which is 44 stairs. Use the corner stair shape on the four corners. That is 12 planks, 44 slabs and 44 stairs, all cherry.",[]],
["Roof: the second layer","From here on the roof is dark oak, which makes the pink edge stand out. Inside the stairs ring, lay a ring of dark oak planks 11 by 9 (36 planks) and put a ring of dark oak slabs on top of it (36 slabs). Then lay a ring of planks 9 by 7 inside that (28 planks), which sits right on top of the house walls, and put a ring of dark oak stairs on top of it, high side facing in (28 stairs). That is 64 planks, 36 slabs and 28 stairs.",[]],
["Roof: the third layer","Same again, one layer higher. Lay a ring of planks 7 by 5 (20 planks) and put slabs on top of it (20 slabs). Then a ring of planks 5 by 3 inside that (12 planks), with stairs on top (12 stairs). That is 32 planks, 20 slabs and 12 stairs.",[]],
["Cap the ridge","The last ring leaves a gap 3 blocks long and 1 wide at the top. Fill it with 3 dark oak planks, level with the last stairs. On top of them, lay a dark oak plank in the middle and a slab at each end. Finish with a lightning rod on the middle plank as a finial. That is 4 planks, 2 slabs and 1 lightning rod.",[]],
["Lay the stone path","From the front steps, lay a path of stone brick slabs, 3 wide and 5 long, running straight out from the stone stairs. Then, from the last row of the path, lay a line of 2 stepping stones to the right, towards where the pond will go. That is 15 slabs for the path and 2 for the stones, 17 in all, on the bottom layer.",[]],
["Stand the torii pillars","The torii is the gate over the path. On the fourth row of the path, stand a cherry log pillar 5 tall on each side of it, leaving the 3 path blocks between them. That is 10 logs.",[]],
["Span the torii","Join the pillars with a row of 3 cherry planks between them on the fourth layer, then lay a row of 7 across the top of the pillars on the sixth layer, sticking out one block on each side. Join the two beams with one more plank in the middle, on the fifth layer. That is 11 planks.",[]],
["Cap the torii","On top of the long beam, lay a row of 9 cherry slabs, which sticks out one more block on each side and gives the gate its curved top. That is 9 slabs.",[]],
["Build the stone lanterns","On the first row of the path, one block either side of it, stack 2 stone bricks and put a lantern on top. That is 4 stone bricks and 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Raise the pond rim","In the front right corner of the garden, as you face the house, leave a gap of 2 blocks from the path (the stepping stones will fill it) and mark a rectangle 7 wide and 5 deep. Trace its edge in stone bricks, one layer high. That is 20 blocks, with a 5 by 3 space in the middle.",[]],
["Fill the pond","Fill the 5 by 3 space inside the rim with water, except for the two corners at the right-hand end, where you put mossy cobblestone as rocks. That is 13 water blocks and 2 mossy cobblestone.",[]],
["Edge the pond with moss","Lay moss blocks on the grass just outside the rim, to soften the stone edge: 4 down the left side, leaving the row with the stepping stones clear, and 7 along the front. That is 11 moss blocks.",[]],
["Lay the bridge deck","The bridge is a 5-block arch across the pond, 3 wide, against the left side of the pond. First lay a deck of cherry planks over the water, 3 wide and 3 long, one layer up. Then put a second row of planks on top of the middle row of the deck, which makes the top of the arch. That is 9 + 3 = 12 planks.",[]],
["Add the arch stairs","Put cherry stairs on each end of the arch, always with the high side towards the middle of the bridge: a row of 3 on the rim at each end, and a row of 3 on the deck at each end, on top of the outer rows of planks. That is 12 stairs, 6 on the rim and 6 on the deck.",[]],
["Rail the bridge","Along both sides of the bridge, follow the arch with cherry fences, 5 on each side: the 3 over the middle on layer 4, and the 2 at the ends on layer 3. That is 10 fences.",[]],
["Light the bridge and the pond","Set a lantern on top of each of the 4 fences at the ends of the bridge rails (layer 4), and one on each of the 4 corners of the pond rim (layer 2). They light the water at night. That is 8 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the big trunk","In the front left corner, level with the torii, stand a 2 by 2 trunk of cherry logs, 7 tall. That is 28 logs.",[]],
["Add the branches","Four branches, each 3 logs. Start them on the sixth layer, touching each side of the trunk, run two blocks out, and turn the last log up by one block. That is 12 logs.",[]],
["Grow the lower canopy","Cover the trunk and branches with cherry leaves in a wide round shape on layers 7 and 8, then let a few hang down one layer lower, on layer 6, for a ragged edge. Leave a few gaps so it looks natural. That is 52 leaves: 3 on layer 6, 24 on layer 7 and 25 on layer 8.",[]],
["Grow the upper canopy","Build the top of the canopy on layers 9, 10 and 11, smaller than the layers below. That is 74 leaves.",[]],
["Plant the small trees","In the two back corners of the garden, plant a smaller tree each: a trunk of 4 cherry logs and a ball of cherry leaves around the top, about 3 blocks across. That is 8 logs and 53 leaves for the pair.",[]],
["Scatter the petals","On the grass under all three trees, scatter pink petals, about one block in three. Leave the stone footing bare. That is 33 pink petals.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Finally, hang lanterns from the underside of the roof, on the seventh layer: 3 across the front (one above the steps and one either side), 2 along the back either side of the middle post, and 1 in the middle of each side. Then hang one more from the middle of the torii's lower beam, on layer 3. That is 8 lanterns.",[]]]},
{id:"aldercrest-house",name:"Aldercrest House",emoji:"🏘️",tags:["Houses","Medieval","Showpiece"],diff:"Hard",time:"3 hr",size:"20×10",photos:[P1],pr:[1.21],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A timber-framed two-storey house on a stepped stone base, with a jettied upper floor, stepped gables, a deep red roof, a dormer, a red-carpet porch, glowing lanterns and flower boxes under every window.",note:'Build reference: <a class="go" href="https://builditapp.com/aldercrest-house/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aldercrest House on Build It App</a>. The 3D model and these steps are my own reconstruction from the screenshots, so the layout and block counts are best guesses, not the original instructions.',steps:[["Lay the main floor","Find flat ground and mark a rectangle 9 blocks wide and 8 deep for the main house. The short side is the front, and left and right are as you face it. Lay a floor of stone bricks across it (layer 1). Mix the bricks so the stone looks old: 55 stone bricks, 9 mossy stone bricks and 8 cracked stone bricks, 72 blocks in all.",[]],
["Lay the wing floor","Add a second floor on the right, 7 wide and 6 deep, touching the right side of the main floor (layer 1). Its back lines up with the back of the main house, so its front sits 2 blocks behind the main front. Use the same mix: 38 stone bricks, 1 mossy stone bricks and 3 cracked stone bricks, 42 blocks.",[]],
["Step the base","Edge the floors with stone brick stairs on layer 1, high side toward the house, so the building sits on a stepped base. On the main house put 9 along the front and 8 down the left side. Round the wing put 6 along the front, leaving the 3rd block from the left open for the entrance, and 6 down its right end. Leave the corners empty. That is 29 stairs.",[]],
["Raise the main stone walls","Run a wall of stone bricks round the edge of the main floor, 4 tall (layers 2–5), mixing in mossy and cracked bricks. Leave out the post spots: the 4 corners, the 4th block back from the front on each long side, and the middle of the front and back. Also leave 6 window gaps 2 tall on layers 3–4: on the front and back walls at the 3rd and 7th blocks from the left, and on the left wall at the 3rd and 6th blocks from the front. That is 76 blocks: 61 stone bricks, 7 mossy stone bricks and 8 cracked stone bricks.",[]],
["Stand the log posts","Fill the 8 post spots with oak logs, 4 tall (layers 2–5): 32 logs. These chunky posts carry the overhanging upper floor, and they give the ground floor its timber-and-stone look.",[]],
["Set the wing posts","Put oak log posts, 4 tall (layers 2–5), round the wing: on the front and the back at the 1st, 5th and 7th blocks from the left, and 1 more on the right end, the 3rd block from the back. That is 7 posts, 28 logs.",[]],
["Raise the wing walls","Build stone brick walls 4 tall (layers 2–5) along the back, the front and the right end of the wing, between the posts. Leave a door gap 2 tall at the 3rd block from the left on the front, with stone above it on layers 4–5. Leave 4 window gaps 2 tall on layers 3–4: on the front at the 6th block, on the back at the 3rd and 6th, and on the right end next to the front post. That is 34 blocks of mixed stone.",[]],
["Hang the door","Stand 2 spruce planks in the door gap of the wing, as the front door (layers 2–3).",[]],
["Glaze the ground floor","Fill every ground-floor window gap with glass panes: 20 in all, 12 in the main house and 8 in the wing (layers 3–4).",[]],
["Lay the upper floor","On layer 6, build the jettied floor, which sticks out past the stone. The main part is 11 wide and 9 deep: it sticks out 1 block past the ground floor on the left and at the front, and its right edge sits over the first column of the wing floor. The wing part is 7 wide and 7 deep, sticking out 1 block at the front and at the right end, and it joins the main part along the right side. Edge both parts in spruce planks, including the line where they meet, and fill the middle with oak planks. That is 55 spruce and 93 oak, 148 blocks.",[]],
["Add the brackets","Under the overhang on layer 5, set 12 oak stairs as brackets, with the high side against the wall. Put 3 along the front of the main house (1st, middle and last block), 3 down its left side (back end, 4th block from the front, and front end), 3 along the front of the wing (1st, 5th and 7th) and 3 down its right end (back, 3rd and last).",[]],
["Raise the spruce posts","Stand spruce planks as posts 4 tall (layers 7–10). On the main house they go at the 4 corners and at the middle of the left and right sides, plus the 5th and 7th block on the front and back (the pair that flank the middle). On the wing, on the front and back at the 1st, 4th and 7th blocks, and 1 in the middle of the right end. That is 17 posts, 68 planks.",[]],
["Fill the upper walls","Fill between the posts with oak planks on layers 7–9, leaving window gaps 2 tall on layers 8–9. Beside each window gap put spruce planks as shutters instead of oak, each 2 tall, one on each side where there is room. The main house has 6 window gaps: the 3rd and 9th blocks of the front and back, and the 3rd and 7th blocks of the left wall. The wing has 6: 2 on the front, 2 on the back and 2 on the right end. That is 54 oak and 36 spruce planks.",[]],
["Glaze the upper windows","Fill all 12 upstairs window gaps with glass panes: 24 in all (layers 8–9).",[]],
["Run the top beam","Between the posts, run a beam of spruce planks along the top of every upper wall on layer 10: 38 planks. This dark line is where the roof will sit.",[]],
["Close in the main gables","On the front and back of the main house, above the top beam, build a triangle of oak planks 5 rows tall (layers 11–15): 9, 7, 5, 3 and 1 wide, each row 1 block in from the last, centered. The front gable gets a window: put 2 glass panes in the middle on layers 12–13. That is 48 planks and 2 panes.",[]],
["Stair-step the gable edges","Edge each gable with oak stairs, high side facing the middle. On each side start 1 block out from the end of the top beam, on layer 10, then step 1 in and 1 up for 6 stairs, ending on layer 15 beside the single top plank. Cap the top with an oak plank on layer 16. The back gable's right side has only 5 stairs, as its lowest spot is already taken by the wing's wall. That is 23 stairs and 2 planks.",[]],
["Add the eave stairs","Between the two gables, set oak stairs on layer 10 along the left side, 1 block out from the wall, high side facing the middle: 7 stairs. Add 1 more at the front right, between the front gable's edge and the wing. That is 8 stairs.",[]],
["Roof the main house","Between the gables, build both slopes of the roof from red nether brick stairs, high side facing the middle. Place 5 rows on each side, each row 1 block in and 1 up from the last, on layers 11–15, and each row is 7 long. That is 70 stairs. The roof is steep, so check each row sits right against the oak edge.",[]],
["Cap the ridge","Run red nether bricks along the top between the two gables, on layer 16: 7 blocks.",[]],
["Close the wing gable","On the right end of the wing, above the top beam, fill a small triangle with oak planks: 5 on layer 11, 3 on layer 12 and 1 on layer 13. That is 9 planks.",[]],
["Roof the wing","The wing roof runs the other way, with its ridge along the wing's length. On the front start with oak stairs on layer 10, 1 block out from the upper wall, then rows of red nether brick stairs on layers 11, 12 and 13, each 1 block in and 1 up, with the high side toward the ridge. Leave a 3 wide gap on layer 11 at the 3rd to 5th blocks for the dormer. Do the same on the back, with oak stairs on layer 10 then red rows on layers 11–13. On the right end, make every stair in the edge oak instead of red, and add oak planks at the end of the ridge. Lay the ridge in red nether bricks on layer 14. That is 33 red stairs, 19 oak stairs, 6 red nether bricks and 1 plank.",[]],
["Build the dormer","In the gap on the front slope, place oak planks on the left and right and a glass pane in the middle (layer 11). Above, put a red nether brick stair on each side, tilted toward the middle, with an oak plank between them (layer 12), and a red nether brick on top (layer 13). In front of the glass, set an oak trapdoor on layer 11 and a rose bush on layer 12 as a flower box. That is 9 blocks.",[]],
["Build the entrance porch","In front of the door, stand 2 oak fence posts 4 tall (layers 1–4), one on each side of the door's column and 3 blocks out from the wall. Roof them on layer 5 with a 3 × 3 canopy: oak planks on the row under the overhang and spruce planks on the other two. Cover the 2 front rows of the canopy with red carpet (layer 6) and lay 3 red carpets on the ground, from the door out to the posts (layer 1). That is 26 blocks: 8 fences, 6 spruce planks, 3 oak planks and 9 red carpets.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang 7 lanterns. Under the overhang, between the brackets: 2 on the main front, 2 on the left side, 1 on the front of the wing and 1 in the middle of the wing's right end. Hang the last from the middle of the porch canopy, over the carpet. They glow warmly against the timber.",[]],
["Add the flower boxes","Set an oak trapdoor in front of each window sill on layer 7, with a rose bush on top on layer 8: 2 on the front of the main house, 2 on the left side, 2 on the front of the wing and 2 on its right end. Add a ninth under the gable window, trapdoor on layer 11 and rose bush on layer 12. That is 9 trapdoors and 9 rose bushes.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","From the red carpet, lay a dirt path leading out from the porch: 1 wide at first, then 3 wide, 5 long in all, leaving a few gaps so it looks worn. That is 11 dirt path blocks.",[]],
["Plant leafy bushes","Round the foot of the house, put oak leaves as bushes, 1 or 2 high: mostly along the back, a pair at the left side, a cluster at the front left corner, a few in front of the wing and at its right end. That is 33 leaves.",[]],
["Scatter flowers","Scatter 19 flowers over the grass: 8 poppies, 9 dandelions and 2 oxeye daisies.",[]],
["Add the grass","Finish with 99 short grass tufts over the open ground, thinner by the house and none on the path.",[]]]},
{id:"brindlewood-manor",name:"Brindlewood Manor",emoji:"🏡",tags:["Houses","Medieval","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"7 hr",size:"23×20",photos:[P10],pr:[1.215],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A walled-garden manor in the medieval timber-and-stone style: a stone ground floor, a plastered and timber-framed upper floor, front balconies, dormers, cobblestone-trimmed gables, a porch, a well, trees and a vegetable bed.",steps:[["Mark out the garden and lay the wall base","On flat ground, mark a 23×20 rectangle for the garden. The side with the gate is the front. Lay one course of stone bricks around the whole outline, but leave a 3-wide gap in the middle of the front side for the gate. That is 79 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the cobblestone wall and pillars","Go round again with a course of cobblestone on top of the stone bricks, and swap in about 4 mossy cobblestone blocks, scattered, so it looks weathered. At 15 spots, build a 4-high stone brick pillar instead: the four corners, both sides of the gate gap, and the rest every 5 or 6 blocks along the walls. The model shows exactly where.",[]],
["Rail the wall, cap the pillars and span the gate","Put an oak fence on top of every cobblestone block that is not a pillar. Cap the 9 pillars that are not at a corner or beside the gate with a stone brick slab. Leave the six corner and gate pillars bare, because they get lanterns later. Finish the gate with a 3-block dark oak plank beam across the gap, level with the pillar tops.",[]],
["Lay the stone plinth","Inside the garden, mark the house: a 15×7 main block with a 7-wide wing sticking out 4 blocks from the front, so the whole house is 15×11. Leave 3 blocks of space between the house and the garden wall at the back and on both sides, and 4 blocks at the front. Lay a one-block ring of stone bricks along that outline, mixing in about 7 mossy stone bricks. Only the outline is needed for now; lay any floor you like inside later.",[]],
["Add the door steps","Place three oak stairs in front of the wing, centered on the front door spot, with their tall backs toward the house. Then place one more oak stair at the middle of the back wall, for the back door.",[]],
["Set the dark oak posts","On top of the plinth, stand dark oak logs 3 blocks high at 12 spots: every outside corner, the two inside corners where the wing meets the main block, one either side of the front door spot, and two on the back wall, 3 blocks either side of the middle.",[]],
["Fill in the main block's walls","Fill between the posts of the main block with cobblestone, 3 blocks high: the back wall, both end walls and the two short stretches of front wall either side of the wing. Mix in about 14 mossy cobblestone. Leave a 1×2 doorway in the middle of the back wall and put a dark oak plank across the top of it. At the 6 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: two on the front, two on the back and one in the middle of each end wall.",[]],
["Fill in the front wing's walls","Do the same for the wing: its front wall and its two side walls, 3 blocks high in cobblestone, with about 4 mossy blocks mixed in. Leave a 1×2 doorway in the middle of the front wall and put a dark oak plank across the top. At the 4 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: one on each side of the front door, just past the posts, and one in the middle of each side wall.",[]],
["Glaze the ground-floor windows","Fill the two open blocks above each of those 10 window spots, 6 on the main block and 4 on the wing, with glass panes. That makes ten 2-tall windows, 20 panes altogether.",[]],
["Lay the floor beam","Run a one-block ring of dark oak planks around the top of the walls, following the whole outline. It carries the upper floor and shows as the dark band between the two storeys.",[]],
["Build the balcony decks","At each end of the front, add a 4×2 oak plank deck on the outer side of the wing, level with the floor beam and touching the main front wall. Hold each deck up with two dark oak logs, 4 blocks tall, under its two outer corners (the ones away from the house).",[]],
["Fence the balconies","Put oak fence along the long outer edge of each deck and along its outer end. The back edge sits against the house and the inner end against the wing wall, so leave those two sides open. That is 5 fence blocks per balcony.",[]],
["Frame the upper floor","Continue the dark oak posts up another 3 blocks from the floor beam at the same corners as downstairs, but leave out the two posts beside the front door, because that wall becomes a wide window. That is 10 posts.",[]],
["Plaster the main block's upper walls","Fill between the posts of the main block with smooth sandstone, 3 blocks high. On the front wall, make a 1×2 doorway onto each balcony, directly above the ground-floor windows, and put a dark oak plank across the top of each one. At the 7 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: one beside each balcony doorway toward the end of the house, three on the back wall (the middle and one either side) and one in the middle of each end wall.",[]],
["Plaster the wing's upper walls","Do the same for the wing: smooth sandstone, 3 blocks high, along its front wall and both side walls. At the 5 window spots, build only the bottom block for now: three side by side over the front door, and one in the middle of each side wall.",[]],
["Glaze the upper windows","Fill the two open blocks above each of the 12 window spots with glass panes. The three spots over the front door sit side by side, so that window reads as one wide window. That is 24 panes in all.",[]],
["Fill the main gable ends","On each end wall of the main block, build a triangle of smooth sandstone on top of the wall: 5 blocks wide in the bottom row, 3 in the middle row and 1 on top. Swap the middle block of the middle row for a glass pane. The roof will overhang this by one block.",[]],
["Main roof, row 1: the eaves","Start with the lowest row of the roof on one long side. Lay a line of spruce stairs sticking out one block past the wall, with the low step facing outward and the tall back toward the middle of the house. Behind it, lay a line of spruce planks directly on top of the wall. Run it the full 15 blocks between the two gable walls, then repeat on the other long side.",[]],
["Main roof, row 2","One block higher and one block closer to the middle: a line of spruce stairs sitting on top of row 1's planks, with a line of planks behind them. Run it the full 15 blocks again, on both sides.",[]],
["Main roof, row 3","The same again, one block higher and one block in: stairs on top of row 2's planks, planks behind them, on both sides.",[]],
["Main roof, row 4 and the ridge","The top row is two lines of stairs, one on each side, with a single line of spruce planks between them along the very top. That line is the ridge, and the two sides of the roof now meet in the middle.",[]],
["Trim the main gables in cobblestone","Along both gable ends, replace the outermost column of the roof with cobblestone: cobblestone stairs where the spruce stairs would go, plain cobblestone where the planks would go. This column sticks out one block past the gable wall and follows the same four-row stair-step shape.",[]],
["Frame the dormer windows","Mark five dormers: two on the front slope, one above each end of the house, and three on the back slope, one in the middle and one 5 blocks either side. Each dormer stands on row 2 of the roof: two dark oak logs with a glass pane between them.",[]],
["Roof the dormers","Give each dormer a small 3-wide gable. In front, put a cobblestone stair on each side with the tall back toward the middle and a cobblestone block between them. Directly behind that, up the slope, repeat the shape in spruce: spruce stairs on each side and a spruce plank in the middle.",[]],
["Fill the front gable end","On the wing's front wall, build the same sandstone triangle as the main gables: 5 blocks wide, then 3, then 1, with a glass pane in the middle of the middle row.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 1: the eaves","Roof the wing the same way as the main house, working from the front wall back toward the main roof. Row 1: along each side wall, a line of spruce stairs overhanging the wall by one block, tall backs toward the middle of the wing, with a line of spruce planks on top of the wall behind it. It is only 3 blocks long on each side, because the main roof already covers the rest.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 2","One block higher and one block in, on both sides: stairs on top of row 1's planks, planks behind them. This row is 4 blocks long on each side, and its far end touches the main roof's second row.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 3","Another block higher and in: stairs and planks again, now 5 blocks long on each side, running back until they meet the main roof.",[]],
["Wing roof, row 4 and the ridge","Two lines of stairs, 6 blocks long, with a line of spruce planks between them along the top for the wing's ridge. It runs from the main ridge out to the front and ends at the same height as the main one.",[]],
["Trim the front gable","Replace the outermost line of the wing roof at the front end with cobblestone stairs and cobblestone blocks, sticking out one block past the gable wall, to match the main gables.",[]],
["Build the chimney","Near the left end of the main ridge, as you face the front, stand a 2×2 chimney on the roof, straddling the ridge with half of it over the back slope. Build it 2 blocks high in stone bricks, then cap it with 4 dark oak logs.",[]],
["Add the front porch","Over the front door, make a 5×2 porch. Stand a dark oak log post, 4 blocks tall, at each outer corner of the front edge. Lay dark oak planks across the top, two rows of five, one row against the house wall and one over the posts. Roof it with a row of spruce planks against the wall and a row of spruce stairs on the outer edge, tall backs toward the house.",[]],
["Set the flower pots","Put a flower pot against the wall under each of the seven upper windows on the front, back and ends, plus one pot on each balcony deck, just in front of the balcony doorway. That is 9 pots.",[]],
["Hang the lanterns","Hang a lantern from the porch beam on each side of the path, from the underside of each of the four lowest corner stairs of the main roof, and from under each balcony deck, between the two support posts. That is 8 hanging lanterns.",[]],
["Stand the balcony lanterns","Stand a lantern on the fence post at each end of every balcony's outer rail. That is 4 lanterns, and they light the balcony doors.",[]],
["Build the well base","In the front-left of the garden, as you face the house, lay a 3×3 square of cobblestone with the middle block replaced by water.",[]],
["Roof the well and hang the chains","Stand an oak fence 2 blocks high at each corner of the well. On top, lay a ring of spruce stairs around the edge, tall backs toward the middle, a spruce plank in the center and a spruce slab on top of that. Hang two chains from the center plank down toward the water.",[]],
["Plant the tree trunks","Plant two trees: one in the front-right of the garden and one in the back-left. Each trunk is 4 oak logs tall.",[]],
["Grow the leaf crowns","Wrap each trunk in a rounded ball of oak leaves, about 5 blocks across. Work from the bottom: a plus shape around the trunk, then a 3×3 with the four tips added, then the widest layer, a 5×5 with the corners cut off, then another 3×3 with tips, then a plus on top. Swap about 5 leaves on each tree for flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Make the vegetable bed","In the back-right corner, mark a 5×3 bed and put one water block in the middle. Hoe the other 14 blocks into farmland and plant wheat on each one. The water in the middle keeps all of it moist. If you want it level with the grass, dig the bed down one block first.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","Lay a 3-wide path of cobblestone slabs from the gate, through the gap, to the front steps: 4 slabs long, so 12 in all.",[]],
["Add the lamp posts and gate lanterns","Beside the path, just beyond the porch, build two lamp posts: 2 oak fence blocks tall with a lantern standing on top. Put a lantern on the gate beam, and one on top of each of the six bare pillars: the four corners and the two beside the gate.",[]],
["Plant the bushes","Plant 14 small bushes around the foot of the house and along the garden walls: nine single blocks of oak leaves and five that are 2 blocks tall, with about half of them topped with flowering azalea leaves.",[]],
["Add barrels and roses","Put a barrel on each side of the front steps, then plant four rose bushes: two beside the porch posts and one beside each end of the house.",[]]]},
{id:"cosy-woodland-lodge",name:"Cosy Woodland Lodge",emoji:"🍂",tags:["Houses","Cozy","Nature"],diff:"Medium",time:"2 hr",size:"26×20",photos:[P3],pr:[1.231],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A cosy two-storey timber lodge with a stone-edged gable, a red brick pier, a glowing entry bay with shutters, a low wing with an arched window, a tall stone chimney and warm lantern light.",note:'Build by @ireniepooh on TikTok. The 3D model and these steps are my own reconstruction from a still photo of the build, so the layout and block choices are best guesses, not the creator’s own instructions.',steps:[["Lay the main floor","Find flat ground and mark a rectangle 7 blocks wide and 9 deep for the main house. The short side is the front, and left and right are as you face it. Lay a floor of stone bricks across it (layer 1). Mix the bricks so the stone looks old: 51 stone bricks, 5 mossy stone bricks and 7 cracked stone bricks. That is 63 blocks.",[]],
["Lay the wing and entry floors","Two more floors, also in mixed stone bricks. The wing is 9 wide and 5 deep and joins the right side of the main house; it sits in the middle of the house's length, so its front is 2 blocks behind the front of the house and its back is 2 blocks in front of the back. The entry bay is 5 wide and 3 deep and sticks out of the front, starting 1 block past the left wall and ending at the middle of the front. That is 60 blocks (51 stone bricks, 4 mossy stone bricks and 5 cracked stone bricks).",[]],
["Edge the walls in stone","On layer 2, run a course of stone bricks along the edge of the main house floor on all four sides, but leave the front right corner open for the door. Mix in some mossy and cracked bricks as before. That is 27 blocks.",[]],
["Set the dark door","In the gap at the front right corner, stack 2 dark oak planks as the door (layers 2–3).",[]],
["Raise the timber posts","Stand dark oak logs on the stone course as posts, 5 tall (layers 3–7). Put one at each corner and then every second block along both long sides, so each side has 5 posts. The front right corner post sits on top of the door, so it starts a layer higher. That is 49 logs.",[]],
["Fill in the plank walls","Fill the gaps between the posts with oak planks, 5 tall (layers 3–7). Leave out the 2 front blocks next to the door post, which are for the brick pier, and leave window gaps 1 wide and 2 tall, on layers 4–5: two on each side wall, in the 2nd and 3rd gaps from the front, and one in the middle of the back wall. That is 70 planks.",[]],
["Add the brick pier","In the 2 blocks left of the door post, on the front, build a pier of bricks the full 5 layers (layers 3–7), leaving a window gap 2 wide and 2 tall on layers 4–5. That is 6 bricks. This pier gives the front its red-brown stripe.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Fill every window gap with glass panes: 14 in all. The front pier window takes 4 (2 × 2), each side wall has 2 windows of 2 panes, and the back wall has 1 window of 2 panes (layers 4–5).",[]],
["Close in the gables","Above the walls build the three-step triangle of the front and back gables (layers 8–10). The front: on layer 8 put 3 oak planks on the left and 2 bricks on the right, on layer 9 put a plank, a glass pane and a brick, and on layer 10 a single glass pane. The 2 panes in the middle make a tall window. The back: 5 oak planks on layer 8, 3 on layer 9 and 1 on layer 10. That is 13 planks, 3 bricks and 2 panes.",[]],
["Edge the gables in stone","Build the stepped stone edge of the roof. On each side of a gable, place stone brick stairs climbing from the wall tops to the middle: 4 stairs on each side, on layers 7, 8, 9 and 10, each one block in and one up, with the high side facing the middle. Finish the top with a stone brick on layer 11. Do this three times: on the front, again one block behind the front so the stone edge is two deep, and on the back. That is 24 stairs and 3 bricks.",[]],
["Roof the eaves","Between the front and back stone edges, place dark oak stairs on layer 7 along both long sides, sticking out 1 block past the walls, with the high side facing the middle. Each side is 6 long: 12 stairs.",[]],
["Roof the middle","Add the next two rows of dark oak stairs up each slope, on layers 8 and 9, each row 1 block in and 1 up from the last, still high side to the middle. That is 24 stairs, 6 per row on each side.",[]],
["Top the roof","Add the last row of stairs on layer 10, then run a ridge of dark oak planks along the top on layer 11. Leave a 2 × 2 hole for the chimney, in the two rows that are 2 and 3 blocks from the back gable's stone edge: skip the ridge block and the stair on its right in each of those two rows. That is 10 stairs and 4 planks.",[]],
["Raise the chimney","In the hole, build a chimney 2 × 2 out of stone bricks, 5 tall (layers 10–14), mixing in a few mossy and cracked bricks. Cap it with 4 stone brick slabs, bottom half, on layer 15. That is 24 blocks: 15 stone bricks, 3 mossy, 2 cracked and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Start the entry bay","Now the glowing entry bay at the front left. On layer 2, run stone bricks round its front and sides, leaving a 1 block gap in the middle of the front for the door. That is 8 blocks: 5 across the front, minus the gap, and 2 on each side.",[]],
["Build the bay walls","On the front, put oak planks at each end, then dark oak planks next to the door gap on each side, 2 tall, as shutters (layers 3–4). In the gap, stack 3 glass panes, a tall glowing door, on layers 2–4. Close the bay's sides with oak planks 2 tall, on layers 3–4. That is 12 oak planks, 4 dark oak planks and 3 panes.",[]],
["Roof the bay","Lay oak planks over the whole bay on layer 5, 5 wide and 3 deep: 15 planks. Along the front edge, put oak slabs, bottom half, on layer 6: 5 slabs. The slabs make a low front lip.",[]],
["Light the bay","Hang a lantern from the bay roof inside, 1 block behind each shutter, on layer 4: 2 lanterns. They glow through the glass door.",[]],
["Edge the wing in stone","On the wing floor, on layer 2, run a course of stone bricks all round its edge: 24 blocks. Mix in mossy and cracked bricks again.",[]],
["Set the wing posts","Put a dark oak log post at each of the wing's 4 corners. The two on the right end are 3 tall (layers 3–5), and the two on the left end, where the wing meets the main house, are 4 tall (layers 3–6). That is 14 logs.",[]],
["Fill the wing walls","Fill between the posts with oak planks: 3 tall along the front, back and right end, and 4 tall on the left end against the main house (layers 3–6). Leave the window gaps: on the front, a gap 3 wide on layer 4 (the 4th to 6th blocks from the left) with 1 more above its middle on layer 5, and on the right end, 1 gap in the middle on layer 4. That is 58 planks.",[]],
["Add the arched window","Fill the gaps with glass panes: the 3 on layer 4 plus the 1 above the middle, which makes a small arch, then the 1 on the right end. That is 5 panes.",[]],
["Close the wing gable","On the right end of the wing, above the wall, fill a small triangle with oak planks: 3 on layer 6 and 1 on layer 7. That is 4 planks.",[]],
["Trim the eaves in stone","Along the front and the back of the wing, 1 block out from the walls, lay a line of stone brick slabs, bottom half, on layer 5. Each line is 9 long: 18 slabs.",[]],
["Roof the wing","With dark oak stairs, high side facing the ridge, build the two slopes of the wing roof. On the front: a row on layer 6 and a row on layer 7. On the back: a row on layer 7 and a row on layer 6, sloping the other way. Run each row from the 2nd block of the wing to the right end, so it meets the main house's roof on the left. That is 32 stairs, 8 in each row.",[]],
["Add the wing ridge","Between the two slopes, along the top, lay a ridge of dark oak planks on layer 8: 8 planks.",[]],
["Add window boxes","Give the windows flower boxes. Under the brick pier window, put 2 oak trapdoors in front of the wall on layer 3 and a poppy on each. Under the gable window, 1 trapdoor on layer 8 with a poppy on top. Under the wing window, 3 trapdoors on layer 3 in front of the wall, with a poppy, a dandelion and a poppy on top. Finally hang a lantern from the front eave slab at the right-hand corner of the wing. That is 6 trapdoors, 5 poppies, 1 dandelion and 1 lantern.",[]],
["Lay the garden path","In front of the entry bay's door, lay a dirt path 2 wide and 5 long, on the door's block and the one to its left: 10 dirt path blocks.",[]],
["Stand the lamp post","To the left of the bay, in line with its front, build a lamp post: a cobblestone base, 4 oak fences and a lantern on top (layers 1–6).",[]],
["Set out barrels","Place 8 barrels: a stack of 2 and 2 singles beside the lamp post, 1 right beside the door post, and 3 in front of the wing towards its right end. Put a lantern on top of the barrel by the door and on the one at the front of the group of 3: 2 lanterns.",[]],
["Build the planter boxes","In front of the wing build two planters: 3 oak planks in a row with azalea leaves on top, set 3 blocks out from the wall, under the window; and another 3 in a row near the right end, one block farther out. That is 6 planks and 6 azalea leaves.",[]],
["Plant leafy bushes","Round the house, put oak leaves as small bushes, 1 or 2 high, mainly at the left of the entry, at the back and round the right end of the wing: 28 leaves.",[]],
["Scatter flowers","Scatter 19 flowers over the grass: 5 poppies, 9 dandelions and 5 oxeye daisies.",[]],
["Add the grass","Finish with 116 short grass tufts over the open ground, thinner by the house and none on the path.",[]]]},
{id:"cozy-cottage",name:"Cozy Cottage",emoji:"🏡",tags:["Houses","Survival","Cozy"],diff:"Medium",time:"2 hr",size:"14×13",photos:[P5],pr:[1.07],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A snug stone-footed cottage with barred cellar windows, a covered porch, white plaster walls between log pillars, a cross-gabled spruce roof, a brick chimney and lanterns by the door.",steps:[["Build the stone foundation","Mark out a rectangle 12 blocks wide and 9 deep on flat ground; the long side with the porch and door is the front. Trace its edge with a ring of stone, one block thick and 3 tall (layers 1–3). Mix the blocks as you go: mostly stone bricks, with cobblestone, mossy cobblestone and mossy stone bricks scattered through it, so the base looks old. In the front wall, leave two gaps on the middle layer (layer 2), each 2 wide: the 3rd and 4th blocks from the left, and the 6th and 7th. They are the cellar windows. That is 110 blocks: 49 stone bricks, 29 cobblestone, 21 mossy cobblestone and 11 mossy stone bricks.",[]],
["Bar the cellar windows","Fill each window gap with oak fences to make bars: 4 fences. Then, inside the cellar, one block back from each window pair, light it up: stand a stone brick on the ground (layer 1) behind the 4th block from the left and behind the 7th, and put a lantern on each (layer 2). That is 2 bricks and 2 lanterns, so the windows glow from outside.",[]],
["Lay the main floor","On top of the foundation, on layer 4, lay planks over the whole 12 × 9 rectangle. Use oak planks for the front-left corner, the porch, which is 7 wide and 2 deep, and spruce planks for the rest. That is 14 oak planks and 94 spruce planks.",[]],
["Build the base of the entrance steps","In front of the 8th to 11th blocks from the left of the front wall, build a stepped pile of stone bricks, 3 rows deep. The outer two columns (the 8th and the 11th from the left) are 1 tall in the front row (layer 1), 2 tall in the middle row, and 3 tall in the row against the house. The two inner columns (the 9th and 10th) are empty in the front row, 1 tall in the middle row, and 2 tall against the house. Then put a flower pot on top of the right-hand column in the middle row (layer 3) and an allium on top of the left-hand one. That is 18 bricks, 1 pot and 1 allium.",[]],
["Lay the entrance stairs","Between the two stone columns, put oak stairs 2 wide, with the high side facing the house: the front row on the ground (layer 1), the middle row on top of the 1-tall bricks (layer 2), and the last row on top of the 2-tall bricks, against the foundation (layer 3). They climb to the floor. That is 6 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the log pillars","Stand 14 pillars of oak log on the floor, each 3 tall (layers 5–7). Count rows from the back (row 1) to the front (row 9), and blocks from the left (block 1) to the right (block 12). Back wall (row 1): blocks 1, 4, 8 and 12. Right wall (block 12): rows 5 and 9. Front of the wing: block 8, rows 8 and 9. Back wall of the porch (row 7): blocks 1, 4 and 7. Left wall (block 1): rows 4 and 9. Front of the porch (row 9): block 7. That is 42 logs.",[]],
["Wall in with white concrete","The house is a main block 12 wide and 7 deep (rows 1–7), with a wing at the front right, blocks 8–12 and rows 8–9. The front left, blocks 1–7 and rows 8–9, is the open porch. Between the pillars, fill the outside walls with white concrete on layers 5 and 6: the back wall, the left wall, the right wall, the front of the wing, the left side of the wing (block 8, rows 8–9), and the back wall of the porch (row 7). Leave two doorways open on both layers: block 10 of row 9 (the front door) and block 2 of row 7 (the porch door). On layer 6 only, also leave 8 window gaps and 3 niches: windows at blocks 3, 6 and 10 of the back wall; rows 3 and 7 of the right wall; rows 3 and 5 of the left wall; and block 6 of the porch back wall; niches at blocks 9 and 11 of row 9 and block 5 of row 7. That is 35 concrete blocks.",[]],
["Lay the roof beams","On layer 7, on top of every gap between the pillars in the outside walls, lay spruce planks, including over the doorways, windows and niches. That is 25 planks, one for every wall gap.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Put a glass pane in each of the 8 window gaps on layer 6: three in the back wall, two in each side wall, and one in the back wall of the porch. That is 8 panes.",[]],
["Hang the doors and lanterns","Stack 2 oak planks in each doorway for a door (layers 5–6): the front door at block 10 of row 9, and the porch door at block 2 of row 7. That is 4 planks. Then stand a lantern in each of the 3 niches (layer 6), so one glows either side of the front door and one by the porch window. That is 3 lanterns.",[]],
["Rail the porch","On layer 5, put oak fences along the front of the porch, blocks 2 to 6 of row 9, and one at the left end, block 1 of row 8. That is 6 fences. The corner pillars stand either side.",[]],
["Furnish the porch","On layer 5 along the back wall of the porch (row 8): a bench of three spruce slabs, bottom half, at blocks 2, 3 and 4; a pink tulip at block 5; a flower pot at block 6; and a barrel at block 7. That is 3 slabs, 1 tulip, 1 pot and 1 barrel.",[]],
["Fill in the gables","Above every wall column, including the pillars, fill with white concrete from layer 8 up to the layer just under where the roof will be. The roof is one block bigger than the house all round and rises one layer for each row you move in from the front or back edge, so counting rows from the one behind the back wall (row 0) the roof stair sits at layer 8, 9, 10, 11 and 12 for rows 0 to 4, and at layer 13 on the ridge, row 5. That makes the fill: the back wall 1 tall, the left wall 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3 tall for rows 1–7, and the right wall 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 tall for rows 1–9. The porch back wall (row 7) is 3 tall across, and the front of the wing is 1, 2, 3, 2, 1 tall for blocks 8–12, with a glass pane in the middle of its second row (layer 9) and a lantern under it on the beam (layer 8). That is 83 concrete, 1 pane and 1 lantern.",[]],
["Roof the eaves","The roof is one block bigger than the house on every side: 14 blocks long (blocks 0–13) and 11 rows deep (rows 0–10). Put a stair on every column at the height for its row, with the high side facing the ridge. In this step do the outer rim: dark oak stairs on layer 8 (rows 0 and 10) and spruce stairs on layer 9 (rows 1 and 9), with a spruce plank under each layer-9 stair on layer 8 wherever there is no concrete. Over the wing, blocks 7–13 of rows 7–10, a second roof runs front to back instead, rising one layer for each block in from block 7 or 13 up to a ridge down block 10; for each column use whichever of the two roofs is higher, and face its stairs along that slope. That is 23 dark oak stairs, 27 spruce stairs and 13 planks.",[]],
["Roof the middle","Continue up the slope: spruce stairs on layers 10 and 11 (rows 2 and 8, then rows 3 and 7), each with a spruce plank under it on the layer below, and the same on the wing roof. That is 59 stairs and 43 planks.",[]],
["Roof the top rows","Next, rows 4 and 6: dark oak stairs on layer 12, each with a spruce plank under it on layer 11, all along the roof. That is 28 stairs and 24 planks.",[]],
["Cap the ridge","Along the ridge, row 5, lay dark oak slabs, bottom half, on layer 13, with a spruce plank under each on layer 12. Then cap the ridge of the front gable the same way: slabs on layer 11 down block 10 of rows 8 to 10, with a plank under each on layer 10. That is 17 slabs and 14 planks.",[]],
["Build the chimney","On the back slope of the roof, on block 9 of row 4, stack 3 bricks (layers 13–15) and put a barrel on top (layer 16) for the chimney pot. That is 3 bricks and 1 barrel.",[]],
["Hang the porch lanterns","Hang a lantern from the roof in the middle of the porch, block 4 of row 8 (layer 8), and two more at the front edge of the porch under the roof, blocks 2 and 6 of row 9 (layer 7). That is 3 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the front garden","In the strip one block in front of the cellar wall (row 10): alliums at blocks 2 and 4, pink tulips at blocks 3 and 6, and a bush of 2 flowering azalea leaves, stacked, at block 7 beside the steps. That is 2 alliums, 2 tulips and 2 azalea leaves.",[]],
["Stack the barrels","Beside the front right corner, outside the 12th block, put two barrels on the ground at rows 8 and 9, and a third on top of the one at row 9. That is 3 barrels.",[]]]},
{id:"treehouse-hideaway",name:"Oak Treehouse Hideaway",emoji:"🌳",tags:["Nature","Cozy","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"5 hr",size:"28×24",photos:[P6],pr:[0.952],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A big oak treehouse on a buttressed trunk: a plank cabin with a fenced balcony under a wide roof, a huge lumpy crown, a leafy shelf with a rope swing, a door under a slab awning, a stream with stepping stones, bamboo, flowers and little red mushrooms.",steps:[["Dig the stream","Find open ground about 28 blocks wide and 24 deep. The side with the door is the front; left and right are as you face the tree from the front. Mark where the trunk will stand, a 5 × 5 square, and note its centre block. The stream runs all the way across the front, 2 blocks wide and 27 long, with its near edge 7 to 9 blocks in front of the centre block, drifting a block or so forward and back as it goes. Dig it 1 block down so the water is level with the grass and fill it with water. Directly in front of the trunk, at the centre block and the one to its left, leave a 2 × 2 gap for the path to cross. That is 50 water blocks (layer 1).",[]],
["Lay the stepping stones","Smooth sandstone slabs, flat on the ground, make a path 2 wide from the door out over the stream. Count 8 rows going out from the trunk, the first one right in front of where the awning will end (it sticks out 2 blocks from the trunk). Use both blocks in each row except: in row 1 use only the left block, and leave row 7 empty so it looks like stepping stones. The left block of the path is on the line of the door, the second block from the left of the trunk's front face, and the right block is the one beside it. Rows 4 and 5 fill the gap in the stream; the water will flow in around the slabs. That is 13 slabs (layer 1).",[]],
["Grow the root buttresses","Round the trunk square build 26 columns of oak logs of different heights, so the foot of the tree flares out like roots. The grid shows each column's height in blocks. Rows run from the back to the front and columns from left to right, T marks the trunk square (built next) and a dot means no log. row 1: · · 2 3 2 3 2 · ·; row 2: · 3 T T T T T 2 ·; row 3: 2 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 4: 3 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 5: 2 5 T T T T T 2 1; row 6: · 3 T T T T T 2 ·; row 7: · · 1 1 1 1 1 · ·. The 5 tall columns on the left are the tallest steps; the single row along the front is the doorstep. That is 58 logs (layers 1–5).",[]],
["Raise the lower trunk","Inside the ring, build the trunk square, 5 × 5, 6 blocks tall out of oak logs (layers 1–6). On the front face, leave the second block from the left empty on layers 2 and 3: that is the door. It is 148 logs, 150 minus the 2 door spots. The logs show their bark on every side, so the trunk looks wide and solid.",[]],
["Plank up the right side","Against the right face of the trunk, on its middle 3 blocks, build a strip of oak planks 3 wide, 1 thick and 10 tall (layers 3–12). Make two windows in its middle column: replace the planks on layers 5–6 and layers 8–9 with glass panes. Under each window, on layers 5 and 8, put an oak slab, bottom half, in the block just outside the lower pane, as a windowsill. That is 26 planks, 4 panes and 2 slabs. The strip starts on top of the 2 tall ledge on that side.",[]],
["Hang the door and awning","In the 2 door spots on the front face, stack 2 spruce planks (layers 2–4). Put an oak fence post on each side of the door, in front of the trunk on the doorstep row, 2 tall each: that is 4 fences. Roof the entrance with oak slabs, bottom half, on layer 4: 5 across, from one block left of the left post to one block right of the right post, and 2 deep, so it sticks out 2 blocks in front of the trunk. That is 2 planks and 10 slabs.",[]],
["Narrow the trunk","Above the ledges, carry the trunk up another 6 layers (layers 7–12), still 5 × 5 but with the 4 corner logs left out so it narrows. That is 126 logs, 21 on each layer. The trunk now reaches layer 12.",[]],
["Build the platform","On layer 13, at the top of the trunk, lay a floor of oak planks 11 wide and 7 deep. It sits on the plank strip at the right and stretches out 5 blocks past the trunk on the left, where it becomes the balcony. Leave the middle 3 × 3 free: the trunk goes on through it. That is 68 planks.",[]],
["Carry the trunk through","In the 3 × 3 gap in the floor, stack oak logs 9 tall (layers 13–21), so the trunk climbs through the cabin and keeps going above the roof. That is 81 logs.",[]],
["Raise the cabin walls","On the platform, build the walls of the cabin as a ring of oak planks 7 wide and 7 deep, 4 tall (layers 14–17). Three of the walls sit flush with the edge of the floor, and the left wall faces the balcony. Leave out a doorway in the middle of the left wall, 2 tall, and a window opening 3 wide and 2 tall in the middle of the front wall and the middle of the right wall, on layers 15–16. That is 82 planks.",[]],
["Glaze the windows","Fill the two window openings with glass panes: 3 across and 2 up, 12 panes (layers 15–16). The panes join up into one big window on each wall.",[]],
["Fence the balcony","On layer 14, set oak fence along the left edge of the floor, 7 blocks, and along the front and back edges of the balcony, 3 blocks each: that is 13 fences. Then raise 3 posts from the left edge, at its two ends and in the middle, with 3 more fences on top of each (layers 15–17), to hold up the roof. That is 22 fences.",[]],
["Lay the roof","On layer 18, lay a flat roof of oak planks 12 wide and 9 deep, with the trunk coming up through the middle: it covers the cabin, sticks out 1 block at the front, back and right, and reaches over the balcony to the tops of the posts. That is 99 planks. Then hang 2 lanterns from the underside of the roof inside the cabin (layer 17), in the front left corner and the back right corner, 1 block in from the walls.",[]],
["Top the roof","On layer 19, over the cabin, lay a 7 × 7 square of oak slabs, bottom half, round the trunk. That is 40 slabs. It gives the roof its stepped edge.",[]],
["Grow the branches","Above the roof the trunk keeps climbing: stack 7 more logs in the middle, layers 22–28. On the right of the trunk, standing on the roof cap, build a stub of 4 logs: 2 stacked beside the trunk (layers 20–21), then 1 to their right on layer 21 and 1 on top of that on layer 22. About 5 blocks above the roof, put four arms of logs sticking out from the trunk on layer 24, 8 blocks to the left and right and 7 forward and back, so the leaves have something to hang from; they will mostly be hidden. Finally, build the branch for the lower leaf shelf: from the plank strip on the right, 2 logs side by side going out on layer 10, starting against the plank strip, then 1 on top of the second one on layer 11 and 4 more going out along layer 11 (that is 7 logs). That is 48 logs in all (layers 10–28).",[]],
["Start the crown: the underside","The crown is built as a lumpy ball of leaves, a shell 2 blocks thick, and this is the one step where you shape things by eye. It is made of one big lump, about 17 wide and 14 deep, centred over the trunk, with smaller lumps on the left, right, top and front, and one hanging low over the roof. Start with the underside, layers 21–24: the hanging lump over the roof and the bottom of the big lump and the left lump. Make the edge ragged by leaving out a leaf here and there. That is 255 leaves.",[]],
["The widest part","On layers 25–29, the crown is at its widest, 24 blocks across and 15 deep. Carry the shell of the big lump up round the arms, bulge the left lump out to the left, and add the right lump about 5 blocks past the trunk. Keep the shell 2 thick and leave a few small holes so the light gets in. That is 578 leaves.",[]],
["Close the top","On layers 30–35, bring the crown in towards the top: the big lump closes over, and the top lump sits on it a little to the left, about 10 blocks wide. Round the top off and leave the edge uneven. That is 441 leaves.",[]],
["Make the lower leaf shelf","On the right of the cabin, round the branch, build a shelf of leaves about 9 wide and 10 deep, 3 layers thick (layers 11–13). Layers 12 and 13 are almost solid; the bottom layer, layer 11, is ragged, with gaps, but make sure the 4 blocks above the swing beam, in the row one in front of the branch, are leaves. That is 145 leaves.",[]],
["Hang the swing ropes","Under the shelf, one block in front of the branch, place a beam of 4 oak fences in a row on layer 10. From the first and last fence hang a rope of 7 fences, down to layer 3. That is 18 fences (layers 3–10).",[]],
["Add the swing seat","Under the ropes, on layer 2, lay 4 oak slabs in a row, bottom half, for the seat. That is 4 slabs.",[]],
["Plant the bamboo","Plant bamboo in 18 loose clumps, 9 on each side of the tree and the stream, with a few behind and a few at the front, round the corners of the plot and along the stream. Each clump is a patch about 3 × 3 with a few gaps, and the stalks are 3 to 6 tall: plant them and trim the tops with an axe to keep them short. That is 229 bamboo in 52 stalks (layers 1–6).",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","Scatter 43 flowers over the grass, thicker near the bamboo: 17 poppies, 12 dandelions and 14 oxeye daisies (layer 1).",[]],
["Add the red mushrooms","Put 12 red mushrooms on the tops of the lower log ledges round the foot of the tree, 4 on the left steps, 4 along the back and 1 on the right, and 3 more on the grass: 1 front left, 1 front right and 1 on the right (layers 1–4). They are tiny, so tuck them in where you can see them from the front.",[]],
["Finish with tall grass","Finally, put short grass in loose clumps on the bare ground, thickest by the stream and the bamboo and none on the path: 121 blocks (layer 1).",[]]]},
{id:"medieval-watchtower",name:"Medieval Watchtower",emoji:"🏰",tags:["Medieval","Defense","Survival"],diff:"Hard",time:"3 hr",size:"22×20",photos:[P9],pr:[1.036],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A tall stone-brick watchtower with window slits, a corbelled crown of merlons, a stairway up to its ledge, sandstone steps, a ruined wall, grassy terraces and a couple of oaks.",steps:[["Raise the first hillside terrace","Mark an 11 × 11 square on flat ground for the tower's base, about 22 blocks across and 20 deep with everything around it. The side with the stairway and door is the front, and left and right are as you face the tower from the front. Behind and beside that square, wrap a band one block wide, touching it, round the back and 4 rows down each side: 13 blocks across the back and 4 along each side, 21 columns in all. Make each column 4 blocks of coarse dirt with a grass block on top (layers 1–5), and take one block off a few of them so the edge is uneven. That is 77 coarse dirt and 21 grass blocks.",[]],
["Add the second terrace","One block further out, wrap a second band round the back and sides: 15 across the back and 5 along each side, 25 columns. Make each 2 blocks of coarse dirt under a grass block (layers 1–3), again taking a block off a few. That is 42 coarse dirt and 25 grass blocks.",[]],
["Add the lowest terrace","One more band, one block further out: 17 across the back and 6 along each side, 29 grass blocks laid on the ground (layer 1).",[]],
["Build the stone plinth","On the marked 11 × 11 square, raise a ring of stone bricks one block thick and 6 tall (layers 1–6). It stands against the first terrace. Mix the bricks as you go, scattering mossy and cracked ones so it looks old. That is 240 blocks: 197 stone bricks, 22 mossy and 21 cracked.",[]],
["Lay the ledge and floor","Cover the whole plinth with a flat plate, 11 × 11, on layer 7. Use cobblestone for the middle 9 × 9 and stone bricks round the edge, with a few mossy ones; the edge is a one-block ledge round the tower. That is 81 cobblestone and 40 stone bricks.",[]],
["Stand the front piers","In front of the plinth, one block out from its front wall, stand 4 piers: at the 1st, 3rd, 9th and 11th blocks across the front. Make each 5 stone bricks tall, then put a stone brick stair on top (layer 6) with the high side against the wall. That is 24 blocks, layers 1–6, with 4 stairs.",[]],
["Build the front stairway","Centred between the middle two piers, 3 blocks wide, build 6 steps up to the ledge. The step furthest out, 6 blocks in front of the wall, is a row of stone brick stairs on layer 1; each step nearer the wall is one layer higher, so the last row, right in front of the wall, is on layer 6. The high side of every stair faces the tower. Fill solid stone brick under each row of stairs. That is 18 stairs and 45 bricks.",[]],
["Build the sandstone steps","Against the right wall of the plinth, build 5 columns of smooth sandstone going outward, counting rows from the back of the plinth. Column 1, touching the wall: rows 6–10, 5 tall. Column 2: rows 6–9, 4 tall. Column 3: rows 7–9, 3 tall. Column 4: rows 7–9, 2 tall. Column 5: rows 8–9, 1 tall. Top each column with smooth sandstone stairs, high side towards the plinth, on its two end rows and a few in the middle, and full blocks elsewhere. Then put a slab on top of the middle of columns 1 and 2, on row 8. That is 45 blocks, 13 stairs and 2 slabs.",[]],
["Raise the ruined wall","Out from the left wall of the plinth, on rows 9 and 10 from the back, build a wall two blocks thick in 6 columns, getting lower as it goes: 6, 5, 5, 4, 3 and 2 blocks tall (layers 1–7). Use stone bricks, mixed with mossy and cracked ones. On columns 1 to 3, add one extra brick on top of every other block, in a checkerboard, like broken battlements. Finish the three outer columns with a stone brick stair on top, facing the plinth, on alternate rows. That is 56 blocks: 3 stairs and 53 bricks.",[]],
["Shape the front terraces","At the front, make two small grassy slopes. On the right, in front of the sandstone steps, starting level with the plinth's front wall: a 5 × 5 patch whose corner next to the plinth is 5 blocks tall (layer 5) and drops one block for every block you move away from that corner, to the right or to the front. On the left, beyond the left pier, a 4 × 4 patch that rises to 4 blocks and drops the same way. Use coarse dirt under grass blocks, and take a block off a few of the higher columns. That is 40 coarse dirt and 41 grass blocks.",[]],
["Start the tower shaft","On the plate, stand a ring of stone bricks 9 wide and one thick, with a ledge one block wide all round it, 3 tall (layers 8–10). In the middle of the front wall leave a 1-block doorway, 2 tall (layers 8–9). That is 94 blocks: 88 stone bricks, 6 mossy and 0 cracked.",[]],
["Raise the first window course","Add 2 more layers of the ring (layers 11–12), but on each of the four walls leave a 1-block slit, 2 tall, at the 3rd and the 7th block of the wall. That is 8 slits. Count the wall as 9 blocks, corners included. The ring takes 48 blocks: 35 stone bricks, 10 mossy and 3 cracked.",[]],
["Glaze the first slits","Put a glass pane in each of the 8 slits, 2 tall (layers 11–12). That is 16 panes.",[]],
["Carry the shaft up","Raise the plain ring 3 more layers (layers 13–15), with no gaps. That is 96 blocks.",[]],
["Raise the second window course","Add 2 more layers (layers 16–17) with the same 8 slits, directly above the first ones. The ring takes 48 blocks.",[]],
["Glaze the second slits","Glass panes again in the 8 new slits, 2 tall (layers 16–17). That is 16 panes.",[]],
["Finish the shaft","Raise the plain ring 2 more layers (layers 18–19). That is 64 blocks.",[]],
["Hang the door","Stack 2 spruce planks in the doorway for a door (layers 8–9). That is 2 planks. You reach it from the ledge at the top of the stairway.",[]],
["Build the corbels","On layer 20, run one more course of the 9-wide ring of stone bricks (32 blocks). Round it, one block out on every side, place a ring of 40 stone brick stairs, 11 × 11, with the high side against the tower, so they stick out like brackets. At the four corners the stairs will join their neighbours into an outer corner.",[]],
["Lay the lookout deck","On layer 21, put a ring of stone bricks round the 11 × 11 edge, on top of the stairs (40 blocks), and fill the 9 × 9 inside with spruce planks (81 planks).",[]],
["Raise the merlons","On the edge of the deck, build the battlements, 2 blocks tall (layers 22–23). Going along each side of 11 blocks from a corner: two blocks up, one gap, two up, one gap, two up, one gap, then two up. The gaps fall on the 3rd, 6th and 9th blocks of every side, and each corner is a merlon. That is 56 blocks: 49 stone bricks, 4 mossy and 3 cracked.",[]],
["Light the battlements","Put a torch on top of each of the 4 corner merlons (layer 24). That is 4 torches.",[]],
["Light the ledge","Stand a lantern on the ledge on each of its 4 corners, and one either side of the doorstep, 1 block each side of the doorway, in front of the wall (layer 8). That is 6 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant two oak trees","On the lowest terrace, in line with the back wall of the plinth: 8 blocks to the right of the middle, stack 6 oak logs on the grass block (layers 2–7), and 8 blocks to the left, stack 5 (layers 2–6). That is 11 logs.",[]],
["Crown the trees","Around the top two logs of each trunk, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves without its corners, with the trunk in the middle. Then put a 3 × 3 square on top, and a plus of 5 leaves on top of that. Where a leaf would land on a terrace block, leave it out. That is 105 leaves, layers 5–9.",[]],
["Finish the terraces","Last, on top of the grass blocks of the terraces, plant 38 short grass, 2 poppies and 4 dandelions in loose clumps, and leave the rest bare (layers 2–6). That is 44 blocks.",[]]]},
{id:"cherry-pagoda",name:"Cherry Blossom Pagoda",emoji:"🌸",tags:["Fantasy","Nature","Showpiece"],diff:"Expert",time:"7 hr",size:"29×29",photos:[P7],pr:[0.908],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A four-tier pagoda on a pond island: tiled dark roofs with upturned pink corners, cherry-wood walls, cream balconies, a ringed spire, a stone causeway and four stepped cherry trees.",steps:[["Lay the moss islands","On flat ground, mark out a square 29 blocks across and find the middle block. In each of the four corners, lay a patch of moss blocks on layer 1, about 5 blocks across and centred 11 blocks out from the middle both ways; these are the tree islands. Let each patch spread a little towards the middle of the corner so the edge looks ragged. Then add a landing in the middle of the front edge, 5 wide and 2 deep, 11 and 12 blocks out from the middle. That is 142 moss blocks.",[]],
["Fill the pond","Between the islands, fill the ground with water on layer 1: a rounded square 21 blocks across, 10 blocks out from the middle each way, with the corners cut off where it meets the islands. Keep clear a 13 × 13 square in the middle for the plinth, a strip 3 wide in front of it for the causeway, and four single blocks for lamp posts, 2 blocks either side of the middle of that strip, 8 and 10 blocks out from the middle. That is 216 water blocks.",[]],
["Lay the plinth","On layer 1 in the middle, lay stone bricks 11 wide and 11 deep, and then wrap them in a ring of stone brick slabs, bottom half, to make a plinth 13 across. In the middle of the front edge, three of the ring blocks are full stone bricks instead of slabs: that is where the steps will go. That is 124 bricks and 45 slabs.",[]],
["Lay the causeway","From the front edge of the plinth, lay a path of stone bricks 3 wide and 4 long straight out across the water to the landing, on layer 1. That is 12 bricks.",[]],
["Raise the lamp posts","Beside the causeway, in the four gaps you left in the water, build a lamp post on each: 2 stone bricks (layers 1–2) with a lantern on top (layer 3). Two stand either side of the path 8 blocks out from the middle, and two 10 blocks out. That is 8 bricks and 4 lanterns.",[]],
["Lay the ground floor","On the plinth, lay cherry planks 11 wide and 11 deep on layer 2, right on top of the stone bricks. This is the floor of the ground-floor hall, and it leaves a ledge of slabs all round. That is 121 planks.",[]],
["Add the door steps","On layer 2, on top of the three full bricks at the middle of the front edge, put stone brick stairs with the high side facing the house. They lift you up from the causeway to the floor. That is 3 stairs.",[]],
["Stand the ground-floor pillars","The walls are a square 9 blocks across, one block in from the edge of the floor, held up by pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 3–7). Along each side the pattern is: a corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner pillar. The gap of three in the middle of the front is the door. That is 12 pillars, 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall in the hall","Fill the gaps between the pillars with cherry planks. On the bottom row (layer 3) and on layer 5, fill every gap except the three-wide door in the front. Leave layer 4 empty for the windows. Then fill every gap on layers 6–7, including above the door, which makes it a doorway 3 wide and 3 tall. That is 74 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the windows","On layer 4, fill every empty gap in the walls with dark oak planks to make dark shuttered windows, except the open door. If you would rather see inside, use glass panes here instead. That is 17 planks.",[]],
["Furnish the shrine","Inside, lay a strip of red carpet 1 wide and 5 long on layer 3, starting just inside the door and running towards the back. Leave one block, then build an altar of 3 stone bricks across the back, 3 blocks behind the middle (layer 3). Put a lantern on each end of the altar and a flower pot in the middle on layer 4. Finally stand a lantern on the floor in each of the four inner corners, 3 blocks from the middle both ways. That is 5 carpets, 3 bricks, 6 lanterns and 1 flower pot.",[]],
["Cap the ground floor","On layer 8, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the balcony floor for the next level, and the roof is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the ground floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 8, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 8, and on top of them (layer 9) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle. That is 68 slabs, 48 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 8: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 9. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 7, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Fence the second-floor balcony","Put a ring of birch fences on layer 9, on the outer plank ring of the balcony, 11 blocks across, just inside the tile ring. It will run round the foot of the walls above. That is 40 fences.",[]],
["Stand the second-floor pillars","On the inner plank ring of the balcony, the one 9 blocks across, stand the pillars again, in the same pattern as the floor below: 12 pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 9–13). A corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner. That is 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall the second floor","Fill every gap between the pillars with cherry planks on the bottom row (layer 9) and on the top three rows, but leave the layer in between empty for the windows. There is no door up here, so all 20 gaps are filled. That is 80 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the second-floor windows","On layer 10, fill the 20 empty gaps with dark oak planks, as on the floor below. That is 20 planks.",[]],
["Cap the second floor","On layer 14, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the balcony floor for the next level, and the roof is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
