["Fence the top-floor balcony","Put a ring of birch fences on layer 21, on the outer plank ring of the balcony, 11 blocks across, just inside the tile ring. It will run round the foot of the walls above. That is 40 fences.",[]],
["Stand the top-floor pillars","On the inner plank ring of the balcony, the one 9 blocks across, stand the pillars again, in the same pattern as the floor below: 12 pillars of cherry log, each 5 tall (layers 21–25). A corner pillar, one empty block, a pillar, three empty blocks, a pillar, one empty block, and the other corner. That is 60 logs.",[]],
["Wall the top floor","Fill every gap between the pillars with cherry planks on the bottom row (layer 21) and on the top three rows, but leave the layer in between empty for the windows. There is no door up here, so all 20 gaps are filled. That is 80 planks.",[]],
["Shutter the top-floor windows","On layer 22, fill the 20 empty gaps with dark oak planks, as on the floor below. That is 20 planks.",[]],
["Cap the top floor","On layer 26, lay a ring of cherry planks on top of the walls, 9 blocks across, and a second ring around the outside of it, 11 blocks across. The outer ring is the foot of the pyramid roof, which is built out from it. That is 72 planks (32 + 40).",[]],
["Roof the top floor","Build a tiled roof in two rings. First the eave: dark oak slabs, bottom half, in a ring 15 blocks across on layer 26, leaving the three blocks at each corner empty for the next step. Then, inside it, a ring 13 across: dark oak planks on layer 26, and on top of them (layer 27) a row of tiles, alternating dark oak stairs and slabs, starting with a stair on each corner, with the stairs' high side facing the middle; the four corner blocks of the tile ring are left empty for the ridge. That is 68 slabs, 44 planks and 20 stairs.",[]],
["Lift the top-floor corners","In the three empty blocks at each corner of the eave ring, lay cherry planks on layer 26: one on the corner and one each side of it. Then put a cherry slab, bottom half, on top of each corner block on layer 27. These pink tips curl up out of the dark roof. That is 12 planks and 4 slabs.",[]],
["Trim the top-floor eaves","Directly under the eave slabs, on layer 25, hang a cream trim round the 15-block ring: birch planks and birch slabs alternately, starting with a slab next to each corner. Leave the four corner blocks empty for lanterns. That is 24 planks and 28 slabs.",[]],
["Raise the pyramid","Carry the top roof up as a steep pyramid of stair rings. The 11-across and 9-across rings sit on planks on layer 27, with tiles on layer 28 (stairs and slabs alternating). The 7-across and 5-across rings sit on planks on layer 28, with plain stairs on layer 29. The 3-across ring has planks on layer 29 and stairs on layer 30, and the middle block gets planks on layers 29 and 30. Every stair has its high side to the middle, and the four corner blocks of every ring stay empty for the ridges. That is 102 planks, 36 slabs and 64 stairs.",[]],
["Run the pink ridges","Down each corner of the roof, climb a ridge of cherry wood, one step for each ring: a cherry plank with a cherry stair on top, on the empty corner block of each ring from the 13-across ring (stair on layer 27) up to the 3-across ring (stair on layer 30). Face every stair towards the middle. Six steps a corner, four corners. That is 24 planks and 24 stairs.",[]],
["Crown it with the spire","On the middle block at layer 31, stack 10 dark oak logs (layers 31–40) for the pole. Thread dark oak slabs, bottom half, round it like discs: a full ring of 8 around the pole on layers 32 and 34, and four in a plus sign on layers 36, 38 and 40. Finish with a dark oak plank on layer 41 and a lightning rod on layer 42. That is 28 slabs, 1 plank and 1 rod.",[]],
["Light the lanterns","Hang a lantern under the cherry plank on each corner of all four eaves (one block below the eave, on layers 7, 13, 19 and 25): 16 in all. Then stand a lantern on the corner post of each of the three balcony rails (layers 10, 16 and 22): 12 more. That is 28 lanterns.",[]],
["Plant the four cherry trees","On each island, in the middle of the patch, stand a trunk of 4 cherry logs (layers 2–5). The two in front get big stepped crowns of cherry leaves: 7 × 7 with the corners cut off on layer 6, 5 × 5 on layer 7, 3 × 3 on layer 8 and one on top at layer 9. The two at the back are smaller: 5 × 5 on layer 6, 3 × 3 on layer 7 and one on top at layer 8. Leave a few gaps in each, but make sure every leaf touches another. That is 16 logs and 207 leaves.",[]],
["Scatter the petals","Finish by scattering pink petals on the moss islands, on layer 2, on roughly one block in three. That is 55 petals.",[]]]},
{id:"starter-farm",name:"Starter Farm",emoji:"🌾",tags:["Farm","Survival","Starter"],diff:"Easy",time:"45 min",size:"17×18",photos:[P8],pr:[1.422],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A round-cornered wheat field ringed with mossy cobblestone and lanterns, with a pumpkin-head scarecrow, a fence gate, leafy hedges and wildflowers around it.",steps:[["Mark the field and place the water","Find flat ground about 17 blocks wide and 18 deep; the finished farm, with its hedge, trees and flowers, takes about that much. In the middle, mark out a 9 × 9 square for the wheat field. The side with the gate is the front, and left and right are as you face the field from the gate. Dig out the one block in the exact middle, the 5th block of the 5th row, and fill it with water (layer 1). One water block keeps farmland up to 4 blocks away moist, so it reaches the whole field.",[]],
["Till the farmland","Hoe the ground inside the 9 × 9 square into farmland (layer 1), leaving out three kinds of block: the water in the middle, the four corner blocks of the square, which stay as plain ground so the field has cut corners, and the 5th block of the 2nd row from the back, which stays bare for the scarecrow. That is 75 farmland blocks. Counted from the back, rows 1 and 9 are 7 blocks wide and rows 2 to 8 are 9 wide, except that row 2 has the gap.",[]],
["Plant the wheat","Plant wheat on every farmland block (layer 2) except the 4th and 6th blocks of row 2 from the back, the two blocks either side of the scarecrow's gap. They stay bare for its arms. That is 73 wheat. Give the field a little time to grow if you want it fully golden.",[]],
["Lay the ring base","Wrap a ring one block thick around the whole field, touching it on every side, in mixed cobblestone and mossy cobblestone (layer 1). Each side has 7 blocks between the corners, and each corner is a little L of 3 blocks: the block on the diagonal and one beside it each way. Leave a 3-block gap in the front side, the 2nd to 4th of its 7 blocks counting from the left; that is the gate. That is 37 blocks: 23 cobblestone and 14 mossy cobblestone. Scatter the mossy ones so the ring looks weathered.",[]],
["Raise the corners and some of the sides","On layer 2, put a full block on top of every corner block (all 12) and on some of the side blocks. On each side, count the 7 blocks between the corners (on the front, from the left). On the back side, the 2nd, 4th and 6th; on the right side, the 2nd, 4th and 6th; on the left side, the 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th; on the front side, only the 7th. Mix mossy cobblestone and cobblestone. That is 23 blocks: 14 mossy and 9 plain.",[]],
["Fill the gaps with slabs","On each side block that did not get a full block, place a slab on layer 2, bottom half, so the ring dips and rises: the 1st, 3rd, 5th and 7th on the back and right sides, the 2nd, 4th and 6th on the left side, and the 6th on the front side. That is 12 slabs: 5 mossy and 7 cobblestone.",[]],
["Step up the corners","On layer 3, put a mossy cobblestone block on top of each corner's diagonal block, then a mossy cobblestone stair on each of the two blocks beside it, with the high side against the new block, so every corner climbs to a little peak. That is 4 blocks and 8 stairs.",[]],
["Raise the gate posts","The two front blocks either side of the gap, the 1st and the 5th of the front side, are the gate posts. Stack two more blocks on each (layers 2–3), mixing cobblestone and mossy cobblestone. That is 4 blocks: 2 cobblestone and 2 mossy.",[]],
["Lay the path","Cover the ground through the gap and in front of it with coarse dirt: the 3 blocks of the gap itself, then 5 blocks across the next row, from just outside one gate post to just outside the other, then 3 blocks across the row after that, lined up with the gap (layer 1). That is 11 blocks.",[]],
["Close the gate","Put a spruce fence in each of the 3 blocks of the gap, on top of the coarse dirt (layer 2). They join onto the gate posts either side. That is 3 fences. If you want a working gate, swap the middle fence for a fence gate.",[]],
["Scatter the boulders","Just outside the ring, touching it or a block away, set 12 single blocks of mossy cobblestone on the ground as boulders: 3 along each of the right, left, back and front. Put a mossy cobblestone slab on top of 3 of them, one each on the right, left and back, so they are two layers tall. That is 15 blocks, layers 1–2. Keep the path clear.",[]],
["Light the ring","Stand a lantern on top of each of the 4 corner peaks and each of the 2 gate posts (that is 6 on layer 4), and on the middle block of the back side and the middle block of the right side (2 on layer 3). That is 8 lanterns.",[]],
["Start the scarecrow","In the bare block in the 2nd row from the back, set a coarse dirt block, then stack a spruce fence on it and a cobblestone block on that (layers 1–3). The fence is the post and the cobblestone is the body. That is 3 blocks.",[]],
["Give it arms","Beside the post and the body, on each side, stack 2 spruce fences (layers 2–3). The lower fence stands on the farmland you left bare and joins the post; the upper one joins the body. That is 4 fences.",[]],
["Add the pumpkin head","On top of the body, on layer 4, set a jack o'lantern with its face turned to the front, towards the gate. That is 1 block.",[]],
["Top it with a hat","On layer 5, put a spruce plank on top of the head, a spruce stair on each side of it with the high side against the plank, and a spruce slab beyond each stair, bottom half, to make a wide brim. Then put one more spruce slab on top of the plank, on layer 6, for the crown. That is 1 plank, 2 stairs and 3 slabs.",[]],
["Grow the back hedge","Leave a one-block gap behind the back of the ring, where the boulders sit, and behind it build a hedge two blocks thick and 15 wide, centred on the field, out of oak leaves. In the middle, 11 blocks wide, make it 2 or 3 blocks tall, about half of each; for the last 2 blocks at each end, make it 1 or 2 tall. Leave the 2nd block in from each end of the back row empty for a tree. Layer 1 takes 28 leaves, layer 2 takes 25 and layer 3 takes 12, 65 in all.",[]],
["Plant two tree trunks","In each of the two gaps, stack oak logs 5 high (layers 1–5). That is 10 logs. They stand in the back row of the hedge, so the hedge wraps round them.",[]],
["Crown the trees","Around the top of each trunk, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves on layers 4 and 5, leaving off the four corners and keeping the trunk in the middle, then a 3 × 3 square on layer 6. That is 98 leaves for both trees, 40 on layer 4, 40 on layer 5 and 18 on layer 6.",[]],
["Add two leafy bushes","On the ground a little way out from each side of the field, set a bush of oak leaves 2 wide and 3 deep: the left one 7 and 8 blocks out from the middle, beside the left boulders, and the right one the same on the right, both lined up with the 2nd to 4th rows of the field from the back. Cover each with 6 leaves on layer 1 and then about 4 or 5 more on layer 2, leaving a few gaps. That is 12 leaves on layer 1 and 9 on layer 2, 21 in all.",[]],
["Scatter the flowers","On bare grass around the build, outside the ring and the boulders, plant 25 flowers in loose groups: 8 poppies, 6 oxeye daisies, 5 dandelions, 4 pink tulips and 2 alliums. Keep the path and the front of the gate clear.",[]],
["Finish with short grass","Finally, fill in a few gaps between the flowers with 9 blocks of short grass, mostly near the ring and the hedge, so the ground looks wild rather than mown.",[]]]},
{id:"mushroom-house",name:"Mushroom House",emoji:"🍄",tags:["Fantasy","Cozy","Nature"],diff:"Medium",time:"1 hr",size:"20×18",photos:[P4],pr:[1.231],photoCredit:"Preview render of this build’s 3D model.",desc:"A fairy-tale mushroom cottage: a five-tier spotted red cap over an uneven white stem, a dark oak awning over the door, a winding path, poppy beds and a little toadstool on a grassy terrace.",steps:[["Build the grassy steps","Find open ground about 20 blocks wide and 18 deep. The side with the door is the front, and left and right are as you face it from the front. Mark where the stem will stand, a 7 × 7 square, and to the right of it, about 4 blocks away and level with its back 5 rows, build three grassy steps, each column 5 blocks long front to back. The column nearest the stem is 3 tall: 2 coarse dirt with a grass block on top. The middle column is 2 tall: 1 coarse dirt and a grass block. The outer column is a single grass block (layers 1–3). That is 15 coarse dirt and 15 grass blocks.",[]],
["Lay the winding path","Dirt path blocks sit level with the grass, so put them in place of the grass blocks on layer 1. Start at the doorstep, the block in front of the left block of the stem's front face, and lay 2 blocks straight out from the door, then a row of 3 in front of those, centred on the same line. Then wander off to the front left in a zigzag: a row of 2 blocks, each row one block further forward and one block further left than the last, for 4 more rows. That is 13 dirt path blocks.",[]],
["Start the stem","Mark the stem: a 7 × 7 square with the corners cut off, three blocks from each corner (the corner block and the two beside it). That leaves 37 blocks. Fill layers 1–2 with white concrete, leaving the door spot empty on layer 2: that is the left block of the 3-block front face. That is 73 blocks.",[]],
["Carry the stem up","Do the same on layers 3–4, again leaving the door spot empty on layer 3. That is 73 blocks.",[]],
["Make the stem uneven","Now raise each column to its own height, with layers 5 and up. The numbers are how many blocks tall each column is in total, counting the layers you have already placed, with a dot where there is no block. Columns marked 4 are already finished. Look at it from the front, rows from the back to the front, left to right. row 1: · · 6 5 4 · ·; row 2: · 6 8 6 6 8 ·; row 3: 5 8 8 8 8 6 5; row 4: 4 8 8 8 8 7 6; row 5: 5 6 8 8 8 8 5; row 6: · 8 6 8 8 6 ·; row 7: · · 4 4 6 · ·. The 17 columns marked 8 reach layer 8 and carry the cap. That is 96 more blocks, layers 5–8.",[]],
["Hang the door","In the door spot, stack 2 dark oak planks (layers 2–3). That is 2 planks. It is the left block of the front face, which is 4 blocks tall.",[]],
["Set the awning posts","In the second row in front of the stem's front face, put a post on each side of the line of the door: one to the left of it and one to the right. Make each 3 tall out of dark oak fence (layers 1–3). That is 6 fences.",[]],
["Roof the awning","On layer 4, in the first row in front of the stem, lay 3 dark oak planks across, from the post on the left to the post on the right, then a dark oak slab, bottom half, at each end. In the second row, lay a row of 5 slabs over the posts, one block wider than the planks on each side. Finish with one more plank on top of the middle plank, on layer 5. That is 4 planks and 7 slabs.",[]],
["Hang a lantern","Hang a lantern from the middle slab between the posts, on layer 3. That is 1 lantern.",[]],
["Lay the first cap tier","The cap starts on layer 9, on top of the stem. Lay a red mushroom block square 11 × 11, centred on the stem so it sticks out 2 blocks on every side, with three blocks cut off each corner, as you did the stem. That is 109 blocks.",[]],
["Second tier","On layer 10, lay a 9 × 9 square, centred, with three blocks cut off each corner. That is 69 blocks.",[]],
["Third tier","On layer 11, lay a 7 × 7 square with just the one corner block cut off at each corner. That is 45 blocks.",[]],
["Fourth tier","On layer 12, lay a 5 × 5 square with the four corner blocks cut off. That is 21 blocks.",[]],
["Top of the cap","On layer 13, finish with a block 5 wide and 3 deep, centred. That is 15 blocks, so the cap is 5 layers deep.",[]],
["Grow a bush","Behind the stem and to the right, 3 to 5 blocks out from its right edge and 1 to 3 rows behind its back, build a bush of oak leaves 3 × 3: 9 leaves on layer 1 and 6 on layer 2, leaving a few gaps. That is 15 leaves.",[]],
["Plant an oak tree","To the left of the stem, 5 blocks out from its left edge and level with the 3rd row from the back, stack 7 oak logs (layers 1–7).",[]],
["Crown the tree","Around the top 2 logs, put a 5 × 5 square of oak leaves without the corners (layers 6–7), then a 3 × 3 square on layer 8, then a plus of 5 leaves on layer 9. The trunk fills the middle of the first three. That is 54 leaves.",[]],
["Add a little toadstool","On the highest grass block of the steps, in the middle of its 5 rows, put a mushroom stem block and then a red mushroom block on top (layers 4–5). That is 1 stem and 1 cap.",[]],
["Plant the poppies","Plant 21 poppies in two beds: 12 in a patch to the right of the door, in front of the right side of the stem, 3 to 6 blocks out from the line of the door; 8 in a patch to the left of the path; and 1 in front of the stem, near the path. That is 21 poppies (layer 1).",[]],
["Scatter some daisies","Scatter 5 oxeye daisies among the poppies and along the path (layer 1).",[]],
["Finish with tall grass","Finally, put short grass in loose clumps on the bare ground all round, and on the grassy steps: 66 blocks, thickest near the beds and the edges, and none right against the stem (layers 1–4).",[]]]}
